\begin{comment}
\noindent\textbf{Resource Efficiency}: Optimal use of computational and system resources (for instance: CPU, memory, and GPU) to ensure that applications and services run with minimal waste. This includes reducing idle resources, minimizing unnecessary computation, and rightsizing resources to match the actual workload. \textit{Examples are:} Reducing idle instances; Rightsizing workloads to prevent over-provisioning; and Using queues to spread out workloads over the available resources in time.\\
%
\noindent\textbf{Network Efficiency}: The optimization of network usage by minimizing unnecessary data transmission, reducing bandwidth overhead, and optimizing routing paths to create communication with minimal energy consumption. \textit{Examples are:} Reducing unnecessary data transfers and routing; Minimizing browser-to-server round trips (caching on client side); Using a Content Delivery Network (CDN) (caching on server side); and Efficient caching to reduce redundant data requests.\\
%
\noindent\textbf{Storage Efficiency}: The effective use and management of storage resources by reducing data duplication, compressing stored data, and eliminating unnecessary or redundant storage usage. This includes efficient data retention policies and optimizing storage access patterns. \textit{Examples are:} Data deduplication; Compressing and archiving data; and Removing outdated or unnecessary files.\\
%
\noindent\textbf{Code Efficiency}: The optimization of software code to reduce unnecessary processing, improve execution speed, and lower resource consumption (for example: CPU, memory). This optimization can be done through profiling, code review, and choice of the algorithm. Efficient code can minimize the system's workload, leading to reduced energy usage and improved overall performance. \textit{Examples are:} Refactoring legacy code; Designing the API efficiently; and Utilizing cloud design patterns.\\
%
\noindent\textbf{Infrastructure Efficiency}: The efficient use of cloud and physical infrastructure, which ensures that computing resources (such as virtual machines, containers, and physical servers) are optimally utilized without over-provisioning or under-utilization. It includes selecting the appropriate infrastructure components for specific workloads. \textit{Examples are:} Deploying applications in regions closer to users; Using managed services to reduce infrastructure overhead since they are more optimized; and Optimizing the selection of cloud instances (for instance: VM types, containers).\\
%
\noindent\textbf{Energy Efficiency}: The reduction of energy consumption across all layers of the computing stack by using energy-efficient hardware, optimizing system usage, and reducing overall power consumption through intelligent deployment strategies and workload management. This mostly includes using renewable energy sources where and when possible. \textit{Examples are:} Deploying applications in data centers powered by renewable energy; Reducing power consumption of servers during low demand; Minimizing hardware usage through efficient scheduling of the workload.\\
%
\noindent\textbf{Dynamic Resource Allocation}: The automatic and real-time allocation or deallocation of system resources (for instance: compute, memory, storage) based on current demand. It helps to resources be available when needed but do not remain idle when demand is low. This reduces over-provisioning and prevents resource waste. This is also about resource usage, but primarily about allocation. \textit{Examples are:} Auto-scaling to match demand fluctuations; Scaling of only the necessary components during peak load; and Dynamically adjusting resource allocations based on workload intensity.
