\begin{abstract}
This paper explores how Large Language Models (LLMs) behave in a classic experimental finance paradigm widely known for eliciting bubbles and crashes in human participants. We adapt an established trading design—where traders buy and sell a risky asset with a known fundamental value—and introduce several LLM-based agents, both in single-model markets (all traders are instances of the same LLM) and in mixed-model “battle royale” settings (multiple LLMs competing in the same market). Our findings reveal that LLMs generally exhibit a “textbook-rational” approach, pricing the asset near its fundamental value, and show only a muted tendency toward bubble formation. Further analyses indicate that LLM-based agents display less trading strategy variance in contrast to humans. Taken together, these results highlight the risk of relying on LLM-only data to replicate human-driven market phenomena, as key behavioral features—such as large, emergent bubbles—were not robustly reproduced. While LLMs clearly possess the capacity for strategic decision-making, their relative consistency and rationality suggest that they do not accurately mimic human market dynamics.
\end{abstract}
\vspace{-1em}
\vspace{-1em}

\section{Introduction}
\vspace{-1em}
\label{intro}
Financial decision-making is a complex and multifaceted process shaped by the interplay of risk, capital, and time. Traditional frameworks emphasize rational trade-offs, but human behavior often deviates due to biases such as perceptual ambiguities, overconfidence and herding behavior, these biases can cause short-term buying pressure and the over-inflation of asset values, culminating in market bubbles. These patterns highlight the persistent challenge of achieving optimal outcomes in dynamic and uncertain financial environments.

While there is extensive literature on human behavior in experimental finance settings, little work has been done on how large language models (LLMs) perform in these paradigms. Do LLMs exhibit similar behavioral biases, are they more "rational" than humans, or do they fall victim to different biases not seen in humans. As LLMs increasingly contribute to domains previously managed by humans it is becoming more important to understand the tendencies and biases that these models exhibit in financial settings. 

Previous studies suggest that interactions with agents differ significantly from those with humans (\cite{Anllo2024}), while others propose that LLM behavior could supplement human studies (\cite{Leng2024}; \cite{Horton2023}). This paper investigates both claims by exploring how LLMs conduct themselves in experimental finance markets and their similarity to human traders. 



\paragraph{Contributions} LLM markets behave much more "rationally" than humans; they generally trade much closer to fundamental value. LLM markets are not similar to human markets and should not be used to mimic or pilot human data. In a competitive setting, the spread between the highest and lowest performing models is fairly moderate—indicating that while some agents capitalize on slightly better trading opportunities, the market does not produce extreme winners or losers.

