\section{Experiment}
We focus on a classic experimental finance paradigm with an easily computable fundamental value, the true mathematical price of an asset; see Equation ~\ref{fundamental_stock_value}. The experimental design, taken directly from \cite{doi:10.1073/pnas.1318416111} and ; \cite{holt2017price} and involves an experimental asset market where subjects trade a risky asset in an open-call market for 30 periods) operated using the oTree experiment platform (\cite{otree-paper}). Participants are given 4 stocks and 100 units of cash. Participants have two options: either invest cash in the risky asset, which pays out dividends of either 0.4 or 1 with equal probability or hold cash which generates 5\% interest rate per trading period. 

At the conclusion of the experiment, the risky asset is redeemed for a fixed value of 14 units, representing the fundamental price, calculated by the formula:
\begin{equation}
\label{fundamental_stock_value}
  \frac{\mathbb{E}[\text{Dividend}]}{\mathbb{E}[\text{Interest Rate}]} = \frac{0.7}{0.05} = 14
\end{equation}

Because of these parameter decisions; dividend schedule, cash interest rate, and redemption price, the fundamental value remains constant throughout the entirety of the experiment, enabling us to objectively classify when the market is in a ”bubble” state \cite{bostian2005price}.

More details of the experiment can be found in Appendix \ref{Additional Experimental Details}.

In adapting the task to LLM-based agents, our primary goal was to best emulate the experiment presented to human subjects. The description of the task setting mirrors the original instruction video, shown to human participants. In every round of the experiment, each agent simultaneously submits orders to the market as well as expectations regarding current and future market prices.

A common challenge in adapting multi-round games to LLM-based players is the persistence of memory between rounds, which is critical to learning and adapting strategies. Building off previous works (\cite{fish2024algorithmiccollusionlargelanguage}, we allow agents to communicate with future versions of themselves by allowing them to read and write their own "Insights" and Thoughts" at each round. This structure incorporates Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning (\cite{wei2022chain}), which has been shown to be helpful in eliciting the reasoning ability of LLMs, and allows for an interpretable window into how agents perceive and wish to interact with the market. Further experiment details and the full prompt are presented in Appendix \ref{Additional Experimental Details} and Appendix \ref{prompt_details}, respectively.

