\section{Discussion and Conclusions}
\vspace{-1em}
In this study, we investigated how Large Language Models (LLMs) behave in an experimental finance paradigm that has historically elicited asset bubbles and crashes when conducted with human participants. Through both single-model markets and mixed-model “battle royale” competitions, we found that LLM-based agents typically converge on prices near the fundamental value, thereby exhibiting more “textbook-rational” behavior than human subjects. Although some single-model markets did display mild bubble-like phenomena—particularly GPT-3.5, which inflated prices without a clear crash, and Mistral and Grok 2, which produced modest run-ups, —these tendencies were weaker than what is commonly observed in human-only markets. Moreover, individual outcomes in LLM-driven markets tended to cluster more tightly around similar final portfolio values, indicating less strategic heterogeneity than seen among humans. These findings collectively suggest that although LLMs may serve as rational traders, they seldom replicate the pronounced boom-and-bust patterns that emerge from human behavioral biases. We also analyze the forecasting behavior of these LLM agents and find that the majority of the LLMs generate more accurate forecasts of future price behavior than human traders, though the performance of LLM agent models varied dramatically in their ability to forecast rationally. Across the board the LLM agents performed differently than humans on most tasks, therefore, our results caution against using LLMs as replacements or for piloting for human participants in experimental finance studies. Future work may explore whether new prompting strategies, incentives can induce more human-like trading patterns from LLM-based agents, but for now it remains clear that these models trade with a consistency and rationality that diverge significantly from the behaviors displayed by human counterparts.


