We now elaborate on the details of the experimental set up. There are 3 practice trading rounds followed by 30 rounds of trading in the main experiment. The practice rounds have no impact on the main experiment rounds and serve only to familiarize participants with the market setting. During each round, participants interact with the market through a sequence of screens.

Below, we describe in greater detail the sequence of screens shown in the human-version of the experiment. Note that (4) is omitted in our adaptation of the experiment for LLM-based agents and screens (1) - (3) are condensed into a single "screen", or more accurately call to the model. Please refer to Appendix \ref{prompt_details} for further discussion of the adaptation of the experiment.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Order Submission Screen (20 seconds): Participants submit orders to buy or sell the asset. To simplify the interface, all orders are entered via a grid with quantity along the horizontal axis and price along the vertical axis. Prices are restricted to be within $\pm 20\%$ of the current market price.
\item Forecast Screen (30 seconds): Participants submit their expectations about current and future market prices. Price forecasts are made by positioning a slider.  All sliders had a lower limit of zero, while the upper limits varied depending on how far out the forecast was made. For the current round and two rounds hence, the upper limit was twice the current market price.  The five-round forecast was limited to 2.5 times the market price, and for the ten-round forecast, the limit is set to triple the market price.  To aid in the aesthetics of the sliders, the upper limit was rounded up to the nearest tick mark.   For example, if the current market price is 36, the upper limit for the current round forecast is 72.  If the tick marks on the slider are determined to be spaced at 5, the upper limit is rounded up to 75.
\item Round Results Screen (10 seconds): Participants see the actual realized price for that period, how many shares were traded in total, and how many shares they traded.
\item Risk-Elicitation Screen (20 seconds): Participants see 4 pairs of lotteries that change as they select riskier or less risky lotteries. This task applies the Dynamically Optimized Sequential Experiment (DOSE) to the \citet{holt_laury_2002} 10-question set, as outlined by \citet{chapman2024dynamically} 

