\section*{Appendix A: A short recap of the Frauchiger--Renner paradox and Local Friendliness no-go theorem}
The protocols of the Frauchiger--Renner~\cite{frauchiger2018quantum} and Local Friendliness~\cite{bong2020strong,cavalcanti2021implications} both rely on quantum realisations of Bell nonlocality~\cite{bell1964einstein,Bell_1976,brunner_bell_2014}.
More specifically, the Frauchiger--Renner paradox transforms Hardy's example~\cite{hardy1993nonlocality} of a Bell scenario with two parties $A,B$ into a protocol with two superobserver-observer pairs. 
In Hardy's Bell scenario, each party can choose one out of two measurements to perform, while in the FR protocol the two measurements are performed in a single round by the corresponding superobserver-observer pair: one by the observer, and the other by their corresponding superobserver.

In the Frauchiger--Renner paradox, Alice and Bob share a Hardy state $\ket{\psi_0}_{S_A S_B} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}(\ket{00}+\ket{10}+\ket{11})_{S_A S_B}$ and measure their system in the computational basis, modelled unitarily as $U_A,U_B$. Next Ursula and Wigner undo their measurements by applying $U_A^\dagger, U_B^\dagger$ and measure $S_A,S_B$ in the $X$-basis, obtaining outcomes $u,w$, respectively. 
Upon Ursula and Wigner obtaining $u=-,w=-$, Ursula next reasons about which outcome Bob has, and reasons about what Bob predicts for Alice's outcome, and what Bob thinks Alice's prediction for Wigner is. All agents use quantum theory to formulate their prediction: from $u=-$ Ursula argues that $b=1$, and from $b=1$ she argues that Bob reasons that $a=0$, from which in turn Alice would argue that $w=+$.
From this chain of reasoning, Ursula finds a contradiction.

A stronger paradox can be found by having only classical observers doing the reasoning (here Ursula and Wigner who need not be modelled unitarily by anyone in the protocol)~\cite{walleghem_refined_2024}.

In the Local Friendliness protocol, additional choices $x,y$ are given to Ursula and Wigner whether to perform their measurement as just described or to look at the outcome of Alice and Bob, respectively. 
In this way, by having Ursula look at Alice's outcome and Wigner perform his measurement, the correlation $a,w$ is empirically accessible after the protocol.
A contradiction is derived when assuming the Local Friendliness assumption, i.e. the conjunction of absolute outcomes of performed measurements and a notion of locality named Local Agency, which allows only events in the future lightcone of a choice to be correlated with that choice.

Both the FR and LF arguments can be constructed from other realisations of Bell nonlocality (and contextuality)~\cite{walleghem_refined_2024,haddara2022possibilistic,haddara2024local,walleghem2023extended,walleghem2024connecting}.


\section*{Appendix B: An LF version of our first protocol and no-go theorem} \label{sec:Appendix_LF}

The protocol 
%to prove \Cref{th:theorem_nosignalling} 
is similar to our first protocol, but now Bob additionally makes a choice whether to reveal his outcome to Wigner or not before falling into the black hole, and Alice encodes her outcome $a$ in the computational $Z$-basis of a qubit $Q_A$ which she sends to another observer Charlie before falling in the black hole. Charlie makes a choice $x=0,1$ whether to measure $Q_A$ in the $Z$-basis (decoding Alice's outcome) for $x=0$ or in the $\ket{\pm}$ basis for $x=1$. 
%This protocol leads to the following no-go theorem, whose proof can be found in the Appendix.

\begin{theorem} \label{th:theorem_nosignalling}
    If an observer can obtain and decode the Hawking radiation encoding the sealed lab state of an observer and still get a short message from that observer, sent inside the black hole, an operational violation of no-signalling is found.
\end{theorem}



\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{th:theorem_nosignalling}.]
Alice and Bob, residing in sealed labs, share an entangled Hardy state, and each measure their qubit in the computational basis, modelled unitarily as $U_A,U_B$. Alice encodes her outcome in the computational basis of a qubit $Q_A$, which she sends to Charlie, and next Alice falls into the black hole. Charlie has a choice $x=0,1$ whether to measure the qubit in the computational $Z$-basis or in the $\ket{\pm}$ basis. Somewhat later, Bob makes a choice $y=0,1$ to reveal his outcome to Wigner or not, and falls into the black hole. Alice and Bob, once inside the black hole, try to send their outcomes to Ursula and Wigner (who will fall into the black hole somewhat later). Before falling in, Ursula and Wigner retrieve the Hawking radiation containing the states of Alice's and Bob's labs, apply the `undoing operation' $U_A^\dagger,U_B^\dagger$, respectively, to the decoded lab states of Alice and Bob in the Hawking radiation. Next Ursula, Wigner and Charlie all fall into the black hole, aiming to still obtain the outcomes $a,b$ of Alice and Bob. 

Regarding the spacetime arrangement (important as our argument will involve no-signalling), Alice falls in the black hole before Bob, such that Ursula can retrieve Alice's Hawking radiation outside of the future lightcone of Bob's choice $y$, and obtain $u,b$ outside of the future lightcone of $y$. Moreover, Charlie makes his measurement choice $x$ such that Wigner's measurement on Bob's Hawking radiation takes place outside of the future lightcone of $x$. The spacetime arrangement is sketched in \Cref{fig:LF_BH}.  

\begin{figure}[h]
         \centering
\includestandalone[width=0.42\textwidth]{figures/LF_BH}
         \caption{The LF-black hole protocol in a Penrose diagram; $x,y$ denote choices by Charlie and Bob, respectively, and the smaller black dots denote operations, by Alice, Ursula, Bob, Wigner and Charlie.}
         \label{fig:LF_BH}
\end{figure}



Alice and Bob measure $S_A,S_B$, which can be modelled as unitaries $U_A,U_B$, and more specifically as $CNOT$ gates if we assume their initial memory states are $\ket{0}_A,\ket{0}_B$, i.e. \begin{equation}
    CNOT_{S_A A} = \Pi^{S_A}_0 \otimes \mathbb{1}_A + \Pi^{S_A}_1 \otimes (|1\rangle\langle 0|+|0\rangle\langle 1|)_{A},  
\end{equation} with $\Pi_0 = \bra{0}\ket{0}$, and similarly for Bob's measurement and Alice's action of preparing her outcome in the qubit $S_C$ she sends to Charlie. Thus, the actions described thus far are \begin{equation}
    CNOT_{S_A S_C} CNOT_{S_A A} CNOT_{S_B B} \ket{\psi_0}_{S_A S_B} \ket{000}_{AB S_C}, 
\end{equation} where we are sloppy about the tensor product ordering in the initial state (we omit some tensor products $\otimes$ and swap operations to bring the initial state into the ordering of the operations that act on it but hope this is clear from the use of indices). 

Note that when Charlie obtains the outcome $c=+$ for $x=1$, we have that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:S_C+}
    \ket{+}\bra{+}_{S_C} CNOT_{S_A S_C} = \ket{+}\bra{+}_{S_C} \otimes \mathbb{1}_{S_A}.
\end{equation} Therefore, if Charlie obtains $c=+$, then Ursula's undoing of Alice will indeed reset her lab back to the initial state, the same that would be obtained if Alice did not prepare her outcome in the computational basis of the qubit $S_C$.

Assuming all involved outcomes $a,b,u,w,c$ can be brought together inside the black hole, the argument goes as follows.


First of all, as one can verify using quantum theory's predictions and \cref{eq:S_C+}, we have \begin{equation}
    p(u{=}-,w{=}-|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+) > 0.
\end{equation} 

Next, quantum theory predicts that $$p(u{=}-,b{=}0|x{=}1,y{=}0,c{=}+)=0.$$ As all of $u,b,c$ are obtained spacelike separated from each other and not in the future lightcone of $y$, we can give a no-signalling argument that the joint outcomes $ubc$ should be independent of $y$. Namely, we could give Ursula and Bob extra choices $x_u,x_b \in \{0,1\}$ whether (for choice $1$) or not to encode their outcomes in the computational basis of extra qubits they hold that are maximally entangled with some extra qubits Charlie holds. Then by measuring these qubits in the computational basis, Charlie can obtain the correlations $ubc$ if Ursula and Bob choose $x_u=1=x_b$. However, these correlations should be independent of $x_u,x_b,y$ by no-signalling as these choices are all spacelike separated from where Charlie obtains these correlations $ubc$. Therefore, we obtain that $$ p(u{=}-,b{=}0|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+)=0.$$


Next, using quantum theory and no-signalling, $$p(a{=}0,b{=}1|x{=}1,y{=}1)=p(a{=}0,b{=}1|x{=}0,y{=}0)=0,$$ and thus also $p(a{=}0,b{=}1|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+)=0$.

Finally, $$p(a{=}1,w{=}-|x{=}1,y{=}1) = p(a{=}1,w{=}-|x{=}0,y{=}1)$$ by no-signalling, and quantum theory gives that $$p(a{=}1,w{=}-|x{=}0,y{=}1) = p(a{=}1,c{=}a,w{=}-|x{=}0,y{=}1) = 0,$$ meaning that  
$$p(a{=}1,w{=}-|x{=}1,y{=}1) = 0$$ and thus also $p(a{=}1,w{=}-|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+) = 0$. 

Thus, we obtain that the distribution $p(a,b,u,w|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+)$ should satisfy the conditions that \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
            &p(u{=}-,w{=}-|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+) > 0, \\
            &p(u{=}-,b{=}0|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+)=0, \\
            &p(a{=}0,b{=}1|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+)=0 \\
            &p(a{=}1,w{=}-|x{=}1,y{=}1,c{=}+) = 0.
    \end{split}
\end{equation} However, this cannot be true as then $u=-,w=-$ can occur, but $u=-$ implies that $b=1$ and $w=-$ implies that $a=0$, but $a=0,b=1$ cannot occur.   
\end{proof} 


\textit{Comparison to the LF protocol.} 
Note that, compared to the LF protocol~\cite{bong2020strong}, which involves Alice, Bob, Ursula and Wigner, we have an additional party involving Charlie who gets a qubit from Alice. This is because we cannot simply let Ursula and Wigner perform their measurements spacelike separated from the choices $y,x$, respectively, and dive in to obtain $u,b$ jointly outside of the future lightcone of $y$ and $a,w$ jointly outside of the future lightcone of $x$. Namely, after Alice and Bob have performed their measurements, choose whether or not to reveal their measurements and fall in the black hole, Ursula and Wigner have to wait some time for the Hawking radiation containing the information about the lab states of Alice and Bob to come out of the black hole. Estimates of this timescale that Ursula and Wigner would have to wait for an old black hole are of the order of the scrambling time $r_s \log r_s$~\cite{hayden2007black} (with $r_s$ the Schwarzschild radius), and furthest we could position Ursula and Wigner away from each other are at antipodal locations from the black hole. But then light rays sent at $x$ or $y$ might reach Wigner or Ursula, respectively, before they can decode the Hawking radiation, so that signalling is possible. The trick with having this additional prepare-and-measure part has been applied in Wigner's friend scenarios as well to obtain EWF arguments based on contextuality rather than nonlocality~\cite{walleghem2023extended,walleghem2024connecting}. 

\section*{Appendix C: Strengthened HR-firewall argument}
Consider the following protocol, a slightly altered firewall paradox. 
Bob holds the early radiation, and distills a qubit (possibly precomputed using Oppenheim and Unruh's argument~\cite{Oppenheim_2014}), maximally entangled with the mode $C$ that Charlie holds near the horizon of an old black hole: $\ket{00}+\ket{11}_{BC}$. The mode $C$ is also maximally entangled with Alice's mode inside the black hole: $\ket{01}-\ket{10}_{AC}$.
Charlie chooses $y=0,1$ to measure his qubit in the $Z$ or $X$ basis. Bob always measures his qubit $C$ in the $Z$ basis. 
Alice chooses $x=0,1$ to measure her qubit in a basis such that Alice and Charlie violate Bell nonlocality, for example by choosing $-(X+Z)/\sqrt{2}$- and $(-Z+X)/\sqrt{2}$-measurements, such that the empirical probabilities $p(a_x,b_y|x,y)$ violate Bell nonlocality~\cite{Bell_1976,brunner_bell_2014} and thus by Fine's theorem~\cite{abramsky2011sheaf,fine1982hidden,fine1982joint} a global distribution $p(a_0,a_1,b_0,b_1)$ reproducing the empirical correlations $p(a_x,b_y|x,y)$ cannot exist. Now, quantum theory predicts that Bob and Charlie will always have the same outcome $b=c$ when $y=0$, as $\langle b, \lnot c |_{BC} (\ket{00}+\ket{11}) = 0$. Therefore, we have that $p(a,b,c|x,y=0) = \delta_{b,c}p(a,b|x,y=0)$ and thus also \begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    p(a,c|x,y=0) &= p(a,c=b|x,y=0) \\ &= p(a,b|x,y=0) \\ &= p(a,b|x,y=1),     
\end{split}
\end{equation} where we used no-signalling to conclude that $p(a,b|x,y) = p(a,b|x)$; we can let Bob perform his measurement before Charlie jumps in so that he obtains $a,b$ spacelike separated from Charlie's choice $y$. 
This implies that the empirical correlations of $p(a,b|x,y=0)$ are those of $p(a,c|x,y=1)$. Therefore, the joint correlations $p(a,c|x,y=0),p(a,c|x,y=1)$ are equal to $p(a,b|x,y=1),p(a,c|x,y=1)$, and should all be marginals from the global distribution $p(a,b,c|x,y=1)$, which is empirically accessible if Alice, Bob and Charlie can meet inside the black hole. 
If such a distribution $p(a,b,c|x,y=1)$ exists, then by a gluing procedure~\cite{walleghem2024connecting,fine1982joint,haddara2024local} we can produce a global distribution reproducing all empirical correlations $p(a,b|x=1,y=1),p(a,b|x=0,y=1),p(a,c|x=1,y=1),p(a,c|x=0,y=1)$, which are the same empirical correlations as the empirical Bell scenario correlations $p(a,c|x,y)$ for $x,y \in \{0,1\}$ that violate Bell nonlocality.
However, as we explained a few lines ago, by Fine's theorem such global distribution cannot exist, and we find a contradiction. 

The gluing procedure unfolds as follows. Denoting $a|x$ by $a_x$, for example using $a_0$ for the outcome variable $a$ when $x=0$, the correlations $p(a_0,b|x=0,y=1)$ can be written as \begin{equation}
   \begin{split}
       p(a_0,&b,c|x{=}0,y{=}1) = p(b,c|x{=}0,y{=}1)p(a_0|x{=}0,y{=}1,b,c) \\ &= p(b,c|x{=}1,y{=}1)p(a_0|x{=}0,y{=}1,b,c),
   \end{split} 
\end{equation} using no-signalling for $p(b,c|x=0,y=1)=p(b,c|x=1,y=1)$, and noting that $p(a_0,b,c|x=0,y=1)$ is also empirically accessible if an observer as Charlie can obtain all of $a_0,b,c$ inside the black hole. 
Therefore, the empirical correlations $p(a_0,b|x=0,y=1),p(a_0,c|x=0,y=1),p(a_1,b|x=1,y=1),p(a_1,c|x=1,y=1)$ can all be obtained from the ``glued'' global distribution \begin{equation}
    p(a_0,a_1,b,c) := p(a_1,b,c|x{=}1,y{=}1) p(a_0|b,c,x{=}0,y{=}1).
\end{equation}


We have thus strengthened the firewall argument to the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}[Strengthened firewall argument]
    Assuming (i) that the early radiation is entangled with the late radiation, (ii) high entanglement across the horizon and (iii) the possibility for Charlie to distill a qubit highly entangled with Bob's from the outside, an operational violation of no-signalling is found.
\end{theorem}



