We note that the value used in the computations, $\nu=0.8$, is smaller than the theoretical bound that guaranties convergence of the perturbation scheme, $\bar\nu_0$, but is larger than the bound found for the global stability, $\nu_0$. In fact, we have numerically observed a $\theta$-periodic stable solution (from $10$ different initial conditions) from  $\nu\simeq-1.6$ to $\nu$ larger than $10$. Clearly the theoretical bounds are not optimal. 

 Finally, out of the $\nu$-region where only the $\theta$-periodic solution exists, we find solutions with different typology. We show in Figure \ref{conf} the phase space $(q(t),\dot q(t))$ for some examples when $\omega=0.8$: (A) $\nu=-4$  the phase space of initial conditions is broken in two, each one has a limiting $\theta$-periodic solution that are symmetric with respect the reflection $(q,\dot q)\mapsto -(q,\dot q)$ ; (B) $\nu=-2$ the stationary solution moves in a limited phase space region and (C) $\nu=-1.85$ where there is a numerically stable $2\theta$-periodic solution \cite{one2}.

