\section{Impact of Medicaid on medical visits}\label{sec:oregon}


All numerical values in this section have been scaled by a factor of 100, as they primarily represent probabilities. 

Understanding the causal impact of expanding public health insurance coverage remains a central issue in health policy. An example is the Oregon Medicaid experiment of 2008, where the random allocation of the opportunity to enroll in Medicaid through a lottery provided a credible instrumental variable for identifying causal effects. Prior research using this lottery has primarily focused on LATE \cite{finkelstein2012oregon}, revealing significant impacts on healthcare utilization, mental health, and financial outcomes, but limited or ambiguous effects on objectively measured physical health indicators \cite{baicker2013oregon}. 

We utilize the Oregon Medicaid lottery as an instrumental variable to explicitly estimate marginal causal effects of insurance enrollment on primary care utilization, specifically examining the probability of any primary care visit within one year and adjusting for relevant covariates such as baseline mental health status (e.g., diagnoses of depression or anxiety). Specifically, the instrument $Z$ is defined as ``Selected in the lottery,'' where $Z = 1$ if the individual was selected in the Oregon Medicaid lottery, and $Z = 0$ otherwise. The treatment is $A = \texttt{data\$approved\_app}$, which indicates whether the individual's Medicaid application was approved; that is, $A = 1$ if approved, and $A = 0$ otherwise. The relevant baseline covariates are $\texttt{W.dep\_dx\_0m}$, a binary indicator equal to 1 if the respondent had ever been told by a health professional that they have depression or anxiety prior to the lottery, and 0 otherwise; \texttt{W.zip\_msa\_list} binary indicating whether zip code of subject is in a metropolitan statistical area; \texttt{W.birthyear\_list} scaled to 0-1 with subject's birth year; and \texttt{W.female\_list} binary indicating gender. The outcome is $Y = \texttt{doc\_any\_12m}$, which equals 1 if the individual had at least one primary care visit during the 12 months following lottery notification, and 0 otherwise.  Sample size is $n=16403$. 

As in Section \ref{hidnohid}, we consider $S$ to be the relevant set of baseline covariates and $S'=\texttt{W.dep\_dx\_0m}$. Recall that we previously justified that the specific choice of covariates included in $g^*$ is irrelevant, as all such choices identify the same causal effect. We now present evidence that the instrument—lottery assignment—is effectively random, that is, independent of covariates. First, the lottery drawings conducted by the state consisted of mechanical, household-level random selections from a fixed list, with no stratification or conditioning on covariates. As reported in \cite{finkelstein2012oregon}, the authors independently replicated the lottery’s random selection procedure through computer simulations, confirming that the draws relied on a genuine random number generator and not on any rule correlated with baseline characteristics. Furthermore, Table II, Panel B in their study reports the results of comprehensive balance tests on the covariates in all cases. The tests fail to reject the null hypothesis of equality between groups, providing strong empirical support for the independence of the instrument. Table V in \cite{finkelstein2012oregon} reports a LATE of $21.2$ for outpatient visits last six months. 

In Table 2, two distinct interventional stochastic policies of the form $h^*(w) = P(Z^* = 1 \mid W_{S'} = w)$ were specified manually. The G-computation formula from Theorem \ref{gcomp} was used to estimate $\Psi = {E}[Y^{h^*}]$, with each component substituted by its corresponding estimate obtained through the TMLE procedure described in Section \ref{sec:semi} including all the 4 relevant baseline covariates in the clever weights and offset, starting from initial estimates fitted using HAL. The reported standard errors $\hat{\sigma}$ are plug-in estimators of $\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(\text{EIC}) / n}$, where EIC denotes the efficient influence curve derived in Proposition \ref{prop:eic}. 


