\textbf{Overall Performance Comparison (RQ1).} To evaluate the overall efficacy of introducing T2I-generated images, we first compare our core proposed method---using SDXL as the T2I model, the keyword-enhanced prompt (P2), and cross-attention fusion (F2)---against the baselines. The results are summarized in \cref{tab:main_results}.

\textbf{Generated images provide consistent performance gains across different model scales.} Our primary finding is that augmenting text with instance-specific generated images leads to statistically significant performance improvements over text-only counterparts, regardless of the base model's scale. When using BERT-base as the foundation, our method (`Ours (BERT + Gen. Image)`) achieves a significant gain over the B1 text-only baseline (e.g., a 2.2\% increase in Macro-F1 for Amazon Reviews).

\textbf{More importantly, this benefit extends to powerful Large Language Models.} The most compelling evidence for our thesis comes from comparing B2 (Llama-2-7B Text-Only) with B6 (Llama-2-7B + Gen. Image). The addition of the synthetic visual modality boosts performance even further, establishing a new state-of-the-art in our experiments (e.g., a 0.7\% absolute Macro-F1 increase on Amazon Reviews). This result is critical: it demonstrates that the information contained in the generated image is not merely redundant to the advanced understanding of a powerful LLM, but rather provides complementary, grounding information that enhances its predictive capabilities. This directly addresses the potential concern that the benefits of a synthetic modality might disappear when a sufficiently strong language model is used. For a more detailed breakdown of model performance, including confusion matrices, see \cref{app:supplementary_results}.

\textbf{Instance-specific generated images prove more valuable than randomly or generically retrieved images.} Our BERT-based model significantly outperforms both B3 (random image features) and B4 (retrieved image features). The comparison with B3 confirms that the performance gain stems from meaningful visual information, not merely from an increase in model parameters. The contrast with B4 is more illuminating: images dynamically generated for a specific text instance are more effective than generic images retrieved based on keywords. This suggests that generated images can capture unique context and details specific to the text instance, which retrieved images lack.

\textbf{The oracle analysis reveals a significant potential for this strategy, highlighting the current bottleneck in T2I generation quality.} The B5 (Human-Curated Image) oracle baseline achieves the highest performance on the BERT-based models. Interestingly, the exploratory B6 model (Llama-2 + Gen. Image) manages to match or even slightly exceed the performance of the B5 oracle on the AG News task. This suggests that as the base language model becomes more powerful, its ability to effectively reason over and fuse even imperfect visual information improves. Nevertheless, the gap between B6 and B5 on the more visually descriptive Amazon Reviews task indicates that further improvements in T2I generation quality are still the key to unlocking the full potential of this strategy.

\textbf{The magnitude of improvement appears to be task-dependent, hinting at the findings for RQ5.} The performance gain is considerably larger on Amazon Reviews (approx. 2.2\% Macro-F1 gain) compared to AG News (approx. 0.3\% Macro-F1 gain). This provides initial support for our hypothesis that the complementary value of a synthetic visual modality is greater for tasks where the text is naturally rich in visual descriptions (e.g., product appearance, usage scenarios).

\textbf{Impact of T2I Models and Prompts (RQ2 \& RQ3).} To deconstruct the key factors in the generation stage, we conducted a detailed ablation study on the Amazon Reviews dataset, with the results presented in \cref{tab:t2i_prompt_impact} and illustrated qualitatively in \cref{fig:gen_strategies}. We observed similar, albeit less pronounced, trends on the AG News dataset, which are omitted here for brevity.

