\section{Coulomb branches}\label{Coulomb}
In this section, we give a short treatment of the Coulomb branch and set up notations that will be important for later sections. We work over the complex numbers $\mathbb{C}$.

\subsubsection*{Lie-theoretic notations}

We let $G$ denote a connected complex reductive group, $T \subset G$ a Cartan subgroup, and $W = N_G(T)/T$ the corresponding Weyl group. We write $\Phi$ for the set of roots of $G$. We fix a subset $\Phi^+ \subset \Phi$ of positive roots, or equivalently, we fix a Borel subgroup $T \subset B \subset G$.

The coweight lattice of $G$ is denoted by $\Lambda$, and its submonoid of dominant coweights is denoted by $\Lambda^+$.


\subsubsection*{Affine Grassmannian} For a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra $A$, let $G_A$ denote the sheaf\footnote{Sheaves are defined with respect to the fppf topology.} on the category of affine schemes over $\mathbb{C}$ (that is, the opposite category of the category of $\mathbb{C}$-algebras) defined by sending a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra $R$ to $G(R \otimes A)$.

Denote $\oh = \mathbb{C}[[t]]$ (the ring of formal power series) and $\ok = \mathbb{C}\cct$ (the field of Laurent series). In particular, we have
\begin{align*}
    G_\oh(R) &= G(R[[t]]), \\
    G_\ok(R) &= G(R\cct),
\end{align*}
for any $\mathbb{C}$-algebra $R$. The affine Grassmannian of $G$ is defined as the quotient
\[
\Gr_G = G_\ok / G_\oh.
\]


Let $\BI$ be the Iwahori subgroup
\[
\BI = \{g \in G_\oh : g(0) \in B\},
\]
that is, $\BI$ is the preimage of $B$ under the evaluation map $\operatorname{ev}_{t=0} : G_\oh \to G$.

For $\lambda \in \Lambda$, let $t^\lambda$ denote the corresponding point in $\Gr_G$. Define the $\BI$-orbit
\[
C_\lambda = \BI \cdot t^\lambda \subset \Gr_G,
\]
and let $C_{\leq \lambda}$ denote its closure. Each $C_\lambda$ is isomorphic to an affine space, and $C_{\leq \lambda}$ admits the structure of a projective variety. We define a partial order on $\Lambda$ by declaring that $\mu \leq \lambda$ if and only if $C_\mu \subset C_{\leq \lambda}$. In particular,
\[
C_{\leq \lambda} = \bigsqcup_{\mu \leq \lambda} C_\mu.
\]
We remark that $C_\lambda$ (and hence $C_{\leq \lambda}$) is $G_\oh$-invariant if and only if $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$. 


\subsubsection*{Equivariant Borel--Moore homology}

In this paper, if $X$ is a complex quasi-projective variety, we use $H_\bullet(X) = H^{-\bullet}(X, \omega_X)$ to denote the Borel--Moore homology groups of $X$ with complex coefficients, using the classical topology. Here, $\omega_X$ is the dualizing complex of $X$. Similarly, if $K$ is an algebraic group acting algebraically on $X$, we define the equivariant Borel--Moore homology $H^K_\bullet(X) \coloneqq H_K^{-\bullet}(X, \omega_X)$,
where both $K$ and $X$ are considered with the classical topology. We refer to~\cite{BernsteinLunts} for the basics of cohomology of equivariant sheaves.


\subsubsection*{The pure gauge Coulomb branch}

Let $G_i = G_{\oh / t^i \oh}$ for any positive integer $i$. For each $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$, the action of $G_\oh$ on $C_{\leq \lambda}$ factors through $G_i$ for all sufficiently large $i$. We set
\[
H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda}) \coloneqq H^{G_i}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda})
\]
for any such choice of $i$. It is easy to see that this definition is independent of $i$. We define
\[
\sA_G=H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \coloneqq \varinjlim_{\lambda \in \Lambda^+} H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda}).
\]
We can similarly define $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$, $H^G_\bullet(\Gr_G)$, and so on.

There is a convolution product $\ast$ on $\sA_G$ turning it into a finitely generated commutative $\mathbb{C}$-algebra~\cite{BFM,BFN}. The resulting algebra $(\sA_G,\ast)$ is called the \emph{(pure gauge) Coulomb branch algebra}. The affine scheme $\spec \sA_G$ is smooth and is known as the \emph{(pure gauge) Coulomb branch}. We now briefly review the construction of the convolution product.

We follow~\cite{MV,BFN}. The convolution product on $H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ is defined using the diagram
\begin{equation}\label{convolution1}
\begin{tikzcd}
    \Gr_G \times \Gr_G & \ar[l, "p"'] G_\ok \times \Gr_G \ar[r, "q"] & G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} \Gr_G \ar[r, "m"] & \Gr_G,
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $p$ and $q$ are the natural projections, and $m$ is given by $m([g,g']) = [gg']$. Let $G_\oh$ act on $\Gr_G$ and $G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} \Gr_G$ from the left, and let $G_\oh \times G_\oh$ act on $G_\ok \times \Gr_G$ by
\[
(g_1, g_2) \cdot (g, [g']) = (g_1 g g_2^{-1}, [g_2 g']).
\]
Then $p$ is $G_\oh \times G_\oh$-equivariant, $m$ is $G_\oh$-equivariant, and $q$ is equivariant with respect to the first $G_\oh$-action on $G_\ok\times\Gr_G$. The convolution product is given by
\begin{equation}\label{product1}
    m_* \circ (q^*)^{-1} \circ p^* : \sA_G \otimes \sA_G \to \sA_G.
\end{equation}

Since we are dealing with infinite-dimensional spaces and groups, the Borel--Moore homology groups and the homomorphisms among them must be treated carefully. We briefly explain this, and refer to~\cite{BFN} for more details.

For $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$, we write $G_\ok^{\leq \lambda}$ for the preimage of $C_{\leq \lambda} \subset \Gr_G$ in $G_\ok$, and let $K_i$ denote the kernel of the natural homomorphism $G_\oh \to G_i$.

Let $\lambda_1, \lambda_2 \in \Lambda^+$ and set $\lambda_3 = \lambda_1 + \lambda_2$. Choose positive integers $i \gg j \gg 0$ such that the actions of $K_j$ on $C_{\lambda_2}$, and the actions of $K_i$ on $C_{\lambda_1}$, $C_{\lambda_3}$, and $G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1}/K_j$ are trivial. The diagram~\eqref{convolution1} induces the diagram below (we use the same symbols for the induced maps):
\begin{equation}\label{convolution2}
\begin{tikzcd}
    C_{\leq \lambda_1} \times C_{\leq \lambda_2} & \ar[l, "p"'] (G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} / K_j) \times C_{\leq \lambda_2} \ar[r, "q"] & G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} \times_{G_\oh} C_{\leq \lambda_2} \ar[r, "m"] & C_{\leq \lambda_3}.
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

The product~\eqref{product1} should be understood as the direct limit over $\lambda_1, \lambda_2 \in \Lambda^+$ of
\[
m_* \circ (q^*)^{-1} \circ p^* : H^{G_i}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda_1}) \otimes H^{G_j}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda_2}) \longrightarrow H^{G_i}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda_3}),
\]
where $p^*$, $q^*$, and $m^*$ are homomorphisms induced by the diagram~\eqref{convolution2}. Explicitly:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The map
    \[
    p^* : H^{G_i}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda_1}) \otimes H^{G_j}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda_2}) \longrightarrow H^{G_i \times G_j}_\bullet\left((G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} / K_j) \times C_{\leq \lambda_2}\right)
    \]
    is the pullback along $p$.

    \item The map
    \[
    q^* : H^{G_i}_\bullet\left(G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} \times_{G_\oh} C_{\leq \lambda_2}\right) \longrightarrow H^{G_i \times G_j}_\bullet\left((G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} / K_j) \times C_{\leq \lambda_2}\right)
    \]
    is the pullback along $q$, with respect to the inclusion $G_i\cong G_i\times\{e\} \subset G_i \times G_j$.

    \item The map
    \[
    m_* : H^{G_i}_\bullet\left(G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} \times_{G_\oh} C_{\leq \lambda_2}\right) \to H^{G_i}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda_3})
    \]
    is the pushforward along $m$.
\end{itemize}

Note that $q^*$ is an isomorphism because the $G_j$-action makes $(G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} / K_j) \times C_{\leq \lambda_2}$ into a principal $G_j$-bundle over $G_\ok^{\leq \lambda_1} \times_{G_\oh} C_{\leq \lambda_2}$.

\subsubsection*{Quantizations}

Let $\Cxh$ be a one-dimensional complex torus, which scales the parameter $t$. It is called the group of loop rotations, and there are induced actions on $G_\ok$, $G_\oh$, and $\Gr_G$. For $z \in \Cxh$ and $g(t) \in G_\ok$, the action is given by
\[
z \cdot g(t) = g(zt),
\]
and the other actions are defined similarly. See \Cref{univbun} and \Cref{Gtorsorappendix} for more discussion on the group of loop rotations.

Note that the diagrams~\eqref{convolution1} and~\eqref{convolution2} are equivariant with respect to the loop rotations. Let $\hbar \in H^2_{\Cxh}(\pt)$ be a generator, so that $H^\bullet_{\Cxh}(\pt)\cong \C[\hbar]$. Then we define the \emph{(pure gauge) quantized Coulomb branch algebra} as
\[
\sA^\hbar_G = H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G).
\]
The same reasoning as above gives the product map
\begin{equation*}
    \sA^\hbar_G \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[\hbar]} \sA^\hbar_G
    \cong
    H^{(G_\oh \times G_\oh) \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G \times \Gr_G)
    \longrightarrow
    H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)
    \cong
    \sA^\hbar_G.
\end{equation*}

As shown in~\cite{BFM}, this defines a noncommutative product structure on $\sA^\hbar_G$, with $\hbar$ a central element. The commutator on $\sA^\hbar_G$ induces a Poisson bracket on $\sA_G = \sA^\hbar_G / \hbar \sA^\hbar_G$. It was shown in~\cite{BFM} that this Poisson bracket defines a symplectic structure on the pure gauge Coulomb branch $\operatorname{Spec} \sA_G$.

\subsubsection*{The BFN Coulomb branch}\label{BFNcoul}Let $\bN$ be a complex representation of $G$, and we fix a decomposition
\[
\bN = \bigoplus_{i=1}^N \C_{\xi_i}
\] 
into one-dimensional $T$-representations, where each $\xi_i$ is a character of $T$, and $\C_{\xi_i}$ denotes the corresponding one-dimensional $T$-representation. We define the following spaces:
\[
\BT = \BT_{G,\bN} = G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} \bN_\oh,
\]
\[
\BR = \BR_{G,\bN} = \{(g, s) \in \BT : g s \in \bN_\oh \},
\]
\[
\BS = \BS_{G,\bN} = \BT_{G,\bN} / \BR_{G,\bN}.
\]

Suppose $d$ is a positive integer, we write $\BT^d$ for the vector bundle
\[
\BT^d = G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} (\bN_\oh / t^d \bN_\oh).
\]
If $\lambda \in \Lambda$, we write $\BT^d_{\leq \lambda}$ for the restriction of $\BT^d$ to $C_{\leq \lambda}$. If, furthermore, $gt^d \bN_\oh \subset \bN_\oh$ for all $[g] \in C_{\leq \lambda}$, then we write $\BR^d_{\leq \lambda}$ as the image of $\BR_{\leq \lambda}=\BR|_{C_{\leq \lambda}}$ in $\BT^d_{\leq \lambda}$. Note that the fibrewise quotient
\[
\BS_{\leq \lambda} \vcentcolon= \BT^d_{\leq \lambda} / \BR^d_{\leq \lambda}
\]
is independent of the choice of such $d$. Moreover, $\BS_\lambda \vcentcolon= \BS_{\leq \lambda}|_{C_\lambda}$ is a vector bundle, whose rank is denoted by $d_\lambda$. For $p \in \Gr_G$, we write $\BS_p$ for the fibre of $\BS_{\leq \lambda}$ at $p$, for $\lambda \in \Lambda$ satisfying $p \in C_{\leq \lambda}$. This definition is independent of the choice of $\lambda$.

For later use, we record the decomposition
\begin{equation}\label{Nweights}
    \BS_{t^\lambda} \cong \bigoplus_{\langle \xi_i, \lambda \rangle < 0} \C_{\xi_i}^{-\xi_i(\lambda)},
\end{equation}
as $T$-representations. In particular, 
\begin{equation}\label{SlambdaRank}
    d_\lambda = -\sum_{\langle \xi_i, \lambda \rangle < 0} \langle \xi_i, \lambda \rangle.
\end{equation}

For each $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$, we choose an integer $d$ so that $\BR^d_{\leq \lambda}$ is defined, and we use the same symbol $z^*_\lambda$ to denote the corresponding Gysin pullbacks
\[H^K_{\bullet}(\BT^d_{\leq\lambda})\longrightarrow H^K_\bullet(C_{\leq\lambda}),\]
where $K$ stands for one of $G_\oh$, $G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$, $T_\oh$, or $T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$. The following is a reformulation of the definition of the Coulomb branch in~\cite{BFN}, which follows from Lemma 5.11 in \textit{loc.~cit}.

\begin{df}[\cite{BFN}]
The \emph{Coulomb branch algebra} $\sA_{G,\bN}$ is the sum over $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$ of the images of $H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \lambda})$ under the composition
\[
H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \lambda})
\longrightarrow
H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\BT^d_{\leq \lambda})
\stackrel{z_\lambda^*}{\longrightarrow}
H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda})
\longrightarrow
H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G),
\]
where the first and last maps are pushforwards along inclusions.

Similarly, the \emph{quantized Coulomb branch algebra} $\sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar$ is defined as the sum over $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$ of the images of $H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \lambda})$ under the composition
\[
H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \lambda})
\longrightarrow
H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\BT^d_{\leq \lambda})
\stackrel{z_\lambda^*}{\longrightarrow}
H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda})
\longrightarrow
H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G).
\]
\end{df}

\subsubsection*{An alternative description}

We now give a new and alternative description of the Coulomb branch algebra $\sA_{G,\bN}$ and quantized Coulomb branch algebra $\sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar$.

\begin{lem}\label{existresol}
There exists an $\BI\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant resolution of singularities\footnote{That is, $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}$ is non-singular and $\rho_\lambda$ is a proper birational map.} 
\[
\rho_\lambda : \widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda} \longrightarrow C_{\leq\lambda}
\]
such that $\rho_\lambda^{-1}(\BS|_{C_\lambda})$ extends to a (necessarily unique) $\BI\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant quotient vector bundle $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda}$ of $\rho_\lambda^{-1}(\BT^d_{\leq\lambda})$. Moreover, we may assume that $\rho_\lambda$ and $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda}$ are $G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant if $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$.
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
Let $d > 0$ be a sufficiently large integer such that $\BS_{\leq\lambda}$ is well-defined. Consider the Grassmannian bundle $\Gr(d_\lambda, \BT^d_{\leq\lambda})$, which parameterizes rank $d_\lambda$ quotients of $\BT^d_{\leq\lambda}$. The vector bundle $\BS_\lambda$ defines a section of $\Gr(d_\lambda, \BT^d_{\leq\lambda})$ over $C_\lambda$. Let $\overline{C}_\lambda$ denote the closure of the image of this section. By construction, the section $C_\lambda \to \Gr(d_\lambda, \BT^d_{\leq\lambda})$ extends uniquely to a $\BI\rtimes\Cxh$-equivairant (or $G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$-equivairant when $\lambda\in\Lambda^+$) map
\[
\overline{C}_\lambda \longrightarrow \Gr(d_\lambda, \BT^d_{\leq\lambda}).
\]

As a result, $\BS_\lambda$ extends to a quotient bundle of the pullback of $\BT^d_{\leq\lambda}$ to $\overline{C}_\lambda$. We then choose $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}$ to be an $\BI\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant (or $G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant) resolution of $\overline{C}_\lambda$, which always exists (see~Theorem 3.27 of~\cite{Kollar}).
\end{proof}

We now fix the $\BI\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant resolution $\rho_\lambda : \widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda} \to C_{\leq\lambda}$ and $\widetilde\BS_{\leq\lambda}$ for each $\lambda \in \Lambda$, and $\rho_\lambda$ is assumed to be $G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant when $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$.

\begin{prop}\label{independentResol}
Let $\lambda \in \Lambda$, and let $d$ be a sufficiently large positive integer such that $\BR^d_{\leq\lambda}$ is well-defined. Then the following subspaces of $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ are equal:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
    \item The sum of the images $e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu})$ in $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ under the pushforward
    \[
    H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}) \xrightarrow{\rho_{\mu*}} H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(C_{\leq\mu}) \subset H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G),
    \]
    taken over all $\mu \leq \lambda$;

    \item The image of $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq\lambda})$ under the composition
    \[
    H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq\lambda})\longrightarrow H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BT^d_{\leq\lambda}) \stackrel{z_\lambda^*}{\longrightarrow} H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(C_{\leq\lambda}) \subset H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G);
    \]

    \item\label{item3} The direct sum
\[
\bigoplus_{\mu \leq \lambda} H_T^\bullet(\pt) \cdot p_{\mu*}\left( e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}] \right),
\]
i.e., the free $H_T^\bullet(\pt)$-submodule of $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ with basis $\{p_{\mu*}\left( e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}] \right)\}_{\mu \leq \lambda}$.
\end{enumerate}

Moreover, each element $e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}]$ is independent of the choice of resolution. The same equivalences hold when $T_\oh$ is replaced by $T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh$. Furthermore, \textup{(1)} and \textup{(2)} remain equivalent when $T_\oh$ is replaced by $G_\oh$ or $G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh$, and only dominant coweights are considered.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
We only prove the proposition for $T_\oh$; the case for $T_\oh\rtimes \Cxh$ proceeds similarly. The cases for $G_\oh$ and $G_\oh\rtimes \Cxh$ are obtained by taking Weyl invariants.

We first show that (1) $\subset$ (2). Consider the fibre diagram
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
\rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu} \ar[r, "\rho'_\mu"] \ar[d, "\widetilde{\pr}"'] 
& \BT^d_{\leq \mu} \ar[d, "\pr"]\\
\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu} \ar[r, "\rho_\mu"]
& C_{\leq\mu} 
\end{tikzcd}.
\]

Let $\widetilde{z_\lambda}^*$ be the Gysin map for the vector bundle $\rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu}$ over $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}$, then for $\gamma\in H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu})$, we have
\begin{equation} \label{pullpushcal}
\begin{aligned}
\rho_{\mu*}\left(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap \gamma\right)
&= \rho_{\mu*}\left(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap \widetilde{z_\lambda}^*\widetilde{\pr}^*\gamma\right) \\
&= \rho_{\mu*}\, \widetilde{z_\lambda}^*\left( \widetilde{\pr}^*e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap \widetilde{\pr}^*\gamma \right) \\
&= z_\lambda^* \,\rho'_{\mu*}\left(\widetilde\pr^*e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap \widetilde{\pr}^*\gamma\right).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Let $\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu}$ denote the kernel of the projection $\rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu} \to \widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}$, and $\iota:\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu}\to \rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu}$ be the inclusion. Since $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \mu}=\rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu}/\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq\mu}$, we have:
\[
\widetilde\pr^*e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu}) \subset \iota_*H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu}).
\]
As a result:
\[
\rho_{\mu*}\big(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap \gamma\big)
\in z_\lambda^* \,\rho'_{\mu*} \iota_*H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu})
\subset z_\lambda^*\,\iota_* H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \mu}),
\]
where the last inclusion follows from $\rho'_\mu(\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu}) \subset \BR^d_{\leq \mu}$. This proves that (1) $\subset$ (2).

To prove (2) $\subset$ (3), note that there is an affine stratification
\begin{equation}\label{Rdecom}
\BR^d_{\leq \lambda} = \bigsqcup_{\mu \leq \lambda} \BR^d_\mu,
\end{equation}
where $\BR^d_{\mu}=\BR^d_{\leq \lambda}|_{C_{\mu}}$ and hence
\[
H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \lambda}) = \bigoplus_{\mu \leq \lambda} H^\bullet_T(\pt)\cdot [\BR^d_{\leq\mu}].
\]
Putting $\gamma=[\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}]$ in \eqref{pullpushcal}, and using $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \mu}=\rho_\mu^{-1}\BT^d_{\leq \mu}/\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq\mu}$ again, we obtain :
\[
\rho_{\mu*}\big(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}]\big)
= z_\lambda^* \,\rho'_{\mu*}[\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu}] = z_\lambda^*[\BR^d_{\leq\mu}],
\]
where the last equality follows from the fact that $\widetilde{\BR}^d_{\leq \mu}$ is mapped birationally to $\BR^d_{\leq\mu}$ under $\rho_\mu'$. This proves (2) $\subset$ (3) and also establishes that the class $\rho_{\mu*}\big(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap [\widetilde C_{\leq\mu}]\big)$ is independent of the choice of resolutions.

To see that the elements $\{\rho_{\mu*}\big(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\mu}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\mu}]\}_{\mu \leq \lambda}$ are linearly independent over $H^\bullet_T(\pt)$, it suffices to show that both the Gysin map $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BT^d_{\leq \lambda}) \to H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda})$ and the pushforward $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq \lambda}) \to H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BT^d_{\leq \lambda})$ are injective.

The former is clear because $\BT^d_{\leq \lambda}$ is a vector bundle over $C_{\leq \lambda}$. For the latter, note that there is an affine stratification:
\[
\BT^d_{\leq \lambda} = \bigsqcup_{\mu \leq \lambda} \BT^d_\mu,
\]
which is compatible with \eqref{Rdecom}. Therefore, it suffices to show that each pushforward $H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BR^d_\mu) \to H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\BT^d_\mu)$ is injective. This is equivalent to verifying that the element $e(\BS_\mu) = e(\BT^d_\mu / \BR^d_\mu) \in H^\bullet_T(C_\mu) \cong H^\bullet_T(\pt)$ is nonzero. However, by \Cref{Nweights}, the fibre of $\BS_\mu$ at $t^\mu$ is a $T$-representation with no zero weights, hence $e(\BS_\mu) \neq 0$.

Finally, it is clear that the subspace defined in (3) is contained in the subspace defined in (1).
\end{proof}

We introduce the notation
\begin{equation*}
e(\BS)\cap H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \coloneqq \sum_{\lambda\in \Lambda^+} \operatorname{Im}\left( e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cap H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}) \to H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \right),
\end{equation*}
and similarly for the versions of $G_\oh\rtimes \Cxh$, $T_\oh$, or $T_\oh\rtimes \Cxh$-equivariant Borel--Moore homology.

Now, the following theorem follows immediately.

\begin{thm}[=\Cref{Introthm3}]\label{AGN}
We have
\begin{equation*}
\sA_{G,\bN} = e(\BS_{G,\bN}) \cap H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) = \left( e(\BS_{G,\bN}) \cap H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \right)^W,
\end{equation*}
and similarly
\begin{equation*}
\sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar = e(\BS_{G,\bN}) \cap H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) = \left( e(\BS_{G,\bN}) \cap H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \right)^W.
\end{equation*}
\end{thm}

The following proposition follows from \Cref{independentResol} and \cite{BFN}. An independent proof is given in \Cref{AppendixStability}.

\begin{prop}\label{stableprod}
    The subspaces $e(\BS) \cap H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ and $e(\BS) \cap H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ are stable under the convolution product.
\end{prop}

We remark that localization
\begin{equation}\label{localizeindepofN}
\left(e(\BS_\bN)\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G)\right)_\loc\coloneq \mathrm{Frac} H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)\otimes_{H^\bullet_{T\times\Cxh}(\pt)} \left(e(\BS_\bN)\cap H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)\right)
\end{equation}
is independent of $\bN$ (\cite{BFN}).



\section{Twisting maps and twisted linearity}\label{twisting}

\subsection{Twisting maps}

Let $X$ be a smooth quasiprojective variety with a $G$-action. Consider the space $G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X$, where $G_\oh$ acts on $X$ with via the homomorphism $\ev_{t=0}:G_\oh\to G$. It is equipped with a left $G_\oh$-action via $h\cdot [g,x]=[hg,x]$. There is a $G_\oh$-equivariant morphism $G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X\to \Gr_G$ by sending $[g,x]\mapsto [g]\in \Gr_G$. We will consider the $G_\oh$-equivariant Borel--Moore homology of $G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X$, defined using finite-dimensional approximation. For each dominant coweight $\lambda\in\Lambda^+$, consider the $G_\oh$-invariant closed subset
\begin{equation}\label{ylambda}G_\ok^{\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X \subset G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X.\end{equation}
We define
\[H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X)\vcentcolon =\varinjlim_{\lambda\in\Lambda^+} H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(G_\ok^{\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X).\]

\subsubsection*{Convolution}

We have a correspondence similar to the convolution diagram \eqref{convolution1} (we will use the notations $p_{G,X}$ and $q_{G,X}$ if we need to specify $G$),
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
\Gr_G\times X& G_\ok\times X \arrow[l, "p_X"'] \arrow[r, "q_X"] & G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X
\end{tikzcd}
\]
which induces the following map,
\begin{equation}\label{twistinghom}
    (q_X^*)^{-1}\circ p_X^*:H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(X)\longrightarrow H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X).
\end{equation}
As in the discussion following \eqref{product1}, the above should be understood using finite-dimensional approximations. For $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$, let $i \gg j > 0$ be positive integers such that the actions of $K_i$ on $C_{\leq \lambda}$ and $G_\ok^{\leq \lambda}/K_j$ are trivial. Then there is a corresponding diagram (we use the same notations for the maps in the finite-dimensional approximation):
\begin{equation}\label{convolution3}
\begin{tikzcd}
C_{\leq \lambda}\times X& (G_\ok^{\leq \lambda}/K_j)\times X \arrow[l, "p_X"'] \arrow[r, "q_X"] & G_\ok^{\leq \lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The map~\eqref{twistinghom} should be understood as the direct limit over $\lambda\in \Lambda^+$ of
\[
(q_X^*)^{-1} \circ p_X^* : H^{G_i\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_j\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X) \to H^{G_i\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(G_\ok^{\leq \lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X),
\]
where $p_X^*$ and $q_X^*$ are homomorphisms induced by the diagram~\eqref{convolution3}. Explicitly:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The map $p_X^*$ is the pullback along $p_X$:
    \begin{align*}
    p_X^* : H^{G_i\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(C_{\leq \lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_j\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)  \longrightarrow H^{(G_i\times G_j)\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet\left((G_\ok^{\leq \lambda} / K_j) \times X\right).
    \end{align*}

    \item The map $q_X^*$ is the pullback along $q_X$, with respect to the inclusion $G_i\cong G_i\times\{e\} \subset G_i \times G_j$:
    \begin{align*}
    q_X^* :H^{G_i\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet\left(G_\ok^{\leq \lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\right)  \longrightarrow H^{(G_i\times G_j)\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet\left((G_\ok^{\leq \lambda} / K_j) \times X\right).
    \end{align*}
\end{itemize}

Note $q_X^*$ is an isomorphism, and that this construction is independent of $i$ and $j$. Similarly, there is also $T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant version of \eqref{twistinghom}, as in the following definition.

\begin{df}\label{Twistingdef}
The \emph{twisting map} is defined to be the composition $(q_X^*)^{-1} \circ p_X^*$, namely,
\begin{equation*}
\tw_G : H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)
\longrightarrow H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X),
\end{equation*}
as well as its $T_\oh$-equivariant counterpart:
\begin{equation*}
\tw_G : H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)
\longrightarrow H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X).
\end{equation*}
\end{df}

Note that we use the same notation $\tw_G$ to denote both homomorphisms. This abuse of notation should cause no ambiguity, as the first homomorphism agrees with the restriction of the second to the subspace of $W$-invariants.

The following proposition is obvious if one notice that both $p_X$ and $q_X$ are quotient maps for a free $G_\oh$-action.

\begin{prop}\label{remarktwisting}
There is a commutative diagram
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes \Cxh}(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes \Cxh}(X) \ar[r,"\tw_G"] \ar[d,"\cong"'] & H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes \Cxh}(G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X) \ar[d,"\cong"] \\
H_\bullet^{(G_\oh\times G_\oh\times G_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(G_\ok\times X) \ar[r] & H_\bullet^{(G_\oh\times G_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(G_\ok\times X)
\end{tikzcd},
\]
where the action of \( G_\oh\times G_\oh\times G_\oh \) on \( G_\ok\times X \) is defined as follows: the first two factors act on \( G_\ok \) from the left and right respectively, while the third factor acts on \( X \). The bottom horizontal map is induced by restricting the equivariance to the subgroup
\[
G_\oh\times \Delta_{G_\oh} \subset G_\oh\times G_\oh\times G_\oh.
\]
\end{prop}
In other words, one may regard $\tw_G$ as identifying the equivariance on $X$ with the equivariance on $\Gr_G$ coming from $\Gr_G=G_\ok/G_\oh$. One can also understand this as saying there is the following fibre product identity:
\[
\left[G_\oh \backslash \Gr_G\right] \times_{[\pt/G_\oh]} \left[G_\oh \backslash X\right] \cong \left[G_\oh \backslash (G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X)\right].
\]


\begin{prop}\label{twistingcompat}
    There is a commutative diagram.
    \begin{equation}\label{twistingcompatdiag}
    \begin{tikzcd}
        H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)\ar[r,"\tw_G"]& H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X)\\
        H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_T)\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)\ar[r]\ar[d]\ar[u,symbol=\subset]& H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(T_\ok\times_{T_\oh}X)\ar[d,equal]\ar[u]\\
        H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_T)\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)\ar[r,"\tw_T"]& H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(T_\ok\times_{T_\oh}X)
    \end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation}
    such that all the maps in the diagram are $W$-equivariant. 
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
In the proof below, we omit the finite-dimensional approximations for clarity, as the necessary constructions should now be clear.

Consider the diagram
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
    \Gr_G\times X& G_\ok\times X \arrow[l,"p_{G,X}"'] \arrow[r,"q_{G,X}"] & G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X\\
    \Gr_T\times X\ar[u,"\iota''"]& T_\ok G_\oh\times X \arrow[l,"p'_X"'] \arrow[r,"q'_X"] \ar[u,"\iota'"]& T_\ok G_\oh\times_{G_\oh}X\ar[u,"\iota"]\\
    \Gr_T\times X\ar[u,equal]& T_\ok\times X \arrow[l,"p_{T,X}"'] \arrow[r,"q_{T,X}"] \ar[u]& T_\ok\times_{T_\oh}X\ar[u,"\cong"]
\end{tikzcd}.
\]
Here, the second row is the base change of the first row via the inclusion $\Gr_T\to \Gr_G$. Since pushforward commutes with smooth pullback in a fibre diagram, the restriction of $\tw_G$ to $H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_T)\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(X)$ is equal to
\begin{equation*}
(q_{G,X}^*)^{-1} \circ p_{G,X}^* \circ \iota''_* = (q_{G,X}^*)^{-1} \circ \iota'_* \circ p^{\prime\,*}_X = \iota_* \circ (q^{\prime \,*}_X)^{-1} \circ p^{\prime\, *}_X.
\end{equation*}
This proves the commutativity of the upper square in \eqref{twistingcompatdiag}. For the lower square, note that we have the following commutative diagram:
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{tikzcd}
        H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times G_\oh\times G_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok G_\oh\times X) \ar[r]\ar[d] & H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times \Delta_{G_\oh})\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok G_\oh\times X) \ar[d]\\
        H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times T_\oh\times T_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok \times X) \ar[r] & H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times \Delta_{T_\oh})\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok \times X) 
    \end{tikzcd}.
\end{equation*}
Here, the horizontal maps correspond to restrictions of equivariant parameters. The left vertical map is the composition
\[
\begin{aligned}
H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times G_\oh\times G_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok G_\oh \times X)
&\to H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times G_\oh\times T_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok G_\oh \times X) \\
&\cong H_\bullet^{(T_\oh\times T_\oh\times T_\oh)\rtimes \Cxh}(T_\ok \times X),
\end{aligned}
\]
and similarly for the right vertical map. In view of \Cref{remarktwisting}, this proves the commutativity of the lower square in \eqref{twistingcompatdiag}.
\end{proof}

For the purpose of defining the shift operators (see \Cref{bbSN}), we require the following variant of the twisting map. Let $\lambda$ be a coweight, and define the fibre product
\begin{equation}\label{GKtilde}
\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} := G_\ok \times_{\Gr_G} \widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}.
\end{equation}
Using the same construction as above, we obtain a map
\begin{equation*}
\tw_{\leq\lambda}:H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(X)
\longrightarrow
H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet\big(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\big).
\end{equation*}
By abuse of notation, we also denote by the same symbol the following map, obtained from $\tw_{\leq \lambda}$ by applying Poincar\'e duality twice.
\begin{equation}\label{twistlambda}
\tw_{\leq\lambda}:
H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(X)
\longrightarrow
H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet\big(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\big).
\end{equation}
Similarly, one can define the $T_\oh$-equivariant version (and its Poincar\'e dual version):
\begin{equation*}
\tw_{\leq\lambda}:
H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(X)
\longrightarrow
H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet\big(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\big).
\end{equation*}

For later use, we record the following lemmas, whose proofs follow immediately from the compatibility of smooth pullbacks with pushforwards, and cap products.
\begin{lem}
The map $\tw_G$ is compatible with $\tw_{\leq\lambda}$ in the sense that the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{tikzcd}
H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(X) \arrow[r, "\tw_{\leq\lambda}"] \arrow[d] & H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet\big(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\big) \arrow[d] \\
H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(X) \arrow[r, "\tw_G"] & H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X)
    \end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
\end{lem}
\begin{lem}\label{twistingfunctorial}
Suppose we have another resolution $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'\to C_{\leq\lambda}$ which factors through $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}\to C_{\leq\lambda}$. Then the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{tikzcd}
H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}') \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(X) \arrow[r, "\tw_{\leq\lambda}'"]\arrow[d] & H_{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet\big(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\prime\,\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\big) \arrow[d]\\
H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(X) \arrow[r, "\tw_{\leq\lambda}"] & H_{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet\big(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X\big)
    \end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
Here, $\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\prime\leq\lambda}$ and $\tw_{\leq\lambda}'$ are defined analogously. The vertical maps are pushforwards.
\end{lem}

\begin{lem}\label{twistingeuler}
Suppose $\Gamma \in H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda})$ and $\alpha \in H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(X)$, we have
\begin{equation*}
\tw_{\leq\lambda}\left( e(\widetilde{S}_{\leq\lambda}) \cap \Gamma \otimes \alpha\right)
=
e(\widetilde{S}_{\leq\lambda}) \cup \tw_{\leq\lambda}(\Gamma \otimes \alpha),
\end{equation*}
where the bundle $\widetilde{S}_{\leq\lambda}$ on the right-hand side is understood as the pullback along the projection
\[
\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X \longrightarrow \widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}.
\]
\end{lem}

\subsection{Twisted linearity}
It is clear that $\tw_G(-\otimes-)$ and $\tw_T(-\otimes-)$ are $H_{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$-linear in the first argument. However, linearity in the second argument is more subtle, which we will explain now.

There is a morphism $u:[G_\oh\backslash\Gr_G]=[G_\oh\backslash G_\ok/G_\oh]\to [\pt/G_\oh]\to [\pt/G]$, where the second map is induced by $\ev_{t=0}:G_\oh\to G$. The pullback map on cohomology
\[ u^*:H_{G}^\bullet(\pt)\longrightarrow H_{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}^\bullet(\Gr_G)\]
defines a second $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-module structure on $H^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$. More specifically, the map $u^*$ sends a characteristic class $c_k(V)\in H_G^\bullet(\pt)$ to the cohomology class
\[u^*(c_k(V))=c_k(G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}V)\in H_{G_\oh\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\Gr_G).\]
Here, $V$ is a $G$-representation, and $G_\ok\times_{G_\oh} V$ is the associated vector bundle on $\Gr_G$. Then, the second module structure is given by
\begin{equation}\label{secondmodule}
\begin{aligned}
H_G^\bullet(\pt) \otimes H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) &\longrightarrow H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G), \\
P \otimes \Gamma &\longmapsto u^*(P) \cap \Gamma.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
By viewing $G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X$ as the quotient of $G_\ok \times X$ by the action of $G_\oh$, one similarly obtains the homomorphism
\begin{equation*}
u^* : H_G^\bullet(\pt) \longrightarrow H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X)
\end{equation*}
by considering the map $G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X\to [\pt/G_\oh]$.

Now we can explain the twisted linearity of $\tw_G$ in the second factor.

\begin{prop}\label{twistinglinear}
Let \( P \in H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(\pt) \). Then
\begin{equation*}
\tw_G(\Gamma \otimes (P \cap \alpha)) = \tw_G((u^* P \cap \Gamma) \otimes \alpha) = u^* P \cap \tw_G(\Gamma \otimes \alpha).
\end{equation*}
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
By \Cref{remarktwisting}, we may understand \( \tw_G \) as the map
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
H_\bullet^{(G_\oh \times G_\oh \times G_\oh) \rtimes \Cxh}(G_\ok \times X) \arrow[r] & H_\bullet^{(G_\oh \times G_\oh) \rtimes \Cxh}(G_\ok \times X)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
obtained by identifying the second and third copies of \( G_\oh \). The proposition follows by observing that: 1) the second $G_\oh$ corresponds to the second $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-module action on $H^{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ via $u^*$; 2) The third $G_\oh$ corresponds to the standard $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-module structure on $H^{G \times \Cxh}_\bullet(X)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{rem}
There is a parallel story for $H_T^\bullet(G/T)$ replacing $H^{G_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$, as explained in \cite{peterson}. Specifically, 
\[
H_T^\bullet(G/T)\cong H_T^\bullet(\pt)\otimes_{H_G^\bullet(\pt)} H_T^\bullet(\pt)
\] 
carries two distinct $H_T^\bullet(\pt)$-module structures. Moreover, for any $T$-space $X$, there is a natural isomorphism
\begin{equation*}
H_T^\bullet(G/T) \otimes_{H_T^\bullet(\pt)} H_T^\bullet(X) \cong H_T^\bullet(G \times_T X)
\end{equation*}
which mirrors the twisting map construction introduced in this section.
\end{rem}



\subsubsection*{Comparison with the previous construction}
Let $G=T$ be a torus. We now compare our version of the twisted map with that of Iritani \cite{Iritani}. For $\lambda \in \Lambda$, let $X^\lambda$ denote the fibre of $T_\ok \times_{T_\oh} X$ over the point $[t^\lambda] \in \Gr_T$. The variety $X^\lambda$ is isomorphic to $X$ as a $T$-variety, but carries the loop rotation action:
\begin{equation*}
z_\hbar \cdot x = \lambda(z_\hbar) \cdot x, \quad \text{for } z_\hbar \in \Cxh,\, x \in X^\lambda.
\end{equation*}

Consider the diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
  & T \times X \ar[ld, "a"'] \ar[rd, "b"] & \\
  X && X^\lambda
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
where the map $a$ is projection to the second factor, and $b$ is the action map of for the $T$-action on $X$ (not $X^\lambda$).

Taking $j = 1$ in~\eqref{convolution3}, one observes that the map
\begin{equation*}
\tw_T([t^\lambda] \otimes -) \colon H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X) \longrightarrow H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^\lambda)
\end{equation*}
is given by the composition
\begin{equation*}
H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)
\xrightarrow{a^*} H_{T \times T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(T \times X)
\xrightarrow{(b^*)^{-1}} H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^\lambda).
\end{equation*}
Here, $T\times T\times \Cxh$ acts on $T\times X$ by the formula
\begin{equation*}
    (z_1,z_2,z_\hbar)\cdot (z,x)=(\lambda(z_\hbar)z_1zz_2^{-1},z_2x).
\end{equation*}
To compute $a^*$ and $b^*$ explicitly, let $E = ET$ and $E' = E\Cxh$ be contractible spaces equipped with free right actions by $T$ and $\mathbb{C}^\times_\hbar$, respectively. Also let $E^\lambda = E$ as a $T$-space, but $\Cxh$ acts on $E^\lambda$ through $\lambda$. Then $a^*$ and $b^*$ correspond to pullbacks along $a'$ and $b'$ in the diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
  & (E^\lambda \times E \times E') \times_{T \times T \times \Cxh} (T \times X) 
    \ar[ld, "a'"'] 
    \ar[rd, "b'"] & \\
  (E \times E') \times_{T \times \Cxh} X 
  && (E^\lambda \times E') \times_{T \times \Cxh} X^\lambda
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
where
\begin{equation*}
a'(e_1, e_2, e', z, x) = (e_2, e', x), \qquad
b'(e_1, e_2, e', z, x) = (e_1, e', z \cdot x),
\end{equation*}
with $z\cdot x$ denoting the original $T$-action on $X$. 

The map $a'$ is a weak homotopy equivalence with inverse
\begin{align*}
c': (E\times E')\times_{T\times\Cxh} X &\longrightarrow (E^\lambda\times E\times E')\times_{T\times T\times\Cxh} (T\times X)\\
c'(e,e',x)&= (e,e,e',1,x).
\end{align*}
One can check that $c'$ is indeed well-defined, and that $b' \circ c' = \mathrm{id}$. 

One can summarize the above as follows. The identity map $(E \times E') \times X \to (E^\lambda \times E') \times X^\lambda$  is equivariant with respect to the group automorphism
\begin{equation}\label{twistedgrouphomo}
T \times \Cxh \longrightarrow T \times \Cxh, \quad (z, z_\hbar) \mapsto \big(z \lambda(z_\hbar)^{-1}, z_\hbar \big),
\end{equation}
and the induced map on $T \times \Cxh$-equivariant cohomology is naturally identified with $\tw_T([t^\lambda] \otimes -)$. We record this in the following proposition.
\begin{prop}\label{abeliantwlinear}
Let $\Phi_\lambda \colon H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X) \longrightarrow H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^\lambda)$
be the (twisted) homomorphism induced by the identity map $\operatorname{id} \colon X \to X^\lambda$, which is equivariant with respect to the group automorphism~\eqref{twistedgrouphomo}. 
Then $\Phi_\lambda$ agrees with the twisting map $\tw_T([t^\lambda] \otimes -)$. In particular, for $P(a,\hbar) \in H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\pt)$ and $\alpha \in H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)$, we have
\begin{equation*}
\tw_T([t^\lambda] \otimes P(a, \hbar) \alpha) = P(a + \lambda(\hbar), \hbar)\, \tw_T([t^\lambda] \otimes \alpha).
\end{equation*}
\end{prop}

\begin{rem}
    $\Phi_\lambda$ is the same as the twisted homorphism defined in \cite[Section 3.1]{Iritani}. 
\end{rem}

\section{Shift operators I: preparation}\label{preparation}

\subsection{Quantum cohomology}\label{qh}

\subsubsection*{Assumptions on the space}
Let $X$ be a smooth, semiprojective complex variety with an algebraic $G$-action. By \textit{semiprojective}, we mean that $X$ is quasiprojective and that the affinization map
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{aff}: X \longrightarrow X^{\mathrm{aff}} \vcentcolon= \spec H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_X)
\end{equation*}
is projective. We assume that $X$ carries an algebraic $G$-action commuting with an auxiliary $\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}$-action satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item all $\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}$-weights on $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_X)$ are non-positive; and
  \item the $\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}$-invariants satisfy $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_X)^{\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}} = \C$.
\end{enumerate}

These conditions imply in particular that $X^{\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}}$ is compact. We refer to $\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}$ as the group of \emph{dilations} or the \emph{conical action}, not to be confused with the group of \emph{loop rotations}, $\C^\times_\hbar$, which acts trivially on $X$.


These assumptions ensure that $X$ has nice cohomological properties, including \Cref{equivformal} below; see \cite{Iritani}. Note that in \cite{Iritani}, it is assumed that the conical group $\Cxd$ is a subgroup of $G$, which is required for defining shift operators in their setting. However, we do not need this assumption in the Coulomb branch setting, as will be explained in \Cref{sectionshiftoperators}.

\begin{prop}\label{equivformal}
  The $G$-action on $X$ is equivariantly formal.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
  See \cite[Proposition 2.1]{Iritani}. In \textit{loc.~cit.}, only abelian $G$ were considered, but the same proof applies to any reductive group $G$.
\end{proof}


\subsubsection*{Equivariant quantum cohomology}

We fix a graded $\C$-basis $\{a_i\}_{i\in I}$ of $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$ and a graded $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-basis $\{\phi_j\}_{j=0}^N$ of $H_G^\bullet(X)$. We denote $\phi_{i,j}\coloneq a_i\phi_j$. Let $\{\tau^{i,j}\}$ be coordinates on $H_G^\bullet(X)$ dual to the basis $\{\phi_{i,j}\}$, and set $\tau = \sum_{i,j} \tau^{i,j} \phi_{i,j}$. We declare the degrees of the coordinates $\tau^{i,j}$ to be
\[
\deg \tau^{i,j} = 2 - \deg (\phi_{i,j}).
\]
We write $\C[[\tau]] = \C[[\tau^{i,j}]]$, where the odd variables (i.e., $\tau^{i,j}$ for $\deg \phi_{i,j}$ odd) anti-commute. In other words, this is the tensor product of the formal power series ring in the even variables with the exterior algebra in the odd variables.
\begin{rem}{\ }
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If $K \subset G$ is a reductive subgroup, then the restriction of $\{\phi_j\}_{j=0}^N$ gives a graded $H_K^\bullet(\pt)$-basis of $H_K^\bullet(X)$. We always assume that such a compatible choice of basis is made.

    \item We may use the notation $\tau_G$ to indicate the dependence on $G$. In this paper, we always assume $\tau = \tau_G$ unless otherwise specified.
\end{enumerate}
\end{rem}

\begin{rem}
    The introduction of the variables $\tau^{i,j}$ is in order to define the quantum connection $\nabla_{\hbar\partial_\hbar}$ \eqref{qconnformula} and shift operators \eqref{bbSNformula}. More specifically, this is due to the grading operator $\mu_X$ \eqref{gradingop} and the assignment $\tau\mapsto \hat\tau$ \eqref{tauhatmap} not being $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-linear. For the sole purpose of defining equivariant quantum cohomology, it is possible to take $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-valued coordinates instead of the $\C$-valued coordinates $\tau^{i,j}$.
\end{rem}

Let $K \subset G$ be a reductive subgroup, we write
\begin{equation*}
\C[q_K] = \C[H_2^{\text{ord},K}(X; \Z)]
\end{equation*}
for the group algebra of the abelian group $H_2^{\text{ord},K}(X; \Z)$. The ring $\C[q_K]$ is equipped with a grading determined by
\begin{equation*}
\deg q^\beta = 2\langle c_1^K(X), \beta \rangle.
\end{equation*}

Let $\Eff(X) \subset H_2^{\text{ord}}(X; \Z)$ be the subset of effective curve classes. We denote $\iota_*: H_2^{\text{ord}}(X; \Z) \to H_2^{\text{ord},K}(X; \Z)$ the natural map. We define $H_G^\bullet(X)[[q_K, \tau]]$ to be the graded completion of $H_G^\bullet(X)[[\tau]] {\otimes}_\C \C[q_K]$ along the direction $\Eff(X)$\footnote{One can see that the product on $\C[[q_G]]$ is well-defined as follows. Choose a closed embedding $X \hookrightarrow \PP^m \times \C^n$. It suffices to verify that the moduli stack of stable maps to $X$ of degree less than or equal to a fixed number $E$ has only finitely many components. This follows from the fact that this moduli stack is a closed substack of the corresponding moduli stack of stable maps to $\PP^m \times \C^n$, which has quasiprojective coarse moduli space. We thank Hiroshi Iritani for explaining this argument to us.}. Concretely, an element of $H_G^\bullet(X)[[q_K,\tau]]$ is a formal sum
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{\beta, m} q^\beta c_{\beta, m} \,  \prod_{\substack{i\in I \\ 0\leq j\leq N}} (\tau^{i,j})^{m_{i,j}}
\end{equation*}
where $\beta$ ranges over $H_2^{\mathrm{ord},K}(X; \Z)$, $m = \{m_{i,j}\}$ runs over multi-indices with finite support on $I\times \{0,1,\dots,N\}$, and the product is taken in some fixed order on this index set. The sum is required to satisfy the following:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Each $c_{\beta, m}$ lies in $H_G^\bullet(X)$.
    \item There exists a finite subset $S \subset H_2^{\text{ord},K}(X; \Z)$ such that $c_{\beta, m} = 0$ unless $\beta \in S + \iota_*(\Eff(X))$.
    \item There exists a finite subset $R \subset \Z$ such that $(c_{\beta, m})_d = 0$ for $d + \sum m_{i,j} \deg \tau^{i,j} + \deg q^\beta \not\in R$, where $(c_{\beta, m})_d$ denotes the degree $d$ component of $c_{\beta, m}$.
\end{enumerate}


\begin{df}\label{equivariantqh}
The \emph{$G$-equivariant quantum cohomology} of $X$ with $K$-equivariant Novikov variables is the ring
\[
  QH^\bullet_G(X)[[q_K,\tau]],
\]
whose underlying vector space is $H^\bullet_G(X)[[q_K,\tau]]$. Its ring structure is defined by the \emph{big quantum product} $\star_{\tau}$, where
\begin{equation*}
  \gamma \star_{\tau} \gamma'
  = \sum_{\beta \in \Eff(X)} \sum_{n=0}^\infty
    \frac{q^{\iota_* \beta}}{n!} \,
    \mathrm{PD} \, \ev_{3*} \Bigl( \ev_1^* (\gamma) \, \ev_2^* (\gamma') \prod_{\ell=4}^{n+3} \ev_\ell^* (\tau) \cap [\overline{M}_{0,n+3}(X,\beta)]^\vir \Bigr)
\end{equation*}
for $\gamma, \gamma' \in H^\bullet_G(X)$. Here, $\overline{M}_{0,n+3}(X, \beta)$ is the moduli stack of genus-zero stable maps to $X$ with $n+3$ marked points and curve class $\beta$, and $[\overline{M}_{0,n+3}(X, \beta)]^\vir$ denotes its virtual fundamental class. Note that $\ev_3$ is proper, thanks to $X$ being semiprojective. The product $\star_{\tau}$ on a general element of $H^\bullet_G(X)[[q_K, \tau]]$ is then defined termwise on its power series expansion.
\end{df}
From now on, we will simply write $q^{\iota_*\beta}$ as $q^\beta$ whenever no confusion is likely to arise.
It is clear that if $K_1\subset K_2\subset G$ are reductive subgroups of $G$, then there is a natural ring homomorphism
\begin{equation}\label{qhbasechange}
  QH^\bullet_{G}(X)[[q_{K_1},\tau]]
  \,\longrightarrow\,
  QH^\bullet_{G}(X)[[q_{K_2},\tau]].
\end{equation}


\subsubsection*{Quantum connection}

We consider the trivial $\C^\times_\hbar$-action on $X$. We follow the notations in \cite[Section 2.1]{IritaniFourier}.
\begin{df}[Quantum connection]\label{def:quantum_connection}
The \emph{equivariant quantum connections} are the operators 
$$\nabla_{\tau^{i,j}}, \nabla_{\hbar\partial_\hbar}, \nabla_{D}:H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_K, \tau]] \to H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_K, \tau]] [\hbar^{-1}],$$
defined as
\begin{align}\label{qconnformula}
\nabla_{\tau^{i,j}} &= \partial_{\tau^{i,j}} + \hbar^{-1}(\phi_{i,j} \star_{\tau}), \nonumber\\
\nabla_{\hbar\partial_\hbar} & = \hbar\partial_\hbar - \hbar^{-1}(E\star_\tau) +\mu_X , \\
\nabla_{Dq\partial_q}  &= Dq\partial_q + \hbar^{-1}(D\star), \nonumber
\end{align}
where $D\in H^2_G(X)$ and $Dq\partial_q$ is the derivation on $\C[[q_K]]$ with $Dq\partial_q q^\beta=\langle D,\beta\rangle q^\beta$, while $E_X$ is the \emph{Euler vector field} and $\mu_X$ is the \emph{grading operator}, defined respectively by the formulas
\[E_X=c_1^G(X)+\sum_{i,j}\frac{\deg(\tau^{i,j})}{2} \tau^{i,j}\phi_{i,j}\]
and
\begin{equation}\label{gradingop}
\mu_X(\phi_{i,j})=\frac 12\left(\deg(\phi_{i,j})-\dim X\right) \phi_{i,j}.
\end{equation}
\end{df}

\begin{df}[Fundamental solution]\label{def:fundamental_solution}
The \emph{fundamental solution} to the quantum differential equation is the operator
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{M}_X : H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G, \tau]][[\hbar^{-1}]] \to H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G, \tau]][[\hbar^{-1}]]
\end{equation*}
defined by
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{M}_X(\gamma) 
= \gamma+\sum_{0\neq \beta \in \Eff(X)}
    \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{q^\beta}{n!} \,
    \mathrm{PD} \circ \ev_{2*} \left( \frac{\ev_1^* (\gamma)}{\hbar - \psi_1}
    \prod_{\ell=3}^{n+2} \ev_\ell^* (\tau) \cap [\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(X, \beta)]^{\vir} \right),
\end{equation*}
where $\psi_1$ is the equivariant first Chern class of the universal cotangent line bundle at the first marked point.
\end{df}

The following proposition is well-known (see \cite{GiventalequivGW,Pandharipande1998,CrepantToricCompleteInt,IritaniFourier}).
\begin{prop}
The operator $\mathbb{M}_X$ is invertible and satisfies the identities 
\begin{align}\label{qconnintertwine}
\nabla_{\tau^{i,j}} \circ \mathbb{M}_X &= \mathbb{M}_X \circ \partial_{\tau^{i,j}}, \nonumber\\
\nabla_{\hbar\partial_\hbar} \circ \mathbb{M}_X &= \mathbb{M}_X\circ \left(\hbar\partial_\hbar - \hbar^{-1}(c_1^G(X)\cup) + \mu_X\right),\\
\nabla_{Dq\partial_q}\circ \mathbb{M}_X &= \mathbb{M}_X\circ \left(Dq\partial_q+\hbar^{-1}(D\cup)\right). \nonumber
\end{align}
\end{prop}

\subsection{Universal \texorpdfstring{$G$}{G}-torsor}\label{univbun}

We fix the standard affine chart $\C = \spec \C[t] \subset \PP^1$ and take the base point $0 \in \C \subset \PP^1$. By the Beauville--Laszlo theorem \cite{BL} (see also \cite{Zhu}), the affine Grassmannian $\Gr_G$ represents the functor on the category of $\C$-scheme.
\begin{equation*}
Z \longmapsto 
\left\{
(\mathcal{P}, \varphi) \,\middle|\,
\begin{array}{l}
\mathcal{P} \text{ is a } G\text{-torsor over } \PP^1_Z, \\
\varphi_\infty : \mathcal{P}|_{(\PP^1 \setminus \{0\})_Z} \xrightarrow{\sim} (\PP^1 \setminus \{0\})_Z \times G \text{ is a trivialization}
\end{array}
\right\} \big/ \cong.
\end{equation*}
In particular, there exists a universal $G$-torsor $\sE \to \Gr_G \times \PP^1$. If $p\in \Gr_G$, we denote $\sE_p$ the restriction of $\sE$ to $p\times\PP^1$. 

We now make the correspondence between a map $f : Z \to \Gr_G$ and the pair $(\mathcal{P}, \varphi_\infty)$ more explicit. Suppose first that $f$ factors through a map $\widetilde{f} : Z \to G_\ok$. Then $\mathcal{P}$ is the unique (up to isomorphism) $G$-torsor over $\PP^1_R$ with local trivializations 
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_\infty : \mathcal{P}|_{Z \times (\PP^1 \setminus \{0\})} \xrightarrow{\sim} (Z \times (\PP^1 \setminus \{0\})) \times G,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_0 : \mathcal{P}|_{Z \times \spec \oh} \xrightarrow{\sim} (Z \times \spec \oh) \times G,
\end{equation*}
such that the induced automorphism
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_\infty|_{Z \times \spec \ok} \circ (\varphi_0|_{Z \times \spec \ok})^{-1} : (Z \times \spec \ok) \times G \to (Z \times \spec \ok) \times G
\end{equation*}
is given by left multiplication by the map
\begin{equation*}
(Z \times \spec \ok) \times G \to Z \times \spec \ok \xrightarrow{\widetilde{f}} G.
\end{equation*}
The existence of such a torsor $\mathcal{P}$ is the content of the Beauville--Laszlo theorem. In general, there exists an fppf cover $\bigsqcup Z_i \to Z$ such that the pullback of $f$ to each $Z_i$ factors through $G_\ok$. One can then construct $\mathcal{P}$ and $\varphi_\infty$ by gluing the corresponding $G$-torsors and local trivializations over the cover, using fppf descent.

Under the above correspondence, the subgroup $G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \subset G_\ok$ acts on $\Gr_G$ by modifying the section $\varphi$ over $\PP^1 \setminus \{0\}$, while loop rotation acts by scaling the coordinate on $\PP^1$. More explicitly, for $z \in \C^\times_\hbar$, let $m_z : \PP^1 \to \PP^1$ be the morphism given by $t \mapsto z^{-1}t$. There is an action of $G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \C^\times_\hbar$ on $\Gr_G$ given by
\begin{equation*}
g=(g_1,z) \cdot (\mathcal{P}, \varphi) = \left( m_{z}^* \mathcal{P},\ g \cdot m_{z}^* \varphi \right),
\end{equation*}
where $g_1$ is regarded as a morphism $\spec \C[t^{-1}] \to G$.

The action of $G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \C^\times_\hbar$ lifts to the universal torsor $\sE$, in the sense of the next lemma, which follows from the functorial description of $\Gr_G$.

\begin{lem}
Suppose $f : Z \to \Gr_G$ is a morphism from a scheme $Z$ corresponding to the pair $(\mathcal{P}, \varphi_\infty)$, and let $g = (g_1, z) \in G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \C^\times_\hbar$. If $(\mathcal{P}', \varphi'_\infty)$ corresponds to the map $g \cdot f : Z \to \Gr_G$, then there exists a $G$-equivariant isomorphism
\begin{equation*}
g_f : \mathcal{P} \xrightarrow{\sim} m_z^*\mathcal{P}'
\end{equation*}
such that $m_z^*\varphi'_\infty \circ g_f = g_1\cdot \varphi_\infty$.
\end{lem}

In particular, if $H \subset G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \C^\times_\hbar$ and $f : Z \to \Gr_G$ is an $H$-equivariant morphism corresponding to the pair $(\mathcal{P}, \varphi_\infty)$, then there is an induced $H$-action on $\mathcal{P}$ such that the projection $\mathcal{P} \to Z \times \PP^1$ is $H$-equivariant (where $\C^\times_\hbar$ acts on $\PP^1$ by scalar multiplication).

Strictly speaking, we have defined the action on $\sE$ only at the level of $\C$-points of $G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \C^\times_\hbar$. In \Cref{Gtorsorappendix}, we will describe the action of $G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \C^\times_\hbar$ as a group scheme by considering its $R$-points, and in particular show that the actions defined above are algebraic.

\begin{exmp}\label{CompareIritani1}
Suppose $G = T = \C^\times$ and $\lambda = 1$. Then
\begin{equation*}
\sE_t \coloneq \sE|_{t} \cong \oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times \cong \C^2 \setminus \{0\}.
\end{equation*}
Let $u, v$ be coordinates on $\C^2$, so that the projection $\C^2 \setminus \{0\} \to \PP^1$ is given by $t = v/u$. Indeed, there are trivializations
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_0\colon \oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times|_{\spec \C[t]} \xrightarrow{\sim} \spec \C[t] \times \C^\times,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_\infty\colon \oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times|_{\spec \C[t^{-1}]} \xrightarrow{\sim} \spec \C[t^{-1}] \times \C^\times,
\end{equation*}
given by
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_0(u, v) = \left( \frac{v}{u}, u \right), \qquad
\varphi_\infty(u, v) = \left( \frac{u}{v}, v \right).
\end{equation*}
It is clear that the transition function $\varphi_\infty \circ \varphi_0^{-1}$ corresponds to left multiplication by $t = v/u$. The subgroup\footnote{$\C^\times$ and $\C^\times_{\C[t^{-1}]}$ have the same underlying $\C$-points} $\C^\times \times \Cxh \subset \C^\times_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \Cxh$ acts on $\oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times$ by $(g, z_\hbar) \cdot (u, v) = (z_\hbar gu, g v)$.

More generally, if $G = T$ is abelian and $\lambda \in \Lambda$ is arbitrary, then
\begin{equation*}
\sE_{t^\lambda} \cong \oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times \times_{\C^\times} T,
\end{equation*}
where $\C^\times$ acts on $T$ via $\lambda$, and $T \times \Cxh$ acts on $\sE_{t^\lambda}$ via $(g, z_\hbar) \cdot (u, v, g') = (z_\hbar u, v, gg')$.
\qed
\end{exmp}

\subsection{Seidel spaces}\label{Seidelspace}

For each $\lambda \in \Lambda$, we fix an $\BI$-equivariant resolution of singularities $\rho_\lambda \colon \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \to C_{\leq \lambda}$ satisfying the conditions in \Cref{existresol}. Let $\sE_{\leq \lambda}$ be the principal $G$-bundle on $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \times \PP^1$ induced by the morphism
\begin{equation*}
\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \xrightarrow{\rho_\lambda} C_{\leq \lambda} \subset \Gr_G.
\end{equation*}
If $p \in \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$, we write $\sE_p$ for the restriction of $\sE_{\leq \lambda}$ to $p \times \PP^1$. In particular, there is a canonical isomorphism $\sE_p \cong \sE_{\rho_\lambda(p)}$.

If $X$ is a $G$-variety, we call
\begin{equation*}
\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X) := \sE_{\leq \lambda} \times_G X
\end{equation*}
the \emph{Seidel space associated to $X$ and $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$}.

By construction, there is a $T \times \C^\times_\hbar$-action on $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$ such that the projection
\begin{equation*}
\pi : \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X) \to \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \times \PP^1
\end{equation*}
is equivariant, where $\C^\times_\hbar$ acts on $\PP^1$ by scalar multiplication. Moreover, if $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$, this action can be upgraded to a $G \times \C^\times_\hbar$-action.
\begin{exmp}\label{CompareIritani2}
The bundle $\sE_{t^\lambda}(X)$ is isomorphic to $\oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times \times_{\Cx} X$, where $\C^\times$ acts on $X$ via the cocharacter $\lambda\colon \C^\times \to T$. The action of $T \times \Cxh$ on $\sE_{t^\lambda}(X)$ is given by (cf.~\Cref{CompareIritani1})
\begin{equation*}
(g, z_\hbar) \cdot (u, v, x) = (z_\hbar u, v, gx).
\end{equation*}
This space is $T \times \Cxh$-equivariantly isomorphic to the Seidel space $E_\lambda$ defined in Section~3.2 of \cite{Iritani}, but with the roles of $0$ and $\infty$ swapped.\qed
\end{exmp}

\subsubsection*{Section classes}

An effective curve class $\beta \in H_2^{\text{ord}}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X); \Z)$ is called a \emph{section class} if $\pi_* \beta = [\pt \times \PP^1]$. Denote by $\Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\mathrm{sec}}$ the subset of section classes.

The projection $\sE_{\leq \lambda}\times X \to X$ is $G$-equivariant, and hence induces a pushforward map in equivariant homology:
\begin{equation*}
H_2^{\text{ord}}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X); \Z)\,\longrightarrow H_2^{\text{ord},G}(X; \Z).
\end{equation*}
For any $\beta \in H_2^{\text{ord}}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X); \Z)$, we denote by $\overline{\beta}\in H^{\text{ord},G}_2(X,\Z)$ its image under this map.

Let $T^{\mathrm{vert}} \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X) \coloneqq \ker(d\pi) \cong \sE_{\leq \lambda}(TX)$ be the relative tangent bundle of $\pi$. The following lemma is immediate.

\begin{lem}\label{degree}
$\deg(q^{\overline{\beta}}) = 2\, c_1(T^{\mathrm{vert}} \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)) \cdot \beta$.
\end{lem}

\subsubsection*{Fibres at $0$ and $\infty$}
There are $G_{\C[t^{-1}]}\rtimes\Cxh$-equivariant isomorphisms
\begin{equation}\label{fibres}
\begin{aligned}
    \sE(X)|_{\Gr_G \times \{0\}} &\cong G_\ok \times_{G_\oh} X, \\
    \sE(X)|_{\Gr_G \times \{\infty\}} &\cong \Gr_G \times X.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
These are straightforward consequences of the construction of $\sE$, see \eqref{fibresofsE} in \Cref{Gtorsorappendix}. We denote
\begin{equation*}
\iota_0:\mathcal X_{\leq \lambda, 0} \coloneqq \pi^{-1}(\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \times \{0\}) \hookrightarrow \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\iota_\infty:\mathcal{X}_{\leq \lambda, \infty} \coloneqq \pi^{-1}(\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \times \{\infty\}) \hookrightarrow \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X).
\end{equation*}
Recall that $\widetilde G_\ok^{\leq\lambda}$ denotes $G_\ok\times_{\Gr_G} \widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}$ (see \eqref{GKtilde}). The following lemma follows immediately from \Cref{fibres}.
\begin{lem}\label{zerofibre}
There are $T \times \C^\times_{\hbar}$-equivariant isomorphisms
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{X}_{\leq \lambda, 0} &\cong \widetilde G_\ok^{\leq\lambda} \times_{G_\oh} X, \\
    \mathcal{X}_{\leq \lambda, \infty} &\cong \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \times X.
\end{align*}
Moreover, these isomorphisms are $G \times \C^\times_{\hbar}$-equivariant if $\lambda \in \Lambda^+$.
\end{lem}
In particular, $\rho_{X,\lambda} \colon \mathcal{X}_{\leq \lambda, 0} \to G_\ok^{\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X$ is a resolution of singularities.

\begin{df}\label{tauhat}
We define a map 
\begin{equation}\label{tauhatmap}
H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X) \to H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)), \qquad \tau \mapsto \hat{\tau},
\end{equation}
as the composition of the following maps:
\begin{equation*}
H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(X) 
\xrightarrow{\pr_X^*} H_{(T_\oh \times G_\oh \times G_\oh) \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(\sE_{\leq \lambda} \times X) \to H_{(T_\oh \times \Delta_{G_\oh}) \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(\sE_{\leq \lambda} \times X) \xrightarrow{\sim} H_{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)),
\end{equation*}
where we identify $H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)$ with $H_{G_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}^\bullet(X)$, and similarly for the other terms. 
\end{df}
The following lemma is an immediate consequence of \Cref{tauhat} and the considerations in \Cref{remarktwisting}.
\begin{lem}\label{tauhat=twisting}
Let 
\begin{equation*}
\widetilde\tau = 1 \otimes \tau \in H^\bullet_{(T \times G_\oh \times G) \rtimes \Cxh}(\widetilde G_\ok^{\leq \lambda} \times X) \cong H^\bullet_{T \times \Cxh}(\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^\bullet_{G \times \Cxh}(X).
\end{equation*}
Then the restriction of $\hat\tau$ to $\cX_{\leq \lambda, 0}$ is equal to $\tw_{\leq\lambda}(\widetilde{\tau})$, and the restriction of $\hat\tau$ to $\cX_{\leq \lambda, 0}$ is equal to the image of $\widetilde{\tau}$ under the restriction of the equivariant parameter
\begin{equation*}
H^\bullet_{(T \times G_\oh \times G) \rtimes \Cxh}(\widetilde G_\ok^{\leq \lambda} \times X)
\longrightarrow
H^\bullet_{(T \times G_\oh \times 1) \rtimes \Cxh}(\widetilde G_\ok^{\leq \lambda} \times X)
\cong
H^\bullet_{T \times \Cxh}(\widetilde C_{\leq \lambda} \times X).
\end{equation*}
\end{lem}
\begin{rem}
    It follows from \Cref{tauhat=twisting} that when $G = T$ is abelian, the class $\hat{\tau}$ agrees with~\cite[Notation 3.8]{Iritani}, except that the roles of the zero and infinity fibres are reversed (cf.~\Cref{CompareIritani2}).
\end{rem}

\subsubsection*{Moduli spaces and virtual fundamental classes}
Let $\beta \in \Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\sec}$. Recall that $\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)$ denotes the moduli stack of genus-zero stable maps to $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$ with $n+2$ marked points and curve class $\beta$. A typical object in this moduli stack is a stable map
\begin{equation*}
\sigma \colon (\Sigma, y_0, y_\infty,y_1,\dots,y_n) \to \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X),
\end{equation*}
where $\Sigma$ is a genus-zero nodal curve and $y_0, y_\infty,y_1\dots,y_n \in \Sigma$ are the marked points.

Since $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$ is smooth, the moduli stack admits a virtual fundamental class (\cite{Intrinsic}),
\begin{equation*}
[\,\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)]^{\vir}\in H^{T\times\Cx_\hbar}_{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)).
\end{equation*}

\begin{df}\label{modulispace}
Let $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n \subset \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)$ denote the substack consisting of those stable maps $\sigma$ such that $\sigma(y_0)$ lies over $0 \in \PP^1$ and $\sigma(y_\infty)$ lies over $\infty \in \PP^1$. In other words, we have the fibre diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{PBM}
\begin{tikzcd}
\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n \ar[r, "j_\cM"] \ar[d] &
\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta) \ar[d, "(\pr_{\PP^1} \circ \EV_0) \times (\pr_{\PP^1} \circ \EV_\infty)"] \\
\{(0, \infty)\} \ar[r] & \PP^1 \times \PP^1
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Here, $\EV_0$ and $\EV_\infty$ denote the \emph{evaluation maps at the marked points $y_0$ and $y_\infty$}, respectively. We also denote
\[
\cM_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n:=\bigsqcup_{\beta\in\Eff(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X))^{\sec}} \cM_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n.
\]
\end{df}
In particular, the evaluation maps restrict to
\[
\ev_0: \cM_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n \longrightarrow \mathcal X_{\leq\lambda,0},\qquad
\ev_\infty: \cM_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n \longrightarrow \mathcal X_{\leq\lambda,\infty}.
\]
In order to distinguish between the evaluation maps on $\mathcal{M}$ and $\overline M$, we use the different symbols $\ev$ and $\EV$, respectively.

We define the virtual fundamental class of $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n$ to be
\begin{equation}\label{virM}
[\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^{\vir} := j_\cM^!\left[\, \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta) \right]^{\vir},
\end{equation}
where $j_\cM^!$ is the refined Gysin pullback
\begin{equation*}
j_\cM^! \colon H^{T\times\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet\big(\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)\big) \to H^{T\times\Cx_\hbar}_{\bullet-4}(\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n)
\end{equation*}
defined via the fibre diagram \eqref{PBM} (see [8.3.21] in \cite{ChrissGinzburg} or part 3(ii)(b) of \cite{BFN}). The degree of the virtual fundamental class equals twice the virtual dimension of $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n$, which is computed as follows (cf.\ \cite{Fulton-Pandharipande,Cox-Katz}):
\begin{align}
\mathrm{vdim}\big(\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n\big)
&= \dim \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X) + c_1\big(T \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)\big) \cdot \beta + n - 3 \nonumber \\
&= \dim X + \dim C_\lambda + c_1\big(T^{\mathrm{vert}} \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)\big) \cdot \beta + n \nonumber \\
&= \dim X + \dim C_\lambda + c_1^G(X) \cdot \overline\beta + n. \label{vdim}
\end{align}
If $p$ is a point in $\Gr_G$ or in $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$, the substack $\cM_p(X, \beta)_n \subset \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_p(X), \beta)$ is defined similarly. We write $\pr_{\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}}:\cM_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n\to {\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}}$ for the projection.


\section{Shift operators II: definition}\label{sectionshiftoperators}
We assume that $X$ satisfies the conditions in \Cref{qh}, that $\bN$ is a representation of $G$, and that there exists an equivariant proper morphism
\begin{equation*}
f \colon X \to \bN.
\end{equation*}
Our goal is to construct a homomorphism
\begin{equation*}
\bbS_{G,\bN,X} \colon \sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H^\bullet_{G \times \C^\times_\hbar}(X)[[q_G,\tau]]
\;\longrightarrow\;
H^\bullet_{G \times \C^\times_\hbar}(X)[[q_G,\tau]]
\end{equation*}
that endows $H^\bullet_{G \times \C^\times_\hbar}(X)[[q_G,\tau]]$ with the structure of an $\sA^\hbar_{G,\bN}$-module.



\subsection{The tautological section and its zero locus}
Throughout this section, $\beta$ will always denote a section class.
\subsubsection*{A lemma on $\sE_p(\bN)$} Recall in \Cref{univbun} that we have a trivialization of $\sE$ over $\Gr_G\times \{\infty\}$. Let $p \in \Gr_G$, there is thus an induced local trivialization of the vector bundle $\sE_p(\bN)$ over $\PP^1$
\begin{equation*}
    \varphi_\infty(\bN): \sE_p(\bN)|_{\spec \C[t^{-1}]} \xrightarrow{\sim} \spec \C[t^{-1}] \times \bN
\end{equation*}
over $\spec \C[t^{-1}]$. We write $\varphi_\infty(\bN)$ simply as $\varphi_\infty$ when no confusion arises. Suppose $s$ is a section of the vector bundle $\sE_p(\bN)$, which corresponds to a morphism $s_\infty: \spec \C[t^{-1}] \to \bN$ under $\varphi_\infty$. We regard $s_\infty$ as an element of $\bN_{\C[t^{-1}]}$. Recall the bundle $\BT=G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}\bN_\oh$.
\begin{lem}\label{inTp}
    $s_\infty \in \BT_p$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
    Let $\widetilde{p}$ be a lift of $p$ in $G_\ok$, and recall that $\BT_p = \widetilde{p} \,\bN_\oh\subset\bN_{\ok}$. There is a trivialization of $\sE_p(\bN)$ over $\spec \oh$:
    \begin{equation*}
        \varphi_0 : \sE_p(\bN)|_{\spec \oh} \xrightarrow{\sim} \spec \oh \times \bN
    \end{equation*}
    such that the transition function $\varphi_\infty \circ \varphi_0^{-1} : \bN_\ok \to \bN_\ok$ is given by the action of $\widetilde{p} \in G_\ok$. Let $s_0: \spec \oh \to \bN$ be the morphism induced by $s$ under $\varphi_0$, and regard it as an element of $\bN_\oh$. Then we have $s_\infty = \widetilde{p} s_0 \in \BT_p$, as claimed.
\end{proof}

\subsubsection*{The tautological section}
Let $\lambda \in \Lambda$, and let $\rho_\lambda \colon \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \to C_{\leq \lambda}$ be a resolution as in \Cref{existresol}. We will construct a section of $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}$ over $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$\footnote{That is, a morphism $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n \to \widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}$ of spaces over $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$.}.

Let $\sigma : (\Sigma, y_0, y_\infty,y_1,\dots,y_n) \to \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$ be a stable map representing a point in $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$. By the definition of the section class, there exists a point $p_\sigma \in \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$ and a unique irreducible component $\Sigma_0$ of $\Sigma$ such that the composition
\begin{equation*}
    \Sigma \xrightarrow{\sigma} \sE_p(X) \to \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \times \PP^1
\end{equation*}
restricts to an isomorphism $r : \Sigma_0 \xrightarrow{\sim} p_\sigma \times \PP^1$. Composing $\sigma \circ r^{-1}$ with the projection $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n \to \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\bN)_n$ gives a section $\overline{\sigma}$ of the vector bundle $\sE_p(\bN) \coloneqq \sE_{\rho_\lambda(p)}(\bN)$. 

By \Cref{inTp}, the image $\overline{\sigma}_\infty$ lies in $\BT_{p_\sigma} \coloneqq \BT_{\rho_\lambda(p_\sigma)}$.

\begin{df}\label{canonicalsection}
    Let $\can_{\leq\lambda} (X)$ be the section of $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}$ over $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$ given by the assignment
    \begin{equation*}
        \sigma \longmapsto [\overline{\sigma}_\infty],
    \end{equation*}
    where $\sigma \in \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$, and $[\overline{\sigma}_\infty]$ denotes the image of $\overline{\sigma}_\infty$ under the projection $\BT_{p_\sigma} \to (\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda})_{p_\sigma}$. 

    Moreover, if $\beta$ is a section class of $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$, we denote by $\can_{\leq\lambda}(X, \beta)$ the restriction of $\can_{\leq\lambda}(X)$ to $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n$.
\end{df}
If $p \in \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$, then the maps $\can_p(X) \colon \cM_p(X)_n \to \widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}|_p$ are defined analogously.

\subsubsection*{The zero locus}

\begin{df}
    We denote by $\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n$ and $\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n$ the zero loci of $\can_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)$ and $\can_{\leq\lambda}(X)$ respectively. We also write $\cZ_{\leq\lambda}^\bN(X,\beta)_n$ and $\cZ_{\leq\lambda}^\bN(X)_n$ if we want to emphasize the representation $\bN$.
\end{df}

In contexts involving multiple representations, we include a superscript to indicate the representation, denoting, for example, $\cZ^{\bN}_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n$ and $\can^{\bN}_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n$.

If $p$ is a point in $\Gr_G$ or in $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$, the substack $\cZ_p(X, \beta)_n \subset \cM_p(X, \beta)_n$ is defined similarly. We regard $\ev_\infty$ as a morphism $\cM_{\leq\lambda}(X)_n\to \widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}\times X$.


\begin{prop}\label{propermoduli}
    The restriction of the evaluation map $\ev_\infty: \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n \to \widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}\times X$ to $\mathcal{Z}_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$ is proper.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Since the morphism $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_{n+1} \to \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$ that forgets the last marked point (and stabilizes) is proper, it suffices to consider the case $n = 0$. For simplicity, we write $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_0 = \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)$.

We first consider the case $X = \bN$. It suffices to prove that for any $p \in \widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$, the restriction of the evaluation map $\ev_\infty: \cM_p(\bN) \to \sE_p(\bN)$ to the zero locus of $\can_p(\bN)$ is proper. We regard $\can_p(\bN)$ as a map $\cM_p(\bN) \to (\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda})_p$.

    Note that we may identify the moduli space $\cM_p(\bN)$ with the vector space of global sections of the vector bundle $\sE_p(\bN)$ over $p \times \PP^1$. Under this identification, the map $\can_p(\bN)$ sends a section $s$ to $[s_\infty]$. It is clear from this description that $\can_p(\bN)$ is a linear map. Note that the composition
    \begin{equation*}
        \BR_p \to \BT_p \to (\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda})_p
    \end{equation*}
    is zero. If $\can_p(\bN)(s) = 0$, then we must have $s_\infty \in \BR_p$, which means $s_\infty \in \bN_\oh \cap \bN_{\C[t^{-1}]} = \bN$. We can summarize this by saying that the assignment $s \mapsto s_\infty$ gives an injective linear map of vector spaces $\ker \can_p(\bN) \hookrightarrow \bN$. However, the evaluation map $\ev_\infty$ also sends $s \mapsto s_\infty$, so this proves the lemma in the case $X = \bN$.

    For the general case, we consider the commutative diagram:
    \begin{center}
    \begin{tikzcd}
    \mathcal Z_{\leq \lambda}(X,\beta) \arrow[r, "j_X", hook] \arrow[d, "\cZ(f)"] 
        & \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X,\beta) \arrow[r, "\ev_\infty"] \arrow[d, "\cM(f)"] 
        & \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X) \arrow[d, "\sE(f)"] \\
    \mathcal Z_{\leq \lambda}(\bN) \arrow[r, "j_{\bN}", hook] 
        & \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\bN) \arrow[r, "\ev_\infty"] 
        & \sE_{\leq \lambda}(\bN)
    \end{tikzcd}
    \end{center}
    It suffices to show that $\cM(f)$ is proper, since this implies the properness of $\cZ(f)$, and hence of the composition $\sE(f) \circ \ev_\infty \circ j_X = \ev_\infty \circ j_{\bN} \circ \cZ(f)$. In particular, this shows that $\ev_\infty \circ j_X$ is proper.

    Properness of $\cM(f)$ follows from the semiprojectivity of $X$. Indeed, $f: X \to \bN$ factors equivariantly as
    \begin{equation*}
        X \stackrel{h}{\longhookrightarrow} \PP^n \times \bN \longrightarrow \bN,
    \end{equation*}
    where $h$ is a closed embedding. Then there is a corresponding factorization of $\cM(f)$:
    \begin{equation*}
        \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X,\beta) \xrightarrow{\cM(h)} \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\PP^n \times \bN, h_* \beta) \longrightarrow \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\bN).
    \end{equation*}
    The natural map induces an isomorphism
    \begin{equation*}
        \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\PP^n \times \bN, h_* \beta) \cong \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\PP^n, (\pr_{\PP^n})_* h_* \beta) \times \cM_{\leq \lambda}(\bN).
    \end{equation*}
    Since $\overline{h}$ is a closed embedding (cf. \cite[Section\ 5.1]{Fulton-Pandharipande}) and $\cM_{\leq \lambda}(\PP^n, (\pr_{\PP^n})_* h_* \beta)$ is proper, the map $\cM(f)$ is also proper, as claimed.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Section-counting map}
\subsubsection*{Virtual fundamental classes}

Consider the fibre diagram
\begin{equation}\label{PBZ}
\begin{tikzcd}
\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n \arrow[r, "j_X"] \arrow[d] & \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n \arrow[d, "\can_{\leq \lambda}(X)"] \\
\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \arrow[r] & \widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}
\end{tikzcd} ,
\end{equation}
where the bottom row is the inclusion of the zero section. We define the virtual fundamental class
\begin{equation}\label{virZ}
[\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^\vir \coloneqq j_X^!\big[\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n\big]^\vir,
\end{equation}
where $j_X^!$ denotes the Gysin pullback
\begin{equation*}
j_X^! \colon H^{T\times\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet\big(\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n\big) 
\longrightarrow H^{T\times\Cx_\hbar}_{\bullet - 2d_\lambda}\big(\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n\big),
\end{equation*}
taken relative to the diagram \eqref{PBZ}. Here, $d_\lambda$ denotes the rank of the vector bundle $\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}$ (see \eqref{SlambdaRank}). We record the following.
\begin{equation}\label{vdim2}
\mathrm{vdim}\big(\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n\big)= \dim X + \dim C_\lambda + c_1^G(X) \cdot \overline\beta-d_\lambda+n.
\end{equation}

We write $\ev_\infty$ in place of $\ev_\infty \circ j_X$ for simplicity. 

\begin{df}\label{Slambda}
We define the \emph{section-counting map}
\begin{equation*}
\widetilde\bbS_{\bN,\leq \lambda} \colon e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda}) \cup H_{T \times \C^\times_\hbar}^\bullet(\mathcal{X}_{\leq \lambda, 0})[[q_G,\tau]] \longrightarrow H^\bullet_{T \times \C^\times_\hbar}(X)[[q_G,\tau]]
\end{equation*}
by setting
\begin{equation}\label{sectioncountingmap}
\widetilde\bbS_{\bN,\leq \lambda}(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda}) \cup \gamma) \vcentcolon= 
\sum_{\beta \in \Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\mathrm{sec}}} 
\sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \,
\PD\circ \pr_{X*} \ev_{\infty*} \left(
    \ev_0^*(\gamma) \prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^*(\hat\tau) \cap 
    [\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^\vir
\right)
\end{equation}
for $\gamma \in H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq \lambda, 0})$. Here, $\pr_X \colon \cX_{\leq \lambda, \infty} \cong \widetilde C_{\leq \lambda} \times X \to X$ is the projection map, and $\mathrm{PD} \colon H_\bullet^{T \times \Cx_\hbar}(X) \to H_{T \times \Cxh}^{2 \dim X - \bullet}(X)$ is the Poincar\'e duality map. The formula for an arbitrary $\gamma \in H_{T \times \C^\times_\hbar}^\bullet(\mathcal{X}_{\leq \lambda, 0})[[q_G,\tau]]$ is defined termwise on its power series expansion.
\end{df}

The above definition makes sense due to the following two lemmas.

\begin{lem}\label{lemmacurveclass2}
There exists a finite subset $S \subset H_2^{\mathrm{ord}, G}(X;\Z)$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\left\{\overline{\beta} \in H_2^{\mathrm{ord}, G}(X;\Z) \,\middle|\, \beta \in \Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\mathrm{sec}} \right\} \subset S + i_*\left(\Eff(X)\right),
\end{equation*}
where $i \colon X \hookrightarrow \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$ is the inclusion of a fibre.
\end{lem}

\begin{lem}\label{graded}
Let $\gamma\in H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})$ be a homogeneous element. Then, each summand in the power series expansion of the right-hand side of \eqref{sectioncountingmap} is of degree $\deg e((\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda}) \cup \gamma)-2\dim C_{\lambda}=2d_\lambda-2\dim C_{\lambda}+\deg\gamma$, which is independent of $\beta$ and $n$.
\end{lem}

\begin{df}\label{bbSN}
We define \emph{the shift operator with matter} $\bN$ to be the map
\begin{equation*}
\bbS_{G, \bN,X} \colon e(\BS_\bN)\cap H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{G \times \C^\times_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]
\longrightarrow H_{T \times \C^\times_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]
\end{equation*}
which sends
\begin{equation}\label{bbSNformula}
e(\BS) \cap [C_{\leq \lambda}] \otimes \alpha \longmapsto 
\widetilde\bbS_{\bN,\leq \lambda} \left(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cup \tw_{\leq \lambda}\left([\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}] \otimes \alpha\right)\right).
\end{equation}
The map $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ is then extended $H_{T \times \C^\times_\hbar}^\bullet(\pt)$-linearly in the first argument. And for a general element of $H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]$, the assignment $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ is then defined termwise on its power series expansion. Here $\tw_{\leq\lambda}$ is the twisting map defined in \eqref{twistlambda}.
\end{df}

Note that by \Cref{twistingeuler}, the expression \eqref{bbSNformula} can also be written as the composition $\bbS_{\bN,\leq\lambda}\circ \tw_{\leq\lambda}(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}]\otimes\alpha)$.

We will simply write $\bbS_{G,\bN}$ when the space $X$ is clear from the context, and we write $\bbS_{G}$ if the representation $\bN=\bf 0$. We will show in \Cref{Wequiv} that $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ is $W$-equivariant and in particular its restriction to the subspace of $W$-invariants gives the map \eqref{Introshiftoperators3}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{lemmacurveclass2}]
We first reduce to the case where $X$ is compact. Indeed, if $C$ is a curve on $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$ representing a section class, we may use the $\C^\times_{\mathrm{dil}}$-action to move $\sigma$ so that it lies in $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X^{\C^\times})$. Hence, we may replace $X$ by $X^{\C^\times}$, which is compact.

Next, we use the $T$-action to move the curve $C$ to a $T$-invariant curve. In particular, its projection $p = \pr_{\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}}(C)$ must be a $T$-fixed point in $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$. We have
\begin{equation*}
\sE_p(X) = \sE_{\rho_\lambda(p)}^T(X),
\end{equation*}
where $\sE^T$ denotes the universal $T$-torsor over $\Gr_T \times \PP^1$. Therefore, we may reduce to the case where $G = T$ is abelian.

Now let $C \subset \sE^T_p(X)$ be a $T$-invariant curve representing a section class with irreducible components $C_0,\dots, C_m$. Then one of them, say $C_0$, must be the constant section
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{Const}_x = \sE_p^T \times_T x
\end{equation*}
for some $x \in X^T$, and all other components lie in the fibres of $\sE_p^T(X) \to p \times \PP^1$. Let $F_1, \dots, F_k$ be the connected components of $X^T$, and let $\beta_i \in \Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\sec}$ be the class represented by $\mathrm{Const}_x$ for some (any) $y \in F_i$. Then the above reasoning gives
\begin{equation*}
[C] \in \Z_{\geq 0} \beta_1 + \dots + \Z_{\geq 0} \beta_k + \Eff(X).
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we may conclude the lemma by taking $S = \{\overline{\beta_1}, \dots, \overline{\beta_k}\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{graded}]
Using \eqref{vdim2} and \Cref{degree}, we see that for any $i_\ell\in I$ and $j_\ell \in \{0, 1, \dots, N\}$, the degree of
\begin{equation*}
\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \,
\mathrm{PD} \circ \pr_{X*} \ev_{\infty*}
    \left( \ev_0^* (\gamma) \prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^* (\tau^{i_\ell,j_\ell} \hat\phi_{i_\ell,j_\ell}) \cap 
    [\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^\vir \right)
\end{equation*}
is equal to
\begin{align*}
& \quad 2c_1^G(X) \cdot \overline{\beta} + 2 \dim X 
-  2 \mathrm{vdim}(\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X, \beta)_n) + \deg \gamma 
 + \sum_{\ell=1}^n \left( \deg \tau^{i_\ell,j_\ell} + \deg \phi_{i_\ell,j_\ell} \right) \\
&= 2d_\lambda - 2 \dim C_{\lambda} + \deg \gamma. \qedhere
\end{align*}
\end{proof}

We adopt the cohomological convention for degrees in Borel--Moore homology. In particular, an element of $H_k^{T \times \Cxh}(\Gr_G)$ has degree $-k$.

Since $\tw_{\leq\lambda}$ increases the degree by $2\dim C_\lambda$ (due to Poincar\'e duality), we obtain the following corollary of \Cref{graded}.\begin{cor}
$\bbS_{G,\bN}$ preserves cohomological degrees.
\end{cor}

\begin{rem}\label{remSemi-ve}
Suppose $G = T$ is abelian, and let $\lambda \in \Lambda$ be semi-negative with respect to $X$, in the sense that $\xi(\lambda) \leq 0$ for every weight $\xi$ of $H^0(X, \oh_X)$ (cf.~\cite[Definition~3.3]{Iritani}).

It is easy to show that there exists a representation $\bN$ with $\lambda$-non-positive weights and a proper $T$-equivariant morphism $f \colon X \to \bN$. By \Cref{Nweights}, we have $\BS_{t^\lambda} = 0$, and hence $\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n = \cM_{\leq \lambda}(X)_n$. In this case, the properness statement in \Cref{propermoduli} follows from~\cite[Lemma~3.5]{Iritani}.

Remark~3.10 of~\cite{Iritani} also notes that the semi-negativity condition implies that the shift operator is defined without localization. One may regard \Cref{propermoduli} and \Cref{bbSN} as generalizations of this observation. In \Cref{propertyshiftoperators} below, we will explain in detail how our construction of shift operators compares with that in~\cite{Iritani}.
\end{rem}

By the $H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$-linearities of $\tw_{\leq\lambda}$ and $\widetilde\bbS_{\bN,\leq\lambda}$, we can extend the shift operators to the localized Coulomb branch algebras (see \eqref{localizeindepofN}).

\begin{df}\label{localisedshiftop}
    Define 
    \[\bbS_{G,\bN,\loc}: \left(e(\BS_\bN)\cap H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)\right)_\loc\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]\longrightarrow H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X)_\loc[[q_G,\tau]]\]
    by extending $\Frac H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$-linearly in the first argument. Here $H_{T\times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)_\loc \coloneq \mathrm{Frac}H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)\otimes_{H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)} H^\bullet_{T\times\Cxh}(X)$ is the localized cohomology.
\end{df}

\subsubsection*{The Seidel representation}
\begin{df}\label{Seidelrepmap}
Setting $\hbar = 0$ defines the \textit{equivariant Seidel representation},
\begin{equation*}
\bbS_{G,\bN}^{\hbar = 0} \colon e(\BS_\bN)\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C} QH_G^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]] \longrightarrow QH_G^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]].
\end{equation*}
The \textit{equivariant Seidel map} is then defined as the map
\begin{align*}
\Psi_{G,\bN,X} \colon & e(\BS_\bN)\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G)\longrightarrow QH_G^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]], \\
&\Gamma \longmapsto \bbS^{\hbar=0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma,1).
\end{align*}
\end{df}

\begin{rem}
    Similar to \Cref{localisedshiftop}, we may define the localized Seidel representation and the localized Seidel map by extending $\Frac H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$-linearly. We will denote them by $\bbS^{\hbar=0}_{G,\bN,\loc}$ and $\Psi_{G,\bN,\loc}$ respectively.
\end{rem}

\subsection{Independence of resolutions}

In this subsection, we prove basic properties of $\bbS_{G,\bN}$, including the independence of choice of resolutions $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}$.

\begin{prop}\label{SGNsecondmodule}
Let \( P \in H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(\pt) \). Then
\begin{equation*}
\bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma \otimes (P \cup \alpha)) = \bbS_{G,\bN}((u^* P \cap \Gamma) \otimes \alpha), 
\end{equation*}
where $u^*:H_G^\bullet(\pt)\to  H_{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\Gr_G)$ is the second module structure defined in \Cref{secondmodule}.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    It follows from \Cref{twistinglinear} and the projection formula.
\end{proof}
The following proposition explains the origin of the name ``shift operator''.
\begin{prop}\label{shiftoplinear}
    The map $\bbS_{T,\bN}$ is twisted-linear, i.e., for $P(a,\hbar)\in H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\pt)$ and any cocharacter $\lambda$,
    \[\bbS_{T,\bN}(e(\BS_{t^\lambda})\cap [t^\lambda]\otimes P(a,\hbar)\alpha)= P(a+\lambda(\hbar),\hbar)\, \bbS_{T,\bN}(e(\BS_{t^\lambda})\cap[t^\lambda]\otimes\alpha).\]
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    It follows from \Cref{abeliantwlinear} and the fact that $\widetilde\bbS_{\bN\leq\lambda}$ is $H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\pt)$-linear.
\end{proof}

Let $1$ denote the class $[C_{\leq0}]\in H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$. For any $G$-representation $\bN$, we have $\BS_{\leq0}=0$, so that $1\in e(\BS_\bN)\cap H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$.
\begin{prop}\label{1=1}
    The operator $\bbS_{G,\bN}(1,-)$ is equal to the inclusion 
    \[
    H^\bullet_{G\times\Cx_\hbar}(X)[[q_G,\tau]]\subset H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]].
    \]
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Note that $\BS_{t^0} = 0$, and by definition, $\tw_{\leq 0}$ is the inclusion map. Therefore, it suffices to show that the section-counting map
\[
\widetilde{\bbS}_{\leq 0} : H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G, \tau]] \longrightarrow H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G, \tau]]
\]
is the identity. Let $\gamma, \gamma' \in H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X)$. Since 
\[
\overline{M}_{0,2}(X \times \PP^1, (0,1)) = X \times \overline{M}_{0,2}(\PP^1, 1),
\]
it is immediate that that $\left\langle \iota_{0*}\gamma, \iota_{\infty*}\gamma' \right\rangle^{X \times \PP^1}_{0, (0,1)} = (\gamma, \gamma')$. Note that $\hat\tau=\tau\otimes 1$. Hence, it suffices to prove that the Gromov–Witten invariant
\begin{equation}\label{n+2GW}
\left\langle \gamma \otimes \mathrm{PD}([0]),\; \underbrace{\tau \otimes 1, \dots, \tau \otimes 1}_{n \text{ times}},\; \gamma' \otimes \mathrm{PD}([\infty]) \right\rangle_{0,(\beta,1)}^{X \times \PP^1}
\end{equation}
vanishes whenever $\beta \neq 0$ or $n \neq 0$. Indeed, the forgetful morphism
\[
\pr : \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(X \times \PP^1, (\beta,1)) \to \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(X, \beta)
\]
has relative dimension $3$ if $\beta \neq 0$ or $n \neq 0$. Therefore,
\[
\pr_*\left( \EV_0^*(1\otimes \PD([0])) \cup \EV_\infty^*(1\otimes \PD([\infty])) \right) = 0,
\]
and by the projection formula, the invariant in~\eqref{n+2GW} vanishes when $\beta \neq 0$ or $n \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}\label{shiftopindependentresol}
    The operator $\bbS_{G,\bN}$ is independent of the choices of resolutions $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'\to C_{\leq\lambda}$ is another resolution of $C_{\leq\lambda}$ on which $\BS_{\lambda}$ extends as in \Cref{existresol}. Replacing $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'$ by a resolution of the fibre product $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'\times_{C_{\leq\lambda}}\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}$ if necessary, we may assume $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'\to C_{\leq\lambda}$ factors as 
    \[\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'\stackrel{r}{\longrightarrow}\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}\longrightarrow C_{\leq\lambda}.\] 
    Let us denote
    \[\widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}=\tw_{\leq\lambda}\left([\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}]\otimes\alpha \right),\quad \widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}'=\tw_{\leq\lambda}'\left([\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}']\otimes\alpha\right).\]
    Let $\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X)$ and $\sE_{\leq\lambda}'(X)$ denote the Seidel spaces corresponding to $\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}$ and $\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}'$, respectively. We write $\hat\tau \in H^\bullet_{T\times\Cxh}(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X))$ and $\hat\tau'\in H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\sE_{\leq\lambda}'(X))$ for the assignments \eqref{tauhatmap} associated to $\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X)$ and $\sE'_{\leq\lambda}(X)$, respectively. By \Cref{twistingfunctorial} and the projection formula, these classes satisfy
    \begin{equation}\label{alphapushforward}\widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}=r_*\widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}',\quad \hat\tau = r_*\hat\tau'.\end{equation}
    Here, we use the same symbol $r_*$ to denote the pushforward maps $H_{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_{\hbar}}^\bullet(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\prime\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh} X)\to H_{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\widetilde{G}_\ok^{\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X)$ and $H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\sE'_{\leq\lambda}(X))\to H^\bullet_{T\times\Cxh}(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X))$, whenever there is no confusion.

    Write $\widetilde\bbS$ and $\widetilde\bbS'$ for the respective section-counting maps \eqref{Slambda}. It remains to verify that
    \[\widetilde\bbS(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cup\widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}')=\widetilde\bbS'(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cup \widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}).\]

    Note that the moduli spaces are related by the fibre diagram
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{tikzcd}
{\overline M_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq\lambda}'(X),\beta)} \arrow[d, "\pr_{\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}'}"'] \arrow[r] & {\overline M_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X,r_*\beta)} \arrow[d, "\pr _{\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}}"] \\
\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}' \arrow[r, "r"]                                                                 & \widetilde C_{\leq\lambda} .
\end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation*}
    Therefore, by functoriality (see \cite[Proposition 5.10]{Intrinsic}), the virtual fundamental classes are related by Gysin pullback
    \[[\,\overline M_{0,n+2}(\sE'_{\leq\lambda}(X),\beta)]^\vir= r^![\,\overline M_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(X),r_*\beta)]^\vir,\]
    and hence the same is true for the zero loci (see \cite[Section 6.4]{Fulton})
    \[[\cZ'_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n]^{\vir}=r^![\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X,r_*\beta)_n]^{\vir}.\]
    Denote by $\ev_0',\ev_\infty',\pr_X'$ the evaluation and projection maps for the spaces associated with $\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}'$. Using the above, we then compute
    \begin{align*}
        \widetilde\bbS'(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cup\widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}') 
        &= \sum_{\beta}\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{ q^{\overline\beta}}{n!} \, \mathrm{PD} \circ \pr'_{X*}\ev'_{\infty*} \left(\ev^{\prime*}_0(\widetilde\alpha'_{\leq\lambda})\prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^{\prime*}(\hat{\tau}')\cap [\cZ'_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n]^{\vir}\right)\\
        &= \sum_\beta \sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{ q^{\overline\beta}}{n!} \, \PD\circ \pr_{X*}\ev_{\infty*}\, r_*\left(\ev_0^{\prime*}(\widetilde{\alpha}'_{\leq\lambda})\prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^{\prime*}(\hat{\tau}') \cap  r^![\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X,r_*\beta)_n]^\vir \right) \\
        &= \sum_{\beta} \sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{ q^{\overline\beta}}{n!}  \, \mathrm{PD} \circ \pr_{X*}\ev_{\infty*} \left(\ev^{*}_0( \widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda})\prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^{*}(\hat{\tau})\cap [\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n]^{\vir}\right)\\
        &= \widetilde\bbS(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cup \widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}).
    \end{align*}
Here, we have used the identification $\overline{r_*\beta}=\overline\beta$ and the last equality is by projection formula and \eqref{alphapushforward}.
\end{proof}



\section{Shift operators III: properties}\label{propertyshiftoperators}

In this section, we establish several key properties of the shift operators. In particular, we prove \Cref{IntroThm2} and \Cref{IntroThm1} as stated in the introduction. Throughout, we assume that the pair $(X, f \colon X \to \bN)$ satisfies the assumptions outlined in \Cref{sectionshiftoperators}.

\subsection{Change of representations and groups}

In this subsection, we study the compatibility of shift operators under changes of the representation or the group. All the properties proved in this subsection are stated for $\bbS_{G,\bN}$, but the analogous statements all hold for $\bbS^{\hbar=0}_{G,\bN}$ and $\Psi_{G,\bN}$ with similar proofs (e.g., by specializing $\hbar=0$ in all the constructions and maps).

\begin{prop}[Change of representations]\label{shiftopfunctoriality}
    Suppose $\bV$ is another $G$-representation, and either of the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
        \item $\bV$ contains $\bN$ as a subrepresentation.
        \item $\bN$ is a quotient representation of $\bV$, and the morphism $f:X\to \bN$ factors through $\bV\to \bN$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then $\bbS_{G,\bV}$ is equal to the restriction of $\bbS_{G,\bN}$ along
    \[e(\BS_{\bV})\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_G)\subset e(\BS_{\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_G).\]
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
We prove case (a); the other case proceeds similarly.

Write $\bV = \bN \oplus \bN'$, and choose a resolution $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$ such that both $\BS_\bN$ and $\BS_{\bN'}$ extend over it. Since the composition $X \to \bV \to \bN'$ is zero, we have $\can_{\leq \lambda}^{\bN'}(X) = 0$. Moreover, since $\can_{\leq \lambda}^{\bV} (X)= (\can_{\leq \lambda}^{\bN}(X), \can_{\leq \lambda}^{\bN'}(X))$, it follows that
\begin{equation*}
\cZ_{\leq \lambda}^{\bV}(X,\beta)_n = \cZ_{\leq \lambda}^{\bN}(X,\beta)_n
\end{equation*}
for any $\beta \in \Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\mathrm{sec}}$.

We also have the identity
\[
[\cZ_{\leq \lambda}^{\bV}(X,\beta)_n]^{\mathrm{vir}} = e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\bN',\leq \lambda}) \cap [\cZ_{\leq \lambda}^{\bN}(X,\beta)_n]^{\mathrm{vir}},
\]
which follows from \eqref{virZ} and excess intersection formula associated to the diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
\cZ_{\leq\lambda}(X,\beta)_n \arrow[r, "j_X"] \arrow[d] & 
\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n \arrow[d, "\can^\bN_{\leq \lambda}(X)"] \\
\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \arrow[r] \arrow[d, equal] & 
\widetilde{\BS}_{\bN, \leq \lambda} \arrow[d] \\
\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda} \arrow[r] & 
\widetilde{\BS}_{\bV, \leq \lambda} = \widetilde{\BS}_{\bN, \leq \lambda} \oplus \widetilde{\BS}_{\bN', \leq \lambda}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}

Let $\ev_0'$ and $\ev_\infty'$ denote the evaluation maps on $\cZ^{\bV}_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n$, and writing $\widetilde\alpha_{\leq\lambda}=\tw_{\leq\lambda}([\widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}]\otimes \alpha)$. Then we verify
\begin{align*}
&\bbS_{G, \bN}(e(\BS_{\bV}) \cap [C_{\leq \lambda}] \otimes \alpha)\\
&= \widetilde{\bbS}_{\bN, \leq \lambda} \left( e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\bN, \leq \lambda}) \cup e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\bN', \leq \lambda}) \cup \widetilde{\alpha}_{\leq \lambda} \right) \\
&= \sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!}\, \PD \circ \pr_{X*} \ev_{\infty*} \left( 
\ev_0^*\left( \widetilde{\alpha}_{\leq \lambda} \cup e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\bN', \leq \lambda}) \right) 
\prod_{\ell=1}^n\ev_\ell^{*}(\hat{\tau})\cap [\cZ_{\leq \lambda}^{\bN}(X, \beta)_n]^{\mathrm{vir}} \right) \\
&= \sum_\beta \sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!}\, \PD \circ \pr_{X*} \ev'_{\infty*} \left( 
\ev_0^{\prime *}(\widetilde{\alpha}_{\leq \lambda}) 
\prod_{\ell=1}^n\ev_\ell^{\prime*}(\hat{\tau})\cap [\cZ_{\leq \lambda}^{\bV}(X, \beta)_n]^{\mathrm{vir}} \right) \\
&= \bbS_{G, \bV}(e(\BS_{\bV}) \cap [C_{\leq \lambda}] \otimes \alpha). \qedhere
\end{align*}
\end{proof}
\begin{cor}\label{CommonDomain}
Suppose $\bN'$ is another representation of $G$, and $g \colon X \to \bN'$ is a $G$-equivariant proper morphism. Then
\[
\bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma \otimes \alpha) = \bbS_{G,\bN'}(\Gamma \otimes \alpha)
\]
for any $\Gamma$ in the intersection of $(e(\BS_\bN) \cap H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G))$ and $(e(\BS_{\bN'}) \cap H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G))$, and $\alpha \in H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)$.
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
Let $\bV = \bN \oplus \bN'$, and let $P \in H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$ be such that $P \Gamma \in e(\BS_{\bV}) \cap H^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$. By \Cref{shiftopfunctoriality}, we have
\[
\bbS_{G,\bN}(P \Gamma \otimes \alpha) = \bbS_{G,\bV}(P \Gamma \otimes \alpha) = \bbS_{G,\bN'}(P \Gamma \otimes \alpha).
\]
The result then follows from the lemma below by taking $R = H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}\label{Lemmatorsionfree}
Let $R$ be an integral domain, and let $\zeta, \zeta' \colon A \to B$ be homomorphisms of torsion-free $R$-modules. Suppose that $\zeta$ and $\zeta'$ agree on a submodule $A' \subset A$ such that 
\[
\Frac(R) \otimes_R A = \Frac(R) \otimes_R A'.
\]
Then $\zeta = \zeta'$.
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
Let $a \in A$. Since $\Frac(R) \otimes_R A = \Frac(R) \otimes_R A'$, there exists $r \in R \setminus \{0\}$ such that $r a \in A'$. Then
\[
r \zeta(a) = \zeta(r a) = \zeta'(r a) = r \zeta'(a),
\]
and since $B$ is torsion-free over $R$, it follows that $\zeta(a) = \zeta'(a)$.
\end{proof}

Recall that the localization $\left(e(\BS_\bN)\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G)\right)_\loc$ is independent of the representation $\bN$ (see \eqref{localizeindepofN}). The following is now immediate.
\begin{cor}[Independence of $\bN$ and $f$]\label{independentofN}
    The localized shift operators $\bbS_{G,\bN,\loc}$ is independent of $f:X\to\bN$.
\end{cor}

\subsubsection*{Change of groups}
Next, we study the functoriality with respect to changing groups.

Following \cite[3(vii)(c)]{BFN}, let $G'\to G$ be a finite covering, and suppose that $\bN$ is a $G$-representation, so it also has an action of $G'$. Then the Pontrjagin dual $F$ of $\pi_1(G)/\pi_1(G')$ acts on $e(\BS_{\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_G)$ such that
\[ e(\BS_{G',\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{T'_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_{G'})= \left(e(\BS_{G,\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_G)\right)^F.\]

\begin{prop}
    Let $G'\to G$ be a finite covering, and let $\bN$ be a $G$-representation, then $\bbS_{G',\bN'}$ is the restriction of $\bbS_{G,\bN}$ to $e(\BS_{\bN'})\cap H^{T'_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_{G'})$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    Note that the equivariant cohomology with respect to $G$ and $G'$ coincides since the coefficient is $\C$. The map $G'\to G$ induces a closed embedding $\Gr_{G'}\hookrightarrow\Gr_G$. For a coweight $\lambda'\in \Lambda_{G'}$ mapping to $\lambda\in \Lambda_G$, there is a natural identification $C_{\leq\lambda'}\cong C_{\leq\lambda}$, which induces an isomorphism $\widetilde{\BS}_{G',\leq\lambda'}\cong \widetilde{\BS}_{G,\leq\lambda}$. Therefore, $\bbS_{G',\bN}(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda'})\cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda'}]\otimes\alpha)$ coincides with $\bbS_{G,\bN}(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cap [\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda}]\otimes\alpha)$.
\end{proof}

Next, consider a short exact sequence 
$1\to G\to \hG\to T_F\to 1$ of connected reductive groups, where $T_F$ is a torus. Let $\bN$ be a $\hG$-representation. Recall that the quantized Coulomb branch algebras for $(\hG,\bN)$ and \((G,\bN)\) are related via \textit{quantum Hamiltonian reduction} with respect to $T_F^{\vee}$, the Pontrjagin dual of \(\pi_1(T_F)\) (see \cite[Proposition~3.18]{BFN}). Specifically, we have
\begin{equation}\label{qhamrestriction} 
\bigl(e(\BS_{\hG,\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{\hT_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_{\hG})\bigr)^{T_F^\vee} \;\cong\; e(\BS_{\hG,\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{\hG_\oh\rtimes\C^\times_\hbar}\bigl(\Gr_G\bigr),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{qhamquotient}
e(\BS_{G,\bN})\cap H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \cong \Bigl(e(\BS_{\hG,\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{\hG_\oh\rtimes\C^\times_\hbar}(\Gr_G)\Bigr) \otimes_{H^\bullet_{\hG\times\C^\times_\hbar}(\pt)}\, H^\bullet_{G\times\C^\times_\hbar}(\pt).
\end{equation}

Now suppose that $X$ has a $\hG$-action such that $f:X\to \bN$ is $\hG$-equivariant and proper. Consider the shift operator $\bbS_{\hG,\bN}$. We will construct a reduced operator $\bbS_{\hG,\bN}^{\mathrm{red}}$ as follows.

First, we restrict $\bbS_{\hG,\bN}$ to the subalgebra \eqref{qhamrestriction}. This map descends to $H_{\hG\times\Cx}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]$ (rather than $q_{\hG}$), since for any section class $\beta$ of the Seidel space corresponding to a coweight $\lambda\in \Lambda_G\subset \Lambda_{\widehat G}$, the assignment $\beta\mapsto \overline\beta$ factors through $H_2^G(X,\Z)\to H_2^{\hG}(X,\Z)$. Next, we quotient out the $T_F$-equivariant parameters by composing with the natural projection map $H_{\hT\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]\to H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]$. Finally, by \Cref{shiftoplinear}, the map descends to
\[\bbS_{\hG,\bN}^{\mathrm{red}}: e(\BS_{G,\bN})\cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}(\Gr_G)\otimes_{\C[\hbar]}\, H_{G\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]\longrightarrow H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]].\]

\begin{prop}[Quantum Hamiltonian reduction]\label{qham}
The shift operator $\bbS_{G,\bN}$ coincides with the reduced operator $\bbS_{\hG,\bN}^{\mathrm{red}}$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
This follows directly from the construction\label{Mention the two seidel spaces are the same}, after specializing the twisting map, the evaluation maps, and the projection map from $\hG$- (or $\hT$-)equivariance to $G$- (or $T$-)equivariance. The only map that requires verification is $\tw_{\leq\lambda}$. Note that for any $f\in H_{T_F}^\bullet(\pt)$ and $\Gamma\subset H^{\hT_\oh\rtimes\Cx_\hbar}_\bullet(\Gr_G)$ we have 
\[\tw_{\leq\lambda}\left(\Gamma\otimes (f\cup\alpha)\right)=f\cup\tw_{\leq\lambda}(\Gamma\otimes\alpha).\]
In other words, the map $\tw_{\leq\lambda}$ for the group $\hG$, when restricted to the subalgebra in \eqref{qhamrestriction}, descends to the quotient in \eqref{qhamquotient}.
\end{proof}



\subsection{Properties of shift operators: the \texorpdfstring{$T$}{T}-compact case} \label{Tcompact}
In this subsection, we assume that the $T$-fixed locus $X^T$ is compact. We will explain in the next subsection how to remove this assumption.

\subsubsection*{Extension to localizations}
Let $\lambda \in \Lambda$ and $\beta \in \Eff(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X))^{\sec}$. By comparing \eqref{virM} and \eqref{virZ}, and applying the excess intersection formula (\cite[Theorem 6.3]{Fulton}), we obtain:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
(j_X)_*[\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^\vir 
&= e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}) \cap [\cM_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^\vir \\
&= e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}) \cap j_\cM^! \left[\,\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)\right]^{\vir}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

Therefore,
\begin{align*}
&\widetilde\bbS_{\bN,\leq \lambda}(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda}) \cup \gamma)\\
&= \sum_{\beta}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \,
  \PD \circ \pr_{X*} \ev_{\infty*} \Bigl(
    \ev_0^* (\gamma) \prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^{*}(\hat{\tau}) \cap 
    [\cZ_{\leq \lambda}(X, \beta)_n]^\vir
  \Bigr) \\
&= \sum_{\beta} \sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \,
  \PD \circ \pr_{X*} \ev_{\infty*} \Bigl(
    \ev_0^* \bigl( \gamma \cup 
    e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}) \bigr) \prod_{\ell=1}^n \ev_\ell^{*}(\hat{\tau})\cap 
    j_\cM^! \bigl[ \,\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta) \bigr]^\vir
  \Bigr) \\
&= \sum_{\beta} \sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \,
  \PD \circ \pr_{X*} \iota_{\infty}^* \EV_{\infty*} \Bigl(
    \EV_0^* \iota_{0*} \bigl( \gamma \cup 
    e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda}) \bigr) \prod_{\ell=1}^n\EV_\ell^*(\hat{\tau})\cap 
    \bigl[\, \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta) \bigr]^\vir
  \Bigr).
\end{align*}
Here, all the pushforwards are understood as using localization.

Originally, $\bbS_{\bN,\leq\lambda}$ is defined on the subspace $e(\widetilde\BS_{\leq\lambda})\cap H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})$. By the above computation, we may extend the definition of $\bbS_{\bN,\leq\lambda}$ to $H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})_\loc$ to obtain a map $\widetilde\bbS_{\leq\lambda}$ that is independent of $\bN$. For $\gamma\in H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})_\loc$, we set 
\begin{equation*}
\widetilde\bbS_{\leq \lambda}(\gamma) \vcentcolon= \sum_{\beta} \sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \,
  \PD \circ \pr_{X*} \iota_{\infty}^* \EV_{\infty*} \Bigl(
    \EV_0^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma) \prod_{\ell=1}^n\EV_\ell^*(\hat{\tau})\cap 
    \bigl[\, \overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta) \bigr]^\vir
  \Bigr).
\end{equation*}

Equivalently, let $\{\eta_i\}$ is a $H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$-basis of $H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})$, and $\{\eta^i\}$ the dual basis under the Poincaré pairing. Then
\begin{equation}\label{localshift1}
\widetilde\bbS_{\leq \lambda}(\gamma) 
= \sum_{\beta,i}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \, \eta_i \int_{[\overline{M}_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)]^\vir}
\EV_0^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma) \prod_{\ell=1}^n\EV_\ell^*(\hat{\tau}) 
\EV_\infty^* \iota_{\infty*} \pr_X^* (\eta^i).
\end{equation}

Similarly, for any $\mu \in \Lambda$ and $\gamma\in H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})_\loc$, we define:
\begin{equation}\label{localshift2}
\widetilde\bbS_{t^\mu}(\gamma) 
= \sum_{\beta,i}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{q^{\overline{\beta}}}{n!} \, \eta_i \int_{[\overline{M}_{0,2}(\sE_{t^\mu}(X), \beta)]^\vir}
\EV_0^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma) \prod_{\ell=1}^n \EV_\ell^*(\hat{\tau})
\EV_\infty^* \iota_{\infty*}(\eta^i).
\end{equation}



\subsubsection*{A localization formula}
Fix $\lambda \in \Lambda$. For any $\mu \leq \lambda$, let $F_\mu$ denote the $T\times\Cx_\hbar$-fixed locus of $\rho_\lambda^{-1}(t^\mu)$. Let $e_\mu \in H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(F_\mu)$ be the Euler class of the normal bundle of $F_\mu$ in $\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}$.

\medskip

Recall that we denote $X^\mu= t^\mu G_\oh\times_{G_\oh} X\subset G_\ok\times_{G_\oh}X$. The restriction of the $X$-bundle $\cX_{\leq\lambda,0}\to \widetilde C_{\leq\lambda}$ to $F_\mu$ is naturally identified with $F_\mu\times X^\mu$. Let $\rho_{X,\lambda} \colon \cX_{\leq \lambda, 0} \to G_\ok^{\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X$ be the resolution map induced by $\rho_\lambda$ (see \Cref{zerofibre}), whose restriction to $F_\mu\times X^\mu$ is equal to the projection map
\[
\pr_{X^\mu} \colon F_\mu \times X^\mu \to X^\mu.
\]
Since each $F_\mu$ is proper, we can define the pushforward $(\rho_{X,\lambda})_*$ in cohomology via localization.

Let $\gamma \in H_{T \times \Cx_{\hbar}}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq \lambda,0})_\loc$, and define
\begin{equation}\label{gammamu}
\gamma_\mu = (\pr_{X^\mu})_*\left( \frac{1}{e_\mu} \cdot \gamma \big|_{F_\mu \times X^\mu} \right).
\end{equation}
Then we have the equality
\[
(\rho_{X,\lambda})_* \gamma = \sum_{\mu \leq \lambda} \gamma_\mu
\]
in the localized cohomology ring
\[
H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(G_\ok^{\leq\lambda}\times_{G_\oh}X)_\loc \cong \bigoplus_{\mu \leq \lambda} H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^\mu)_\loc.
\]


\begin{prop}\label{Localprop}
For any $\gamma \in H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\cX_{\leq\lambda,0})$, let $\gamma_\mu$ be defined by~\eqref{gammamu}. Then we have
\[
\widetilde\bbS_{\leq \lambda}(\gamma) = \sum_\mu \widetilde\bbS_{t^{\mu}}(\gamma_\mu).
\]
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
We apply the virtual localization formula \cite{vloc}, following closely the proof of \cite[Proposition~3.12]{Chow}.

We write $\overline M_n = \overline M_{0,n+2}(\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X), \beta)$ and $\overline M_{\mu,n} = \overline M_{0,n+2}(\sE_{t^\mu}(X), \beta)$ for a section class $\beta$ in $\sE_{\leq \lambda}(X)$. The latter is empty if $\beta$ is not represented by a curve contained in $\sE_{t^\mu}(X)$. We have
\[
j_\mu:F_\mu \times \sE_{t^\mu}(X) \cong F_\mu \times_{\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}} \sE_{\leq \lambda}(X) \hookrightarrow \sE_{\leq\lambda}(X),
\]
and hence inducing
\[
\overline j_\mu: F_\mu \times \overline M_{\mu,n}\cong  F_\mu \times_{\widetilde{C}_{\leq \lambda}}\overline M_n \hookrightarrow \overline M_n.
\]
Taking $T \times \Cxh$-fixed loci, we obtain
\[
\overline M_n^{T \times \Cxh} = \bigsqcup_{\mu \leq \lambda} F_\mu \times \overline M_{\mu,n}^{T \times \Cxh}.
\]
Therefore, the virtual localization formula implies that
\begin{equation}\label{LocaliseMvir}
    [\overline M_n]^\vir =\sum_{\mu\leq\lambda}\overline j_{\mu*}\left(\frac 1{e_\mu} \left[F_\mu]\times [\overline M_{\mu,n} \right]^\vir\right).
\end{equation}


Now let $\phi \in H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)$. Let us write $\EV_{\mu,i}:\overline M_{\mu,n}\to \sE_{t^\mu}(X)$ for the evaluation maps on $\overline M_\mu$, and $\pr_{\overline M_\mu}: F_\mu \times \overline M_{\mu,n}\to \overline M_{\mu,n}$ be the projection. We compute:
\begin{align*}
\int_{[\overline{M}_n]^\vir} 
& \EV_0^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma) 
  \prod_{\ell=1}^n \EV_\ell^* (\hat{\tau}) \,
  \EV_\infty^* \iota_{\infty*} \pr_X^* (\phi) \\
&= \sum_{\mu \leq \lambda} 
   \int_{[F_\mu] \times [\overline{M}_{\mu,n}]^\vir} 
   \frac{1}{e_\mu} \,
   \overline{j}_\mu^* \EV_0^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma) 
   \prod_{\ell=1}^n \EV_\ell^* (\hat{\tau}) \,
   j_\mu^* \EV_\infty^* \iota_{\infty*} \pr_X^* (\phi) \\
&= \sum_{\mu \leq \lambda} 
   \int_{[F_\mu] \times [\overline{M}_{\mu,n}]^\vir} 
   \frac{1}{e_\mu} \,
   \EV_{\mu,0}^* j_\mu^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma) 
   \prod_{\ell=1}^n \EV_\ell^* (\hat{\tau}) \,
   \pr_{\overline{M}_\mu}^* \EV_{\mu,\infty}^* \iota_{\infty*} (\phi) \\
&= \sum_{\mu \leq \lambda} 
   \int_{[\overline{M}_{\mu,n}]^\vir} 
   \EV_{\mu,0}^* \iota_{0*} (\gamma_\mu) 
   \prod_{\ell=1}^n \EV_{\mu,\ell}^* (\hat{\tau}) \,
   \EV_{\mu,\infty}^* \iota_{\infty*} (\phi).
\end{align*}

Here, the first equality follows from \eqref{LocaliseMvir} and the last equality follows from \eqref{gammamu}. In view of equations \eqref{localshift1} and \eqref{localshift2}, the result follows.
\end{proof}
\begin{cor}\label{TGcompatible}
For any $ \Gamma\in H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_{\bullet}(\Gr_T)_\loc \cong H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_{\bullet}(\Gr_G)_\loc $ and any $\alpha\in H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]$, we have
\[
\bbS_{G,\bN,\loc}(\Gamma\otimes\alpha) = \bbS_{T,\bN,\loc}(\Gamma\otimes\alpha).
\]
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
This follows from \Cref{twistingcompat} and \Cref{Localprop}.
\end{proof}


\begin{cor}\label{Wequiv}
    The map $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ is $W$-equivariant.
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
By \Cref{TGcompatible} and \Cref{shiftopindependentresol}, $\bbS_{G,\bN}$ and $\bbS_{T,\bN,\loc}$ agrees on their common domain of definition.

By \Cref{twistingcompat}, the map $\tw_T$ is $W$-equivariant, so it remains to show that $\bigoplus \widetilde\bbS_{t^\lambda}$ is also $W$-equivariant. Observe that the $G_{\C[t^{-1}]} \rtimes \Cxh$-action on $\sE(X)$ induces an $N(T)$-action on
\begin{equation*}
\bigsqcup_{\mu \in \Lambda} \sE_{t^\mu}(X),
\end{equation*}
which is compatible with the natural $N(T)$-actions on both $T_\ok \times_{T_\oh} X$ and on $\Gr_T$.

This implies that $\bigoplus \widetilde\bbS_{t^\lambda}$ is $W$-equivariant, because the integrand in \eqref{localshift2} involves Gromov--Witten invariants of $\bigsqcup_{\mu \in \Lambda} \sE_{t^\mu}(X)$, which are invariant under the $N(T)$-action.
\end{proof}

In view of \Cref{Wequiv}, it makes sense to take the $W$-invariant part of $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$. This gives a map, still denoted by the same symbol,
\[\bbS_{G,\bN,X}: \sA^\hbar_{G,\bN}\otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{G\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]\longrightarrow H_{G\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]].\]


\subsubsection*{Module property}
For each $\lambda$, consider
\begin{equation*}
\bbS_{t^\lambda} \coloneqq \widetilde \bbS_{t^\lambda} \circ \Phi_\lambda :
H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)_\loc[[q_G,\tau]] \longrightarrow 
H_{T \times \Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)_\loc[[q_G,\tau]],
\end{equation*}
where $\Phi_\lambda = \tw_T([t^\lambda] \otimes -)$ is the twisting map (see \Cref{abeliantwlinear}), and $\widetilde \bbS_{t^\lambda}$ is defined in~\eqref{localshift2}.

\begin{prop}\label{moduleprop}
    For $\lambda,\mu\in\Lambda$, $\alpha\in H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(X)$ and $P\in H_{T\times\Cx_\hbar}^\bullet(\pt)$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \bbS_{t^{\lambda+\mu}}(\alpha) = \bbS_{t^\lambda} (\bbS_{t^\mu}(\alpha))  , \quad
    \bbS_{t^\lambda} (P\alpha) = \Phi_\lambda(P)\,\bbS_{t^\lambda} (\alpha).
\end{align*}
\end{prop}

\begin{rem}\label{IritaniRemark}
Consider the pairing $(-,-)_\lambda$ on $H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)$ given by $
(\alpha, \alpha')_\lambda \coloneqq \Phi_\lambda(\alpha, \alpha')$,
where $(-,-)$ is the original Poincar\'e pairing. Let $\bbS_\lambda^{\Iri}$ denote the ``adjoint'' of $\bbS_{t^\lambda}$ in the sense that $(\bbS_\lambda^{\Iri}(\alpha), \alpha') = (\alpha, \bbS_{t^\lambda} (\alpha'))_\lambda.$

The operator $\bbS_\lambda^{\Iri}$ agrees with the shift operators defined in~\cite[Definition 3.9]{Iritani}, provided that in \emph{loc.~cit.} one replaces all $\hat{d} - \sigma_{\min}$ with $\overline{d}$. The appearance of the adjoint is due to a difference in the conventions for the zero and infinity fibres in our definition of Seidel spaces versus that in Iritani’s (cf.~\Cref{CompareIritani2}).
\end{rem}

The following is a slight modification of \cite[Definition 3.13]{Iritani}.
\begin{df}[Shift operator on the Givental space]\label{GiventalShift}
Let $F_1, F_2, \dots$ be the fixed components of $X^T$. Let $N_i = \bigoplus_\alpha N_{i,\alpha}$ denote the normal bundle to $F_i$ in $X$, where $N_{i,\alpha}$ is the subbundle on which $T$ acts via the character $\alpha \in \Lambda^\vee$. Let $r_{i,\alpha,1}, r_{i,\alpha,2}, \dots$ denote the Chern roots of $N_{i,\alpha}$.

Let $\lambda \in \Lambda$, and $\beta_i=\beta_{\lambda,i} \in \Eff(\sE_{t^\lambda}(X))^{\sec}$ be the class of the section $\sE_{t^\lambda}(x)$ for a point $x \in F_i$. We define
\begin{equation*}
\Delta_i(\lambda)\coloneq q^{\overline{\beta_i}} 
\displaystyle\prod_{\alpha, j} 
\frac{
  \prod_{c = -\infty}^{0} (r_{i,\alpha,j} + \alpha + c\hbar)
}{
  \prod_{c = -\infty}^{-\alpha(\lambda)} (r_{i,\alpha,j} + \alpha + c\hbar)
}
\;\in\; H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(F_i)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]].
\end{equation*}
The \emph{shift operator on the Givental space}
\[
S_{t^\lambda} \colon H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]] 
\to H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]]
\]
is defined via the following commutative diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=10em]
H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]] \arrow[r, "S_{t^\lambda}"] \arrow[d]
  & H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]] \arrow[d] \\
H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^T)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]] \arrow[r, 
"{\phantom{ S_{t^\lambda}} \bigoplus_i \Phi_\lambda\circ \Delta_i(\lambda) }"]
  & H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^T)_{\loc}[[q_G,\tau]]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
Here, the vertical arrows are the natural restriction maps, and the bottom arrow uses the decomposition
\[
H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X^T)_{\loc} \cong \bigoplus_i \Frac(H^\bullet_{T \times \Cxh}(\pt)) \otimes H^\bullet(F_i),
\]
where $\Delta_i(\lambda)$ is interpreted as the operator of multiplication by $\Delta_i(\lambda)$ on the $i$-th summand.
\end{df}

\begin{lem}\label{intertwiningFundamentalsolution}
We have $\bbS_{t^\lambda} = \mathbb{M}_X S_{t^\lambda} \mathbb{M}_X^{-1}$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
This is essentially \cite[Theorem 3.14]{Iritani}. After addressing the differences in conventions for $0$ and $\infty$ (see~\Cref{CompareIritani2}), the argument in \emph{loc.~cit.} carries over to our setting upon replacing each occurrence of $\sigma_i - \sigma_{\min}$ with $\overline{\beta_i}$.
\end{proof}
\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{moduleprop}]
The second equality follows from \Cref{shiftoplinear}, so it suffices to prove the first. By \Cref{intertwiningFundamentalsolution}, it is enough to show that $S_{t^{\lambda+\mu}}(\alpha) = S_{t^\lambda}(S_{t^\mu}(\alpha))$. By localization, we may assume that $\alpha$ is the pushforward of a class in $H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(F_i)$. This reduces the problem to checking the identity
\[
\Phi_{\lambda+\mu}(\Delta_i(\lambda+\mu))=\Phi_\lambda(\Delta_i(\lambda))\cdot \Phi_{\lambda+\mu}(\Delta_i(\mu)),
\]
which follows from a direct computation.
\end{proof}


\begin{thm}\label{moduleproperty1}
The operator $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ defines an $\sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar$-module structure on the equivariant quantum cohomology ring $QH_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]$.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
Let $\Gamma=\sum_{\lambda} a_\lambda [t^\lambda]$ and $\Gamma' = \sum_{\mu} b_\mu [t^\mu]$, where $a_\mu,b_\mu\in H_{T\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$. By \Cref{TGcompatible} and \Cref{moduleprop}, we have
\begin{align*}
\bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma ,\bbS_{G,\bN} (\Gamma',\alpha)) & = \sum_{\lambda} a_\lambda \bbS_{t^\lambda}\left(\sum_\mu b_\mu \bbS_{t^\mu}(\alpha)\right) \\
&= \sum_{\lambda,\mu} a_\lambda \Phi_\lambda(b_\mu) \,\bbS_{t^{\lambda+\mu}}(\alpha) \\
&= \bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma\ast \Gamma',\alpha).
\end{align*}
Here, we used the formula
\[[t^\lambda]\ast b_\mu = \Phi_\lambda(b_\mu)\ast [t^\lambda],\]
which is twisted linearity of convolution product \eqref{convolution2} (see \cite[Section 4(ii)]{BFN}).

Together with \Cref{1=1}, the theorem follows.
\end{proof}


\subsubsection*{More properties}

We now collect other properties of $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ that follow from the localization result of \Cref{TGcompatible}.

\begin{prop}\label{CommuteConnection}
    For any $\Gamma\in \sA^\hbar_{G,\bN}$, the shift operator $\bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma,-)$ commutes with the quantum connections (see \Cref{def:quantum_connection}), i.e.,
    \[[\nabla_{\tau^{i,j}},\bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma,-)]=0,\quad [\nabla_{\hbar\partial_\hbar},\bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma,-)]=0,\quad [\nabla_{Dq\partial_q}, \bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma,-)]=0.\] 
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
By localization, it suffices to consider the case where $G = T$ is abelian. By \eqref{qconnintertwine} and \Cref{intertwiningFundamentalsolution}, the claim reduces to showing that $S_{t^\lambda}$ commutes with $\partial_{\tau^{i,j}}$, $\hbar \partial_\hbar - \hbar^{-1}(c_1^T(X) \cup) + \mu_X$, and $Dq \partial_q + \hbar^{-1}(D \cup)$, which follows easily from a direct computation (cf.\ \cite[Corollary 2.11]{Iritaniblowup}).
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}\label{propA}
There is a commutative diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
\sA_{T,\bN}^\hbar \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_T,\tau]] \arrow[d] \arrow[r, "{\bbS_{T,\bN}}"] 
  & H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_T,\tau]] \arrow[d] \\
e(\BS_\bN) \cap H_\bullet^{T_\oh \rtimes \Cxh}(\Gr_G) \otimes_{\C[\hbar]} H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]] \arrow[r, "{\bbS_{G,\bN}}"] 
  & H_{T \times \Cxh}^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
This proposition asserts that $\bbS_{G,\bN} = \bbS_{T,\bN} \otimes_{\C[[q_T,\tau]]} \C[[q_G,\tau]]$,
when restricted to the common domain of definition. This follows directly from \Cref{TGcompatible}, applied both for $G$ and for $T$.
\end{proof}

By specializing $\hbar=0$, we also obtain properties for Seidel representation and Seidel homomorphisms. 

\begin{cor}\label{compatqprod}
The map $\bbS_{G,\bN}^{\hbar = 0}$ is compatible with the quantum product, in the sense that
\begin{equation*}
\bbS^{\hbar = 0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma, \alpha_1 \star_{\tau} \alpha_2) = \bbS^{\hbar = 0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma, \alpha_1) \star_{\tau} \alpha_2.
\end{equation*}
In particular,
\begin{equation*}
\bbS^{\hbar = 0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma, \alpha) = \Psi_{G,\bN}(\Gamma) \star_{\tau} \alpha.
\end{equation*}
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
This follows from \Cref{CommuteConnection}. Setting $\hbar=0$ in the equality $[\hbar\nabla_{\tau^{i,j}}, \bbS_{G,\bN}(\Gamma,-)]=0$ yields the desired result.
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}\label{equivseidelmap}
The map $\Psi_{G,\bN}$ is a ring homomorphism.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
This follows directly from \Cref{moduleproperty1} and \Cref{compatqprod}:
\begin{equation*}
\bbS^{\hbar = 0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma \ast \Gamma', 1)
= \bbS^{\hbar = 0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma, \bbS^{\hbar = 0}_{G,\bN}(\Gamma', 1))
= \Psi_{G,\bN}(\Gamma) \star_{\tau} \Psi_{G,\bN}(\Gamma'). \qedhere
\end{equation*}
\end{proof}

For $a, b \in \sA_{G,\bN}$, choose lifts $\widetilde{a}, \widetilde{b} \in \sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar$. There is a Poisson algebra structure on $\sA_{G,\bN}$ given by the bracket
\begin{equation*}
\{a, b\} = \frac{1}{\hbar}(\widetilde{a} \widetilde{b} - \widetilde{b} \widetilde{a}) \mod \hbar.
\end{equation*}
It is known that this Poisson bracket induces a symplectic structure on the smooth locus of $\spec \sA_{G,\bN}$ (see \cite[Proposition 6.15]{BFN}).

\begin{df} 
A closed subscheme $Z \subset \spec \sA_{G,\bN}$ is said to be \emph{coisotropic} if the radical of its defining ideal is a Lie subalgebra of $\sA_{G,\bN}$. A coisotropic subscheme $Z \subset \spec \sA_{G,\bN}$ is said to be \emph{Lagrangian} if $\dim Z = \frac{1}{2} \dim \spec \sA_{G,\bN} = \dim T$.
\end{df}

Note that these definition agree with the classical notions of coisotropic and Lagrangian subvarieties in the smooth case (see \cite[Proposition 1.5.1]{ChrissGinzburg}).
\begin{prop}\label{Lagrangian property}
The subscheme $V(\ker \Psi_{G,\bN,X}) \subset \spec \sA_{G,\bN}$ is coisotropic. Moreover, if all infinite sums involved in the quantum product and shift operators converge upon evaluation at a homomorphism
\begin{equation*}
q_0 \colon \C[q_G,\tau] \to \C,
\end{equation*}
and if we denote by
\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{G,\bN,X}^{q_0} \colon \sA_{G,\bN} \to QH_{G}^\bullet(X)
\end{equation*}
the resulting specialization, then $V(\ker \Psi^{q_0}_{G,\bN,X})$ is Lagrangian.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
This follows from \cite[Theorem I]{Gabber} (see also \cite[page 56]{ChrissGinzburg}).
\end{proof}



\subsection{Properties of shift operators: the general case} In this subsection, we show that the results in the previous subsection hold without the $T$-compact assumption.

Set $\hG=G\times \Cxd$, where $\Cxd$ is the group of conical action (see \Cref{preparation}).
\begin{lem}\label{existrepresentation}
There exists a $\hG$-representation $\bV$ and a $\widehat{G}$-equivariant proper morphism $g \colon X \to \bV$.
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\bV^\vee$ be a finite-dimensional $\hG$-invariant subspace of $H^0(X, \oh_X)$ such that the corresponding map $\sym^\bullet\bV^\vee \to H^0(X, \oh_X)$ is surjective. In other words, the induced morphism $\spec H^0(X, \oh_X) \to \bV$ is a closed embedding. The composition
\[
X \to \spec H^0(X, \oh_X) \to \bV
\]
is clearly $\hG$-equivariant and proper, as required.
\end{proof}

\begin{thm}\label{thmgeneral}
\Cref{TGcompatible}, \Cref{Wequiv}, \Cref{moduleproperty1}, \Cref{CommuteConnection}, and \Cref{propA} all hold without the $T$-compactness assumption.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
Let $\bV$ and $g \colon X \to \bV$ be as in \Cref{existrepresentation}. All the stated properties have been established for $\bbS_{\hG, \bV, X}$ in the last subsection. By \Cref{qham}, these properties also hold for $\bbS_{G, \bV, X}$.

Now, by \eqref{independentofN}, $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$ and $\bbS_{G,\bV,X,\loc}$ agree on $\sA^\hbar_{G,\bN}\subset \sA^\hbar_{G,\bV,\loc}$. Therefore, the desired properties of $\bbS_{G, \bN, X}$ follows from the corresponding properties of $\bbS_{G,\bV,X,\loc}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}
\Cref{compatqprod}, \Cref{equivseidelmap} and \Cref{Lagrangian property} also hold without the $T$-compactness assumption.
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
The same arguments as in the proofs of \Cref{compatqprod}, \Cref{equivseidelmap} and \Cref{Lagrangian property} apply verbatim.
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}
\Cref{IntroThm2} and \Cref{IntroThm1} are valid.
\end{cor}

\begin{rem}\label{CompareIritaniRemark}
    Suppose $X^T$-compact, Iritani in \cite{Iritani} defined shift operators for the $T$-action on $X$. By the proof of \Cref{existrepresentation}, there always exists a $T$-representaion $\bN$ with a equivariant proper map $f:X\to \bN$. Therefore, the shift operators $\bbS_{T,\bN}$ defined as in \Cref{bbSNformula} recovers Iritani's definition, up to adjoint and Novikov variables (see \Cref{IritaniRemark}).
\end{rem}

The following corollary follows from \Cref{IntroThm2} immediately.
\begin{cor}\label{Non-equivariant}
    There are linear maps
    \begin{align*}
        S_{G,\bN,X} &\colon\sA_{G,\bN}^\hbar\otimes QH^\bullet(X) \longrightarrow QH^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]][\hbar], \\
        S_{G,\bN,X}^{\hbar=0} &\colon\sA_{G,\bN}\otimes QH^\bullet(X)\longrightarrow QH^\bullet(X)[[q_G,\tau]] .
\end{align*}
    obtained by specializing the equivariant parameters $H_G^\bullet(\pt)$ to zero. Moreover, $S_{G,\bN,X}^{\hbar=0}$ defines a module action.
\end{cor}
Note that $\C^\times_\hbar$ acts on $X$ trivially, so it makes sense to refer to $S_{G,\bN,X}$ and $S_{G,\bN,X}^\hbar$ as the non-equivariant limits of the shift operator $\bbS_{G,\bN,X}$. See \Cref{Non-equivariantExample} for a sample calculation.


\subsection{Product formula}
Recall that for a product variety, its quantum cohomology satisfies a Künneth formula \cite{Behrend}. The equivariant version also holds: if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are smooth semiprojective $G$-varieties, then
\begin{equation}\label{QHkunneth}
QH^\bullet_{G\times\Cxh}(X_1\times X_2)[[q_{G\times G},\tau_{G\times G}]]\cong QH^\bullet_{G\times\Cxh}(X_1)[[q_G,\tau_G]]\otimes_{H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)} QH_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X_2)[[q_G,\tau_G]].
\end{equation}
Here, $\tau_{G\times G}$ is the bulk parameters associated to the $G\times G$-action on $X_1\times X_2$. The left-hand side is defined to be the $G\times G\times\Cxh$-equivariant quantum cohomology under specialization of equivariant parameters along $\Delta G\subset G\times G$. 


This compatibility reflects a similar structure on the level of Coulomb branches. Indeed, the diagonal map
\[\Delta:\Gr_G\longhookrightarrow \Gr_G\times\Gr_G\]
induces a pushforward homomorphism
\begin{equation}\label{Delta}
    \Delta_*: H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G)\;\longrightarrow\; H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G) \otimes_{H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)}H_\bullet^{G_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}(\Gr_G).
\end{equation}
Setting $\hbar=0$, the spectrum of this map gives the flat $\spec H_G^\bullet(\pt)$-group scheme structure on $\spec \sA_{G,0}$, realized as the regular centralizer of the Langlands dual group $\widecheck{G}$ (\cite{BFM}).

Moreover, if $\bN_1$ and $\bN_2$ are two representations of $G$, it is clear that $\Delta^{-1} (\BS_{G, \bN_1}\boxtimes \BS_{G, \bN_2}) \cong \BS_{G, \bN_1 \oplus \bN_2}$. Therefore, \eqref{Delta} restricts to give a homomorphism (denoted by the same symbol)
\begin{equation*}
    \Delta_{*}: \;\sA^{\hbar}_{G, \bN_1 \oplus \bN_2}\;\longrightarrow\;
    \sA^\hbar_{G, \bN_1} \otimes_{H_{G \times \Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)} \sA^\hbar_{G, \bN_2}.
\end{equation*}
In particular, for each representation $\bN$, there is a group scheme action of $\spec \sA_G$ on $\spec \sA_{G, \bN}$.

\begin{prop}\label{kunneth}
Let $f_i : X_i \to \bN_i$ be a $G$-equivariant proper morphism for $i = 1, 2$, satisfying the assumptions of \Cref{sectionshiftoperators}. Then we have
\begin{equation}\label{Kunnetheq}
\bbS_{G,\bN_1\oplus\bN_2}(\Gamma\otimes \gamma_1\otimes \gamma_2)=(\bbS_{G,\bN_1}\otimes \bbS_{G,\bN_2}) (\Delta_*(\Gamma)\otimes \gamma_1\otimes\gamma_2),
\end{equation}
for any $\Gamma\in \sA^\hbar_{G,\bN_1\oplus\bN_2}$, any $\gamma_i \in H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(X_i)[[q_G,\tau_G]]$, for $i=1,2$. Here the tensor product between the $\gamma_i$'s on the left-hand side is taken over $H_{G\times\Cxh}^\bullet(\pt)$, and on the right-hand side taken over $\C$. We have used the identification given by Künneth formula \eqref{QHkunneth}.


\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Using the same reasoning as in \Cref{thmgeneral}, we only need to prove the $G = T$ case.

To simplify notation, we write $a^\lambda \vcentcolon= e(\BS_{\bN_1, t^\lambda}) \cap [t^\lambda]$, $b^\lambda \vcentcolon= e(\BS_{\bN_2, t^\lambda}) \cap [t^\lambda]$ for $\lambda \in \Lambda$, and $c^\lambda \vcentcolon= e(\BS_{\bN_1 \oplus \bN_2, t^\lambda}) \cap [t^\lambda]$. Then we have
\begin{equation*}
\Delta_{*}(c^\lambda) = a^\lambda \otimes b^\lambda.
\end{equation*}

For any $\gamma_1 \in H_{T \times \Cx_{\hbar}}^\bullet(X_1)[[q_T,\tau]]$ and $\gamma_2 \in H_{T \times \Cx_{\hbar}}^\bullet(X_2)[[q_T,\tau]]$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{prod1}
(\bbS_{T, X_1} \otimes \bbS_{T, X_2}) \circ (\Delta_{*}(c^\lambda) \otimes \gamma_1 \otimes \gamma_2)
= \bbS_{T, X_1}(a^\lambda \otimes \gamma_1) \otimes \bbS_{T, X_2}(b^\lambda \otimes \gamma_2).
\end{equation}

On the other hand, we have
\begin{align}\label{prod2}
\bbS_{T \times T, X_1 \times X_2}((a^{\lambda} \otimes b^{\lambda}) \otimes (\gamma_1 \otimes \gamma_2))  
&= \bbS_{T \times T, X_1 \times X_2}((a^{\lambda} \otimes 1) \otimes \bbS_{T \times T, X_1 \times X_2}((1 \otimes b^{\lambda}), \gamma_1 \otimes \gamma_2)) \nonumber \\
&= \bbS_{T \times T, X_1 \times X_2}((a^{\lambda} \otimes 1) \otimes \gamma_1 \otimes \bbS_{T, X_2}(b^{\lambda}, \gamma_2)) \nonumber \\
&= \bbS_{T, X_1}(a^{\lambda} \otimes \gamma_1) \otimes \bbS_{T, X_2}(b^{\lambda} \otimes \gamma_2).
\end{align}
Here, the first equality follows from \Cref{moduleproperty1}; the second equality follows from the fact that 
$\sE^{G \times G}_{t^{(0,\lambda)}}(X_1 \times X_2) \cong X_1 \times \sE_{t^\lambda}(X_2)$, 
and so only sections that are trivial in the $X_1$-direction contribute (see \Cref{1=1} for a similar argument). The third equality holds for similar reasons. Now \eqref{prod1} and \eqref{prod2} agree. Moreover, $\bbS_{T \times T, X_1 \times X_2}((a^{\lambda} \otimes b^{\lambda}) \otimes (\gamma_1 \otimes \gamma_2))$ is equal to the right-hand side of \eqref{Kunnetheq} after specialization of equivariant parameters along $\Delta T\subset T\times T$. This completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications/calculations}\label{Applications}
\subsection{Examples}
To illustrate the rationality of $\Psi_{T,\bN}$, we compute from definition \eqref{bbSNformula} the map $\Psi_{T,\bN}$ for $X=\bN$.
\begin{prop}\label{abeliancase}
Let $G=T=(\Cx)^k$ and $X=\bN= \bigoplus_{j=1}^n\C_{\xi_j}$, then
\[\Psi_{T,\bN}(e(\BS_{t^\lambda})\cap [t^\lambda])= q^{\lambda}\prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)>0} \xi_j^{\xi_j(\lambda)}, \text{ and}\]
\begin{align}\label{abelianformula}
\Psi_{T,\bN,\loc}([t^\lambda])= q^{\lambda}\frac{\prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)>0}\xi_j^{\xi_j(\lambda)}}{\prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)<0}\xi_j^{-\xi_j(\lambda)}}.
\end{align}
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Let $[t^\lambda]\in \Gr_T$, by \Cref{CompareIritani2}, the associated Seidel space is given by
\[ \sE_{t^\lambda}(\bN)\cong \bigoplus_{j=1}^n \oh_{\PP^1}(-\xi_j(\lambda)).\]
It is straightforward to verify that the only contribution comes from $2$-points invariants. The associated moduli space of sections, with marked points on the fibres over $0$ and $\infty$, is
\[\cM_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0= H^0\left(\PP^1,\sE_{t^\lambda}(\bN)\right)=\bigoplus_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)\leq0} \C_{\xi_j}^{-\xi_j(\lambda)+1},\]
and its virtual fundamental class is
\[[\cM_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0]^\vir = e(H^1(\PP^1,\sE_{t^\lambda}(\bN))\cap  [\cM_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0]=\prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)>0}\xi_j^{\xi_j(\lambda)-1}\cap [\cM_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0].\]
By the proof of \Cref{propermoduli} in the special case $X=\bN$, we have
\[\cZ_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0\cong \prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)\leq0} \C_{\xi_j}.\]
The inclusion $\cZ_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0\hookrightarrow\cM_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0$ is a regular embedding. Consequently, 
\[[\cZ_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0]^\vir=\prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)>0}\xi_j^{\xi_j(\lambda)-1}\cap [\cZ_{t^\lambda}(\bN)_0].\]
Finally, the evaluation map $\ev_\infty:\cZ_{t^\lambda}(\bN)\to \bN$ is simply the inclusion. Thus, we obtain
\[
\Psi_{T,\bN}(e(\BS_{t^\lambda})\cap [t^\lambda])=q^{\lambda}\prod_{j:\xi_j(\lambda)>0} \xi_j^{\xi_j(\lambda)} \in H_T^\bullet(\bN)[q_T][[\tau]],
\]
as desired. By \eqref{Nweights}, we obtain \eqref{abelianformula}. 
\end{proof}

Next, let us consider a slightly more sophisticated example. This demonstrates the existence of non-equivariant limits as in \Cref{Non-equivariant}. As we will see, the statement of \Cref{Non-equivariant} cannot possibly hold without the introduction of $G$-equivariant Novikov variables.

\begin{exmp}\label{Non-equivariantExample}
Let $G = \operatorname{GL}_2$ act on $\PP^1$ by fractional linear transformations, and consider the induced action on $X = T^*\PP^1$. Let $T = (\C^\times)^2 \subset \operatorname{GL}(2,\C)$ denote the subgroup of diagonal matrices. The cohomology of $T^*\PP^1$ admits the following presentation
\[
H^\bullet_{(\C^\times)^2 \times \C^\times_\hbar}(T^*\PP^1) = \frac{\C[x, a_1, a_2, \hbar]}{\langle(x + a_1)(x + a_2)\rangle},
\]
where $x$ is the pullback of $c_1(\mathcal O_{\PP^1}(1))$ along $T^*\PP^1\to \PP^1$, and $a_i$ are the equivariant parameters of $T$.

Let us denote $q_{T,\lambda}^0$ (resp. $q_{T,\lambda}^\infty$) to be the $T$-equivariant Novikov variables associated to the constant section at $0$ (resp. $\infty$) of the Seidel space $\sE_{t^\lambda}(X)$. One can compute that
\begin{align*}
\bbS_{t^{(1,0)}}(1) &= \frac{a_1 - a_2 + \hbar}{(a_1 - a_2)^2} \left(q^0_{T,(1,0)}(x+a_2) - q^\infty_{T,(1,0)}(x+a_1)\right),  \\
\bbS_{t^{(0,1)}}(1) &= \frac{a_2 - a_1 + \hbar}{(a_1 - a_2)^2} \left(q_{T,(0,1)}^\infty (x+a_1) - q^0_{T,(0,1)}(x+a_2)\right).
\end{align*}

Notice that there exists a $G$-equivariant proper morphism $T^*\PP^1 \to \mathfrak{gl}_2$. For simplicity, let us denote the fixed point classes $z_1=[t^{(1,0)}]$ and $z_2=[t^{(0,1)}]$. In this case, a class $p(a_1, a_2)z_1 \in \sA^\hbar_{(\Cx)^2, \mathfrak{gl}_2}$ if and only if $p$ is divisible by $(a_1 - a_2)^2$. On the other hand, we find
\begin{align*}
S_{(\Cx)^2, \mathfrak{gl}_2, T^*\PP^1}((a_1 - a_2)^2 z_1 \otimes 1) &= (q^0_{T,(1,0)} - q^\infty_{T,(1,0)})\hbar x, \\
S_{(\Cx)^2, \mathfrak{gl}_2, T^*\PP^1}((a_1 - a_2)^2 z_2 \otimes 1) &= (q^\infty_{T,(0,1)} - q^0_{T,(0,1)})\hbar x.
\end{align*}

It is more interesting to consider the non-abelian shift operators. Consider the class
\[
e(\BS) \cap [C_{\leq (1,0)}] = (a_1 - a_2)\left(z_1-z_2\right) \in \sA^\hbar_{\operatorname{GL_2},\mathfrak{gl}_2}.
\]
Let $\eta:\C[q_T]\to\C[q_G]$ be the base change map, then the relevant $G$-equivariant Novikov variables are identified as follows
\[q_G \coloneq \eta(q_{T,(1,0)}^0)=\eta(q_{T,(0,1)}^\infty),\quad q_{G}'\coloneq \eta(q^\infty_{T,(0,1)})=\eta(q^0_{T,(0,1)}).\]

Then, we find
\[
\bbS_{\operatorname{GL_2},\mathfrak{gl}_2,T^*\PP^1}(e(\BS)\cap [C_{\leq(1,0)}]\otimes 1)= (q_G-q_G')(2x+a_1+a_2) - (q_G+q_G')\hbar.
\]
Note that $2x+a_1+a_2$ is the negative of equivariant Euler class of $T^*\PP^1$. So,
\[
S_{\operatorname{GL}_2, \mathfrak{gl}_2, T^*\PP^1}(e(\BS) \cap [C_{\leq (1,0)}] \otimes 1) = -(q_G - q_G')e(T^*\PP^1) -(q_G+q_G')\hbar.
\]
In particular, we have 
\[
S^{\hbar=0}_{\operatorname{GL}_2, \mathfrak{gl}_2, T^*\PP^1}(e(\BS) \cap [C_{\leq (1,0)}] \otimes 1) = -(q_G - q_G')e(T^*\PP^1).
\]
Note that the expression admits a non-equivariant limit only after identifying the $q_T$ variables via $\eta$. Without this identification, the cancellation needed for existence of the limit does not occur. \qed
\end{exmp}

\begin{exmp}
It is known that for adjoint matter $\mathfrak{g}$, the Coulomb branch $\sA_{G,\mathfrak{g}}$ is isomorphic to $\C[T^* \widecheck{T}]^W$ (see~\cite[Section~6(vi)]{BFN}). Continuing from \Cref{Non-equivariantExample}, we may compute explicitly the Seidel map
\[
\Psi_{\operatorname{GL}_2, \mathfrak{gl}_2, T^*\PP^1} \colon \C[T^*(\C^\times)^2]^{\Z_2} \longrightarrow QH_{\operatorname{GL}_2}^\bullet(T^*\PP^1)[[q_G]].
\]
It sends
\begin{alignat*}{3}
    z_1 z_2 \ &\mapsto\   q_G q_G', \qquad &
    z_1^{-1} z_2^{-1} \ &\mapsto\   q_G^{-1} q_G'^{-1}, \\
    z_1 + z_2 \ &\mapsto\   -q_G - q_G', \qquad &
    (a_1 - a_2)(z_1 - z_2) \ &\mapsto\   -(q_G - q_G') \, e^{\operatorname{GL}_2}(T^*\PP^1). 
\end{alignat*}
\qed
\end{exmp}


\subsection{A new characterization of the Coulomb branch}\label{sectiontelemanglue}
In this section, we characterize the Coulomb branch algebra as the largest subalgebra of the pure gauge Coulomb branch algebra for which the shift operators are defined before localization. First, let us introduce the following definition, which appeared in~\cite{3dmirror} and~\cite{functoriality}.

\begin{df}
The $G$-representation $\bN$ is called \textit{gluable} if for all nonzero $T$-weights $\xi_1,\xi_2$, we have $\xi_1$ is not a negative multiple of $\xi_2$.
\end{df}

In particular, the $G\times\Cx_{\mathrm{dil}}$-representation, where $\Cx_{\mathrm{dil}}$ acts on $\bN$ via scaling, is gluable.

\begin{thm}[=\Cref{Introthm4}]\label{Telemangluing}
Let $\bN$ be a gluable $G$-representation. Then the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}\label{gluingcd}
\begin{tikzcd}
\sA_{G,\bN} \arrow[r, "\Delta_*"] \arrow[d]
  & \sA_{G} \otimes_{H^\bullet_{G}(\pt)} \sA_{G,\bN}
    \arrow[rr, "{\id\otimes \Psi_{G,\bN}} "] 
    && \sA_G \otimes_{H^\bullet_{G}(\pt)} QH^\bullet_{G}(X)[[q_G,\tau]]\arrow[d] \\
\sA_{G} \arrow[r, "\Delta_*"]
  & \sA_{G} \otimes_{H^\bullet_{G}(\pt)} \sA_{G}
    \arrow[rr, "{\id\otimes \Psi_{G,\bN,\loc}}"]
    && \sA_G\otimes_{H^\bullet_{G}(\pt)} QH^\bullet_{G}(X)_\loc [[q_G,\tau]] .
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Moreover, if $\bN$ is gluable and $X = \bN$, then the outer square is Cartesian.
\end{thm}
We remark that the last statement of \Cref{Telemangluing} recovers Teleman's result that the Coulomb branch $\operatorname{Spec} \sA_{G \times \C^\times_\hbar, \bN}$ is the affinization of the scheme obtained by gluing two copies of $\operatorname{Spec} \sA_{G \times \C^\times_\hbar}$ along a rational map.

\begin{proof}
The first assertion follows directly from the construction. 

For the second assertion, it suffices to prove the claim for the $T_\oh$-equivariant version of the diagram. The $G_\oh$-equivariant case then follows by taking $W$-invariant parts of the homomorphisms.

We begin by recalling that the Coulomb branch algebra admits a natural filtration (see \Cref{stableprod}):
\[e(\BS_{\bN})\cap H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)=\bigcup_{\lambda\in \Lambda}\im \left(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq\lambda})\cap H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\widetilde{C}_{\leq\lambda})\right).\]
The diagram \eqref{gluingcd} consists of homomorphisms of filtered $H_{T}^\bullet(\pt)$-algebras. To show that it is Cartesian, it suffices to verify this on each filtered piece.

For clarity, denote the entries in the $T_\oh$-eqvuiariant version of \eqref{gluingcd} as follows: let $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ correspond to the top-left, top-right, bottom-left and bottom-right entries, respectively. Define the pullback $P\vcentcolon=B\times_D C=\ker(B\oplus C\to D)$ in the category of filtered $H_{T}^\bullet(\pt)$-modules.

Since both $B\to D$, $C\to D$ and $A\to C$ are all injective, the induced map $A\to P$ is injective. So, it suffices to show that $A\to P$ is surjective. By induction on $\lambda$, we may reduce to showing that the map on associated graded, $\gr A\to \gr P$, is surjective. 

Recall that the filtration on $A$ is given by
\[F^\lambda A= H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{\leq\lambda}),\quad F^{<\lambda}A=H^{T_\oh\rtimes\Cxh}_\bullet(\BR^d_{<\lambda}).\]
Let $e\in H_{T}^\bullet(\pt)$ be the Euler class of the tangent space of $C_\lambda$ at $[t^\lambda]$. The natural map $F^{<\lambda}A\to F^\lambda A$ is the pushforward map associated to the closed subset $\BR^d_{<\lambda}\to \BR^d_{\leq\lambda}$. The long exact sequence in Borel--Moore homology yields an isomorphism
\[\gr^\lambda A\stackrel{\sim}{\longrightarrow} H_\bullet^{T_\oh}(\BR^d_\lambda)\cong H_T^\bullet(\pt) \cdot e(\BS_{t^\lambda})\cdot\frac{1}{e}[t^\lambda].\]

On the associated graded level, the bottom horizontal arrow in the diagram sends
\[[t^\lambda]\longmapsto [t^\lambda]\otimes [t^\lambda]\longmapsto [t^\lambda]\otimes \Psi_T([t^\lambda]).\]
Meanwhile, for the pullback $P$, 
\[\gr^\lambda P \subset \gr^\lambda H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G) \cong H_T^\bullet(\pt)\cdot \frac 1e[t^\lambda].\]
Suppose that $c[t^\lambda]\in \gr^\lambda P$. By definition of pullback, we must have 
\[ce\Psi_{T,\bN,\loc}([t^\lambda])\in H_T^\bullet(\bN)[[q_T,\tau]].\]
When $\bN$ is gluable, by \Cref{abeliancase}, the denominator of $\Psi_{T,\bN,\loc}([t^\lambda])$ is exactly $e(\BS_{t^\lambda})$, and no cancellation of equivariant parameters occurs. Thus, $ce$ is divisible by $e(\BS_{t^\lambda})$.

Hence, $c[t^\lambda]$ lies in the image of $\gr^\lambda A$, and the map $\gr^\lambda A\to \gr^\lambda P$ is surjective. This completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Peterson isomorphism for reductive groups}\label{Peterson}

In this subsection, we set $\tau=0$ and consider only the small quantum cohomology.

We compute the homomorphism $\Psi_{G,X} = \Psi_{G,\mathbf{0},X}$ in the case where $X$ is a partial flag variety. The main result is \Cref{PetersonIso}, which generalizes the Peterson isomorphism for simply connected groups~\cite{peterson,Lam-Shimozono,Chow}.

The ideas for the computations presented here are all from~\cite{Chow}, and we do not claim originality for it. The only new input here is the use of equivariant Novikov parameters. We hope that \Cref{PetersonIso} offers compelling evidence that working with equivariant Novikov variables is the natural setting for studying quantum cohomology.



We begin by introducing some notations. Let $R$ be the set of roots of $G$, and let $R^+ \subset R$ be the set of positive roots. Let $P \supset B$ be a standard parabolic subgroup of $G$, and let $R_P \subset R$ be the set of roots of the Levi subgroup of $P$, so that $R_P^+ = R_P \cap R^+$. In particular, we have the decomposition
\[
\operatorname{Lie} P = \operatorname{Lie} B \oplus \bigoplus_{\alpha \in R_P^+} \mathfrak{g}_{-\alpha}.
\]
We denote the Weyl group of $P$ by $W_P$. Throughout this section, we regard the quantum cohomology vector space as
\[
QH^\bullet_G(G/P)[q_G] = H^\bullet_G(G/P) \otimes \mathbb{C}[H_2^{\text{ord},G}(G/P, \mathbb{Z})],
\]
and we will show that the image of $\Psi_{G,G/P}$ is actually contained in this space.

Let $X = G/P$ and $v \in W/W_P$. Let $\ell_P(v)$ denote the length of the minimal representative of $v$ in $W$, respectively. Define
\begin{align*}
    \sigma(v) = \overline{BvP} \subset G/P, \quad \sigma^-(v) = \overline{B^-vP} \subset G/P.
\end{align*}
Then $\dim(\sigma(v)) = \ell_P(v)$ and $\dim(\sigma^-(v)) = \dim G/P - \ell_P(v)$.

In \Cref{Convexity}, we will show that $\mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta)$ is smooth of expected dimension for any $\beta \in \Eff(\mathcal{E}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P))^{\mathrm{sec}}$. Moreover, since $\ev_\infty: \mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta) \to G/P$ is $B$-equivariant, it is transverse to all $B^-$-orbits. Therefore,
\begin{equation}\label{sumofintegrations}
    \Psi_{G,G/P}([C_{\leq \lambda}]) = \sum_{\beta} q^{\overline{\beta}} \sum_{v \in W/W_P} \#\left(\mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P,\beta) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v)\right) \cdot \sigma(v),
\end{equation}
where $\#(S)$ for a set $S$ is $0$ if $S$ is infinite, and is the cardinality of $S$ otherwise.

Let $v \in W$, and consider the point $s_{\lambda,v} \in \mathcal{E}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P)$ representing the constant section
\[
\mathcal{E}^T_{t^\lambda} \times_T v \subset \mathcal{E}_\lambda \times_G (G/P),
\]
where $\mathcal{E}^T$ is the universal $T$-torsor over $\mathrm{Gr}_T \times \mathbb{P}^1$. Let $\beta_{\lambda,v} \in H_2^{\text{ord}}(\mathcal{E}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P);\Z)$ be the corresponding section class. We write $\lambda = w_\lambda(\lambda^-)$, where $\lambda^-$ is antidominant, and $w_\lambda$ is the longest-length element in the coset $w_\lambda \operatorname{Stab}_W(\lambda^-)$.

\begin{df}
   We say that $\lambda \in \Lambda$ is \emph{$P$-allowed} if for any $\alpha \in R_P^+$,
\[
\langle \alpha, \lambda^- \rangle = 
\begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } w_\lambda(\alpha) < 0, \\
    -1 & \text{if } w_\lambda(\alpha) > 0.
\end{cases}
\] 
\end{df}


\begin{lem}\label{zerodimcompo}
Let $\beta \in \Eff(\mathcal{E}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P))^{\mathrm{sec}}$, and let $v \in W$. Suppose 
\[
\mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v)
\]
is finite. Then $v \in w_\lambda W_P$, $\lambda$ is $P$-allowed, and
\[
\mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta_{\lambda,w_\lambda}) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(w_\lambda) = \{s_{\lambda, w_\lambda}\}.
\]
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
Let $s \in \mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v)$. Since the space is $T$-invariant, $s$ must be a $T$-fixed point, hence lying over some $t^\mu \in C_{\leq \lambda}$.

\textbf{1) Showing that $\mu = \lambda$:} Suppose $\mu < \lambda$. Then $s$ descends to give a point in
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{M}_{\leq\mu}(G/P, [s]) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v),
\end{equation*}
but
\begin{equation*}
\dim \mathcal{M}_{\leq\mu}(G/P, [s]) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v) 
< \dim \mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, [s]) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v) = 0,
\end{equation*}
a contradiction. Therefore, $\mu = \lambda$, and we must have $s = s_{\lambda,w}$ for some $w \in W$.

\textbf{2) Showing that $v \in w W_P$:} Since $\ev_\infty(s_{\lambda,w})=wP$, we have $wP \in \sigma^-(v)$. On the other hand, since $\ev_\infty$ is $B$-equivariant and $\sigma^-(v)$ is $B^-$-invariant, we must have $\sigma(w)\cap \sigma^-(v)=wP$, this shows that $v\in w W_P$.


\textbf{3) Showing that $v \in w_\lambda W_P$:} This condition is equivalent to requiring that for all $\alpha \in R^+ \setminus R_P^+$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle v(\alpha), \lambda \rangle \leq 0, \quad \text{and equality holds only if } v(\alpha) < 0.
\end{equation*}

Let $\alpha \in R^+ \setminus R_P^+$. Then the normal bundle to $s_{\lambda,v}$ in the direction of $-v(\alpha)$ is 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^1}(\langle v(\alpha), \lambda \rangle).
\end{equation*}
We must have $\langle v(\alpha), \lambda \rangle \leq 0$, because otherwise we can deform $s_{\lambda,v}$ while fixing the infinite point. 

Moreover, if $\langle v(\alpha), \lambda \rangle = 0$, then we can deform $s_{\lambda,v}$ while moving the infinite point in the $-v(\alpha)$ direction. We must have $v(\alpha) < 0$, otherwise this deformation remains contained in $\ev_\infty^{-1}(B^-(vP))$.

\textbf{4) Verifying that $\lambda$ is $P$-allowed:} We introduce some notations. For $\alpha\in R$, we write $U_\alpha:\C\to G$ the inclusion of the corresponding root subgroup. If $f\in \ok$, we also write $U_\alpha(f)$ for the corresponding element in $G_\ok$.

Let $\alpha \in R_P^+$. If $w_\lambda(\alpha) < 0$, then $U_{-w_\lambda(\alpha)}(z)t^\lambda \cdot w_\lambda=w_\lambda$, for any $z\in \C$, and $t\in\Cx$. Therefore, $s$ can deform to the constant section $s_z$ over $U_{-w_\lambda(\alpha)}(z)t^\lambda \in C_\lambda$ with value $w_\lambda$. This contradicts to our assumption that $\mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(v)$ is finite unless $U_{-w_\lambda(\alpha)}(z)t^\lambda=t^\lambda$ for any $z\in \C$. This means
\begin{equation*}
\langle \lambda^-, \alpha \rangle = \langle \lambda, w_\lambda(\alpha) \rangle \geq 0,
\end{equation*}
and must in fact equal $0$ because $\lambda^-$ is antidominant.

If $w_\lambda(\alpha) > 0$, then $U_{-w_\lambda(\alpha)}(zt)t^\lambda \cdot w_\lambda=w_\lambda$, for any $z\in \C$, and $t\in\Cx$. Similar to the above case, we must have
\begin{equation*}
\langle \lambda^-, \alpha \rangle = \langle \lambda, w_\lambda(\alpha) \rangle \geq -1.
\end{equation*}
Since $\lambda^-$ is antidominant, $\langle \lambda^-, \alpha \rangle$ can only be $0$ or $-1$. The former case, however, implies $w_\lambda(\alpha)<0$ by definition of $w_\lambda$. Therefore, we must have $\langle \lambda^-, \alpha \rangle=0$.

This confirms that $\lambda$ is $P$-allowed.
\end{proof}

We want to calculate the class $\overline{\beta_{\lambda,v}}\in H_2^{\text{ord},G}(G/P;\Z)=H^P_2(\pt;\Z)$. Let $EP$ be a contractible space with a free $P$-action, and $BP=EP/P$. The long exact sequence of homotopy groups associated to the fibration $P\to EP\to BP$ shows that $\pi_1(BP)=1$ and $\pi_2(P)\cong \pi_1(P)$. In particular, the Hurewicz theorem in algebraic topology implies $H_2^{\text{ord},P}(\pt;\Z)=H_2^{\text{ord}}(BP;\Z)\cong\pi_1(P)$.

We identify $\pi_1(T)$ with $\Lambda$; and for $\lambda\in\Lambda$, we write $\lambda_P$ for the image of $\lambda$ under the natural map $\pi_1(T)\to \pi_1(P)$. We write $\lambda^-_P$ for $(\lambda^-)_P$

\begin{lem}\label{Lemmacurveclass}
    $\overline{\beta_{\lambda,v}}=v^{-1}(\lambda)_P\in \pi_1(P)$.
\end{lem}


\begin{thm}\label{PetersonIso}
\[
\Psi_{G,G/P}([C_{\leq \lambda}]) =
\begin{cases}
    q^{\lambda_P^-} \sigma(w_\lambda) & \text{if } \lambda \text{ is } P\text{-allowed}, \\
    0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
In particular, the homomorphism
\[
\sA_G\to QH_G^\bullet(G/B)[q_G]
\]
becomes an isomorphism if we localize $\sA_G$ by all class $[C_{w_0(\lambda^-)}]$ for all $\lambda^-\in \Lambda^-$.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
The last statement follows from the first. To prove the first statement, we observe that
\begin{equation*}
    \dim \mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta_{\lambda,w_\lambda}) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(w_\lambda)=\dim C_\lambda + \dim G/P + \sum_{\alpha \in R^+ \setminus R_P^+} \langle\alpha,\lambda^-\rangle - \ell^-(w_\lambda),
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
    \dim C_\lambda=-\sum_{\alpha\in R^+}\langle\alpha,\lambda^-\rangle-|R^+|+\ell(w_\lambda).
\end{equation*}
If $\lambda$ is $P$-allowed, then 
\begin{equation*}
    \ell(w_\lambda)=\ell^-(w_\lambda)+|R_P^+|+\sum_{\alpha\in R_P^+}\langle\alpha,\lambda^-\rangle.
\end{equation*}
To see this, notice that $\ell(w_\lambda)$ (resp. $\ell_P(w_\lambda)$) is the number of $\alpha\in R^+$ (resp. $\alpha\in R^+\setminus R_P^+$) with $w_\lambda(\alpha)<0$, and $\sum_{\alpha\in R_P^+}\langle\alpha,\lambda\rangle$ is minus the number of $\alpha\in R^+_P$ with $w_\lambda(\alpha)>0$. Combining the above formula together, we see that $\dim \mathcal{M}_{\leq\lambda}(G/P, \beta_{\lambda,w_\lambda}) \times_{\ev_\infty} \sigma^-(w_\lambda)=0$ when $\lambda$ is $P$-allowed.

Now the theorem follows from \Cref{sumofintegrations}, \Cref{zerodimcompo}, and \Cref{Lemmacurveclass}.
\end{proof}


\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{Lemmacurveclass}]
Consider the diagram
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{tikzcd}
            \pi_2(\sE_{\leq\lambda}\times (G/P))\ar[r]&\pi_2(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P))\ar[r]\ar[d]&\pi_1(P)\ar[r,"1"]\ar[d,equal]&\pi_1(\sE_{\leq\lambda}\times (G/P))\\
            \pi_2(EP)\ar[r]&\pi_2(BP)\ar[r,"\cong"]&\pi_1(P)\ar[r]&\pi_1(EP),
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation*}
    where the rows are the long exact sequence associated to the fibrations $P\to \sE_{\leq\lambda}\to \sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P)$ and $P\to EP\to BP$ respectively. The vertical homomorphisms are induced by the classifying map $\sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P)\to BP$ corresponding to the principal $P$-bundle $\sE_{\leq\lambda}\to \sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P)$.
    By a diagram tracing, it remains to show that the boundary homomorphism $\pi_2(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P))\to \pi_1(P)$ sends $\beta_{\lambda,v}$ to $v^{-1}(\lambda)$.

    Let $\oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times$ be the $\Cx$ bundle associated to the tautological bundle of $\PP^1$. By checking the transition functions, it is easy to see that the restriction of $\sE_{\leq\lambda}$ to $\beta_{\lambda,v}$ is isomorphic to $\oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times\times_{v^{-1}(\lambda)} P$.

    This implies that there is a commutative diagram
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{tikzcd}
            \pi_2(\PP^1)\ar[r]\ar[d,equal]&\pi_1(\Cx)\ar[d]\\
            \pi_2(\PP^1)\ar[r]\ar[d]&\pi_1(P)\ar[d,equal]\\
            \pi_2(\sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P))\ar[r]&\pi_1(P),
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation*}
where the horizontal maps are boundary homomorphism associated to the fibrations $\Cx\to \oh_{\PP^1}^\times\to\PP^1$, $\Cx\to \oh_{\PP^1}(-1)^\times\times_{v^{-1}(\lambda)}P \to\PP^1$, and $P\to \sE_{\leq\lambda}\to \sE_{\leq\lambda}(G/P)$ respectively. Note that the left lower map sends $[\PP^1]$ to $\beta_{\lambda,v}$ and the right upper map sends the generator $1\in \Z\cong \pi_1(\Cx)$ to $v^{-1}(\lambda)_P$. Now the lemma follows from the convention that the upper boundary homomorphism sends $[\PP^1]$ to the generator $1\in \pi_1(\Cx)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{rem}
There is a ring homomorphism 
\[
\C[\Lambda]^W \cong \C[\Lambda^-] \longrightarrow \sA_G,
\]
which sends $q^{\lambda^-}$ to the class $[C_{\leq -w_0(\lambda^-)}]$. The induced morphism 
\[
\spec \sA_G \longrightarrow \spec \C[\Lambda]^W
\]
has fibres that are, set-theoretically, equal to the supports of $QH_G^\bullet(G/P)$ (after specializing $q_G$ accordingly). This gives a foliation of the Coulomb branch by the Lagrangians corresponding to partial flag varieties (cf.~\cite{Tel}). This statement can be proved using ideas similar to those in~\cite{Chow2} for simply connected $G$.

\end{rem}




\subsection{Peterson isomorphism with matters}\label{SectionPeterson}
In the case when $\bN=0$ and $X=G/P$ is a partial flag variety, by a theorem of \cite{Lam-Shimozono} and also \cite{Chow}, the Novikov variables $q^\beta$ lies in the image of the homomorphism $H_\bullet^{T_\oh}(\Gr_G)\to QH^\bullet_{T}(G/P)[q_G]$. In particular, this implies that for different $G/P$ and $G/P'$, or for $G/P$ equipped with different symplectic forms, the corresponding Lagrangians $\mathbb L_{G,\bN}(G/P)$ and $\mathbb L_{G,\bN}(G/P')$ have empty intersections. 

The analogous statement for general $\bN$ is not true. The Novikov variables $q^{\overline\beta}$ may not lie in the image of $e(\BS_\bN)\cap H^{T_\oh}_\bullet(\Gr_G)\to QH_T^\bullet(G/P\times\bN)[q_G]$. Nevertheless, \Cref{MatterPeterson} provides partial results, identifying a subset of quantum Schubert classes which do lie in the image of the Seidel map.

\begin{prop}\label{NNdual}
    Let $Y$ be a smooth projective $G$-variety, let $\bN$ be a $G$-representation and $\bN^\vee$ be its dual representation. For any $\lambda\in \Lambda$, we have 
    \begin{equation}\label{NNdualeq}
        \Psi_{G,\bN,Y\times\bN}(e({\BS}_{\bN})\cap C_{\leq\lambda})= \Psi_{G,\bN^\vee,Y}(e({\BS}_{\bN^\vee})\cap C_{\leq\lambda}),
    \end{equation}
    under the pullback isomorphism $H_G^\bullet(Y)\cong H_{G}^\bullet(Y\times\bN)$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    By \Cref{kunneth}, we reduce to the case \( Y = \pt \). By \Cref{qham}, we may replace \( G \) with \( G \times \Cx \), where \( \Cx \) acts on \( \bN \) via the scaling action. Now, we may apply \Cref{TGcompatible} to reduce to the abelian case, which follows from the calculation in \Cref{abeliancase}. 
\end{proof}

Now we set $\tau=0$ and only consider small quantum cohomology.
\begin{cor}\label{Matterpeterson2}
    The equivariant Seidel map $\Psi_{G,\bN,G/B\times\bN}$ induces a birational morphism $\spec QH_G^\bullet(G/B\times\bN)[q_G]\to \spec \sA_{G,\bN}$.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
    This follows from \eqref{NNdualeq} and the $\bN=0$ case (\Cref{PetersonIso}).
\end{proof}

The following proposition is a generalization of \Cref{Peterson}. The assumption is satisfied if $G=G'\times \Cxd$, where the $\Cxd$-factor acts on $\bN$ by scaling.

\begin{cor}\label{MatterPeterson}
Let $\rho:\C^\times \subset G$ be the inclusion of a central subgroup, and let $\bN$ be a representation whose $\C^\times$-weights are all positive (resp.\ negative). Let $X = G/P \times \bN$, and fix $\lambda \in \Lambda$. Then for sufficiently large $n > 0$, we have
\begin{align*}
\Psi_{G ,\, \bN,\, X}\bigl(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda - n\rho}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\lambda - n\rho}]\bigr) 
&= q^{(\lambda-n\rho)_P^-} \sigma(w_{\lambda}), \\
\text{resp.} \quad 
\Psi_{G,\, \bN,\, X}\bigl(e(\widetilde{\BS}_{\leq \lambda + n\rho}) \cap [\widetilde{C}_{\lambda + n\rho}]\bigr)
&= q^{(\lambda+n\rho)_P^-} \sigma(w_{\lambda}),
\end{align*}
under the pullback isomorphism $H_G^\bullet(G/P)\cong H_G^\bullet(G/P \times \bN)$.
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
    This follows from \Cref{PetersonIso} and \Cref{NNdual} applied to $Y=G/P$, along with the observation that $\BS_{\bN^\vee,\leq \lambda-n\rho}=0$ (resp. $\BS_{\bN^\vee,\leq \lambda+n\rho}=0$) for $n$ sufficiently large. 
\end{proof}

\begin{rem}
    For each specialization $\varsigma \colon \mathbb{C}[q_G] \to \mathbb{C}$, we obtain a Lagrangian subvariety
\[
\mathbb{L}_{\varsigma} = \mathrm{Supp}\left(QH_G^\bullet(G/B \times \bN) \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[q_G]} \mathbb{C}\right).
\]
For any two distinct specializations $\varsigma_1$ and $\varsigma_2$, it is easy to see that the corresponding Lagrangians $\mathbb{L}_{\varsigma_1}$ and $\mathbb{L}_{\varsigma_2}$ are disjoint. Therefore, the previous corollary may be viewed as a generalization of Teleman's result in the case $\bN = 0$ \cite[Theorem 6.8]{Tel}.
\end{rem}

