\subsection{Signal Analysis}

Obtaining the polarization from the Q-meter phase signal requires three steps. First the baseline, the background Q-curve that comes from the electronic response of the system, must be removed to leave only the portion of the signal from material polarization. To do this, a Q-curve response is recorded in conditions as close to experimental conditions as possible, but with the polarization removed. This is accomplished by shifting the magnetic field to move the Larmor frequency well out of the frequency sweep range. 

Next, any drift of the Q-curve away from the original baseline must be removed by fitting the portion of the baseline-subtracted signal outside the polarization peak, called the \textit{wings} of the signal, to a polynomial and subtracting that away. The final result should contain only the polarization signal sitting on a flat background for summation, such as seen in Figure \ref{fig:te} for an enhanced proton signal.

