In this Letter, we also employ the LLG equation, but we introduce the ``kick'' {\em microscopically} by self-consistently coupling LLG equation to computational quantum transport~\cite{Waintal2024} of photoexcited electrons. Such electrons are described by   
the time-dependent nonequilibrium Green's function (TDNEGF) formalism~\cite{Gaury2014,Popescu2016,stefanucci2025}, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig1}. Thus, TDNEGF+LLG framework~\cite{Petrovic2018,Bajpai2019a,Suresh2020,Petrovic2021,Suresh2021} makes it possible to introduce into the LLG equation, in {\em numerically exact} fashion, the effect of photocurrent~\cite{Bajpai2019} ignited by fsLP. The photocurrent will become spin-polarized as it propagates through the magnetic environment created  by LMMs. In addition, since LMMs within AF CAR in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig0} will be  noncollinear due to inevitable thermal fluctuations ~\cite{Suresh2023,Ghosh2022} or applied magnetic field in experiments~\cite{Bae2022,Diederich2022,Diederich2025} that we also include [Eq.~\eqref{eq:hamilc}], the nonequilibrium spin density 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:spin}
\langle \hat{\mathbf{s}}_{ia} \rangle (t) = \mathrm{Tr}_\mathrm{spin} [{\bm \rho}^{\rm neq}(t) {\bm \sigma}],  
\end{equation}
of photoexcited electrons will lead to nonzero spin-transfer torque (STT)~\cite{Ralph2008,Nikolic2018,Belashchenko2019} on the LMMs $\mathbf{M}_i$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stt}
\mathbf{T}_i (t) = J_{sd} \left[ \sum_{a=c,v} \langle \hat{\mathbf{s}}_{ia}  \rangle (t) \right] \times \mathbf{M}_i(t).
\end{equation}
The STT describes~\cite{Ralph2008,Nikolic2018,Belashchenko2019} how flowing electrons transfer spin angular momentum to local magnetization through $sd$ exchange interaction $J_{sd}$~\cite{Cooper1967}. Here $a=c,v$ labels each of two orbitals per site [Fig.~\ref{fig:fig0}];  \mbox{${\bm  \sigma} = (\hat{\sigma}_x,\hat{\sigma}_y,\hat{\sigma}_z)$} is the vector of the Pauli matrices;   ${\bm \rho}^{\rm neq}(t)$ is time-dependent nonequilibrium density matrix~\cite{Gaury2014,Popescu2016,Bajpai2020}; and trace 
$\mathrm{Tr}_\mathrm{spin} [\ldots]$ is over quantum states in the spin space only. Such microscopically computed STT is sent into the LLG equation,  
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:llg}
	\partial_t \mathbf{M}_i & = & -g_0 \mathbf{M}_i \times \mathbf{B}^\mathrm{eff}_i + \alpha_G \mathbf{M}_i \times \partial_t \mathbf{M}_i  + \frac{g_0}{\mu_M}\mathbf{T}_i,  
\end{eqnarray}
while dynamics of $\mathbf{M}_i(t)$ modifies the quantum Hamiltonian [Eq.~\eqref{eq:hamilq}] of electrons within the loop in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig1}. This then establishes self-consistency  between TDNEGF and LLG calculations, as initially 
photoexcited spin current, \mbox{$ I^{S_{\alpha}}  = \mathrm{Tr} [{\bm \rho}^{\rm neq}(t) \hat{I}^{S_{\alpha}}]$}, will be modified by the dynamics of $\mathbf{M}_i(t)$;   whereas their trajectories are, in turn, affected by updated spin current and STT $\mathbf{T}_i(t)$ exerted by it. In LLG Eq.~\eqref{eq:llg}  $g_0$ is gyromagnetic factor~\cite{Evans2014}; $\mu_M$ is the magnitude of LMMs~\cite{Evans2014}; \mbox{$\mathbf{B}^{\rm eff}_i = - \frac{1}{\mu_M} \partial \mathcal{H} /\partial \mathbf{M}_{i}$} is effective magnetic field obtained from {\em classical} Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}$  for LMMs~\cite{Scheie2022,Esteras2022};  Gilbert damping is chosen as $\alpha_G=0.01$ as the typical value for Cr-based 2D magnets~\cite{Esteras2022};  and we use shorthand notation $\partial_t \equiv \partial/\partial t$. 

