\section{Introduction}
The rough Heston model proposed by  \cite{el2019characteristic} is a one-dimensional stochastic volatility model in which the asset price $S$ has the following dynamic:
\[
   dS_t=S_t\sqrt{V_t}dW_t,
\]
\[
  V_t=V_0+\frac1{\Gamma(\alpha)}\int_0^t(t-s)^{\alpha-1}\gamma(\theta-V_s)ds+\frac1{\Gamma(\alpha)}\int_0^t(t-s)^{\alpha-1}\gamma\nu\sqrt{V_s}dB_s,
\]
where $\gamma,\theta,V_0$ and $\nu$ are positive constants, $W$ and $B$ are two standard Brownian motions with correlation $\rho$. The parameter $\alpha\in(1/2,1)$ governs the smoothness of the volatility sample path. It is a modified version of the celebrated Heston model proposed by  \cite{heston1993closed}, motivated from  empirical observations of roughness in the volatility time series, see  \cite{gatheral2018volatility}.  \cite{el2019characteristic} establish the link between nearly unstable Hawkes processes and fractional volatility models and obtain the characteristic function of the log-price in the rough Heston model, which can be expressed in terms of the solution of a fractional Riccati equation. Their work is based on the theoretical results of the scaling limit of nearly unstable Hawkes processes studied by \cite{jaisson2015limit,jaisson2016rough}.

Let us then introduce the integer-valued autoregressive (INAR($\infty$)) process. Let $\{\epsilon_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}$ be i.i.d. Poisson($\nu$) random variables. For $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, $k \ge 1$, $l \ge 1$, let $\xi_l^{(n,k)} \sim \text{Poisson}(\alpha_k)$ be Poisson random variables, independent of $\{\epsilon_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}$. An INAR($\infty$) process is an integer-valued time series model $\{X_n\}_{n \in \mathbb{Z}}$ that extends the traditional INAR($p$) processes to infinite order, satisfying the following system of stochastic difference equations:
\[
  \epsilon_n = X_n - \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{l=1}^{X_{n-k}} \xi_l^{(n,k)}, \quad n \in \mathbb{Z}.
\]
If we introduce the so-called \textit{reproduction operator} $``\circ"$, which is defined as 
\[
  \eta \circ Y := \sum_{n=1}^Y \xi_n^{(\eta)},
\]
for a random variable $Y$ that takes non-negative integer values and a constant $\eta \ge 0$, then the above expression can be rewritten as:
\[
  \epsilon_n = X_n - \sum_{k=1}^\infty \alpha_k \circ X_{n-k}.
\]
We refer to $\xi_n^{(\eta)}$ as the \textit{offspring variable} and $\left\{\xi_n^{(\eta)}\right\}_{n\geq 1}$ as the \textit{offspring sequence}. Additionally, we define $\nu$ as the \textit{immigration parameter}, $\left\{\epsilon_n\right\}_{n\geq 1}$ as the \textit{immigration sequence}, and $\alpha_k \ge 0$ as the \textit{reproduction coefficient} for each non-negative integer $k$.


A cumulative INAR($\infty$) process (also known as a discrete Hawkes process) starts from time $1$, i.e. $\{N_n\}_{n\ge1}$, and is defined by
\begin{equation}
  N_n := \sum_{s=1}^n X_s.
\end{equation}


Hawkes processes, introduced by \cite{hawkes1971spectra}, are continuous-time self-exciting point processes widely used in various fields. Discrete-time analogs, such as cumulative INAR($\infty$) processes, provide similar modeling capabilities, particularly for count data observed at fixed time intervals. However, the scaling limits of heavy-tailed, unstable cumulative INAR($\infty$) processes differ considerably from those of continuous-time counterparts, which warrants dedicated theoretical investigations. Under certain conditions, the Poisson autoregressive process can be viewed as an INAR($\infty$) process with Poisson offspring. For a comprehensive discussion of Poisson autoregressive models and their connections to INAR and Hawkes processes, please refer to \cite{fokianos2021multivariate} and \cite{huang2023nonlinear}. Alternatively, the INAR($\infty$) process can be equivalently defined via its conditional intensity expression, which highlights its similarity to the Hawkes process. Specifically, if we let an INAR($\infty$) process $\{X_n\}_{n \geq 1}$ start from time $1$, it can also be defined by:
  \begin{equation}\label{process_definition}
  \lambda_n := \nu + \sum_{s=1}^{n-1} \alpha_{n-s} X_s,
  \end{equation}
  where $\nu > 0$ is the immigration rate, and $\{\alpha_n\}_{n \geq 1} \in \ell^1$ represents the offspring distribution with $\alpha_n \geq 0$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Given the history $\mathcal{F}_{n-1}$, the count $X_n$ follows a Poisson distribution with parameter $\lambda_n$, i.e.,
  \[
  X_n \mid \mathcal{F}_{n-1} \sim \text{Poisson}(\lambda_n).
  \]



Discrete Hawkes processes have been studied in several applications. For example,  \cite{xu2022self} proposed this model to study the deposit and withdrawal behaviors of money market accounts. \cite{wang2024scaling} prove that the fractional CIR process limit can also be obtained by scaling a sequence of heavy-tailed nearly unstable cumulative INAR$(\infty)$ processes by imitating the method in \cite{jaisson2016rough}. Specifically, they study a heavy-tailed unstable INAR($\infty$) with
\[
  \alpha_n\sim \frac{K}{n^{1+\alpha}}\ \text{as}\ n\rightarrow\infty,
\]
where the constant $\alpha\in(\frac12,1)$. Inspired by the continuous-time case in \cite{jaisson2016rough}, they consider a sequence of INAR processes $\{X_n^\tau\}_{n\in\mathbb N}$. The asymptotic setting is that $\tau\rightarrow\infty$. The sequence of the parameters $\{\lambda_n^\tau\}$ are defined for $n\in\mathbb N$ by
\[
  \lambda_n^\tau:=\nu^\tau+\sum_{s=1}^{n-1}\alpha_{n-s}^\tau X_s^\tau,\ n\in \mathbb N,
\]
where $\nu^\tau$ is a sequence of positive real numbers. In this paper, we will show that this method is useful in obtaining the limit as a rough Heston model.

% 
Simulation of the rough Heston model, in particular, recently has attracted significant attention in academia. \cite{liang2017strong} proposed an Euler-Maruyama method. \cite{abi2019multifactor} introduced a multi-factor approximation approach, and \cite{ma2022fast} developed a fast Monte Carlo method. \cite{callegaro2021fast} made a significant breakthrough in solving the fractional Riccati equation arising in the rough Heston model, developing a hybrid numerical algorithm based on power series expansions that substantially outperforms traditional methods in both speed and stability. More recently, \cite{RICHARD2021109} and \cite{richard2023discrete} made significant contributions by studying Euler-type discrete-time schemes for the rough Heston model. Their work rigorously proves the convergence of the discrete-time schemes to solutions of modified Volterra equations, which are shown to share the same unique solution as the initial equations. 
Notably, \cite{yang2024} presented a highly efficient continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) approximation method for pricing European and American options under the rough stochastic local volatility models, including as a special case the rough Heston model.


The modeling of financial market volatility has long been a central topic in quantitative finance, with recent advances focusing on capturing both microstructural dynamics and macroscopic price behaviors.
In this paper, we for the first time establish a theoretical connection between nearly unstable bivariate heavy-tailed INAR($\infty$) processes and the rough Heston model. This offers a novel framework for understanding not only the microstructural foundation of the rough Heston model, but also the efficient simulation of it.  The main contributions of the paper are
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We rigorously prove that under appropriate scaling conditions, the sequence of bivariate cumulative INAR($\infty$) processes converges in law to the rough Heston model (see Theorem \ref{theorem1}).
    \item This  provides a rigorous mathematical microstructural foundation for understanding the rough Heston model, i.e.  how microscopic trading activities aggregate into macroscopic volatility patterns, bridging the gap between discrete-time order flow dynamics and continuous-time prices processes.
    \item Based on the established theoretical convergence, and noting that it is relatively easy to simulate the INAR($\infty$) process, we propose a novel efficient simulation framework to price various (path-dependent) options under the rough Heston model. Numerical results demonstrate the improved accuracy and efficiency of the proposed framework as compared to the literature, e.g., the Euler-Maruyama method.  
\end{enumerate}

