%\subsubsection{Computational Performance}
For the classical Heston case, while our INAR($\infty$) approach requires 15.9695 seconds to compute all four types of options as compared to 6.1310 seconds for the Euler-Maruyama method, it offers a significant advantage in terms of model flexibility. Specifically, our framework can seamlessly accommodate both rough and classical volatility regimes without requiring changes to the underlying computational architecture. This unified treatment enables practitioners to efficiently explore different volatility assumptions within a single implementation framework before judging whether a rough or non-rough volatility model should be used, making it particularly valuable for comparative studies and model risk assessment.



\section{Conclusion}\label{sec4}
In this paper,  on the theoretical side, we have established a novel link between nearly unstable heavy-tailed INAR($\infty$) processes and the rough Heston model, offering a new perspective on volatility modeling in financial markets. By employing discrete scaling limit methods, we demonstrate how microstructural order flow dynamics—modeled through a bivariate cumulative INAR($\infty$) process—induce rough volatility at the macroscopic price level. This fractional volatility model effectively captures the long-memory properties of asset prices and provides a rigorous explanation for the near-critical phenomena commonly observed in financial time series.

On the empirical side, we have developed an efficient  Monte Carlo simulation framework that leverages parallel computation of 100,000 paths to price various types of options under the rough Heston model. For European options, our method produces pricing results comparable to existing methods (such as \cite{richard2023discrete}), with differences typically within a few basis points, while incurring less computational time. Furthermore, we have successfully extended the pricing framework to more complex path-dependent options, e.g.,  Asian options, lookback options and barrier options. 

Our numerical experiments  demonstrate that the INAR($\infty$) framework is not only theoretically well-grounded but also practically viable. The method accurately captures the characteristics of rough volatility, exhibits excellent flexibility in handling path-dependent options, and achieves more efficient computational performance as compared to the literature.

This research can be further developed along multiple directions. Given the high efficiency of the implementation, the model could be applied to calibration with real market data, particularly in a high-frequency trading environment. The framework could be extended to include more types of exotic options, while computational efficiency may be further optimized, especially when handling large-scale path-dependent options valuations. Additionally, it is of interest to explore limit order book modeling applications.

