				Since $b_l$ is a red-colored agent, we have $\mu(b_l) \prec_H \nu(b_l)$. This, together with \eqref{equation same assignments} and the fact $\mu(b_1) \prec_H \cdots \prec_H \mu(b_m)$, implies $\mu(b_{l+1}) \preceq_H \nu(b_l)$. Because of this, and since $p(P_{b_l}) \prec_H \mu(b_{l+1})$, by single-peakedness of $P_{b_l}$, we have $\mu(b_{l+1}) \mathrel{R_{b_l}} \nu(b_l)$. This, together with the fact $\nu(b_l) \mathrel{P_{b_l}} \mu(b_l)$, implies $\mu(b_{l+1}) \mathrel{P_{b_l}} \mu(b_l)$.


